<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
7. Changes to the ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM Coding System 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed above in section II.B.1. of this preamble, the ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM is a coding system that is used for
the reporting of diagnoses and procedures performed on a patient. In September 1985, the ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM Coordination
and Maintenance Committee was formed. This is a Federal interdepartmental committee charged with the mission of
maintaining and updating the ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM. That mission includes approving coding changes, and developing
errata, addenda, and other modifications to the ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM to reflect newly developed procedures and technologies
and newly identified diseases. The Committee is also responsible for promoting the use of Federal and non-Federal
educational programs and other communication techniques with a view toward standardizing coding applications
and upgrading the quality of the classification system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Committee is co-chaired by the National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS) and HCFA. The NCHS has lead responsibility
for the ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM diagnosis codes included in Volume 1_Diseases: Tabular List and Volume 2_Diseases:
Alphabetic Index, while HCFA has lead responsibility for the ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM procedure codes included in Volume
3_Procedures: Tabular List and Alphabetic Index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Committee encourages participation in the above process by health-related organizations. In this regard, the
Committee holds public meetings for discussion of educational issues and proposed coding changes. These meetings
provide an opportunity for representatives of recognized organizations in the coding fields, such as the American
Health Information Management Association (AHIMA) (formerly American Medical Record Association (AMRA)), the
American Hospital Association (AHA), and various physician specialty groups as well as physicians, medical record
administrators, health information management professionals, and other members of the public to contribute ideas
on coding matters. After considering the opinions expressed at the public meetings and in writing, the Committee
formulates recommendations, which then must be approved by the agencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Committee presented proposals for coding changes at public meetings held on May 6, August 5, and December 2, 1993,
and finalized the coding changes after consideration of comments received at the meetings and in writing within 30
days following the December 1993 meeting. The initial meeting for consideration of coding issues for implementation
in FY 1996 will be held on May 5, 1994. Copies of the minutes of these meetings may be obtained by writing to one of the co-chairpersons
representing NCHS and HCFA. We encourage commenters to address suggestions on coding issues involving diagnosis
codes to: Sue Meads, Co-Chairperson; ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM Coordination and Maintenance Committee; NCHS; Rm. 9&hyph;58;
6525 Belcrest Road; Hyattsville, Maryland 20782. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00038 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Questions and comments concerning the procedure codes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be addressed to: Patricia E. Brooks, Co-Chairperson; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM Coordination and Maintenance Committee; HCFA, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Office of Coverage and Eligibility Policy; Rm. 401 East High 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rise Building; 6325 Security Boulevard; Baltimore, Maryland 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
21207. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM code changes that have been approved will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
become effective October 1, 1994. The new ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM codes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are listed, along with their proposed DRG classifications, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Tables 6a and 6b (New Diagnosis Codes and New Procedure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Codes, respectively) in section IV of the addendum to this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed rule. As we stated above, the code numbers and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their titles were presented for public comment in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM Coordination and Maintenance Committee meetings. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Both oral and written comments were considered before the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
codes were approved. Therefore, we are soliciting comments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only on the proposed DRG classification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Further, the Committee has approved the expansion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certain ICD&hyph;9&hyph;CM codes to require an additional digit for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
valid code assignment. Diagnosis codes that have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replaced by expanded codes, other codes, or have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deleted are in Table 6c (Invalid Diagnosis Codes). The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
invalid diagnosis codes will not be recognized by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
GROUPER beginning with discharges occurring on or after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1994. The corresponding new or expanded codes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are included in Tables 6a and 6b. The committee did not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delete any procedure codes effective for October 1, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Revisions to diagnosis and procedure code titles are in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Tables 6d (Revised Diagnosis Code Titles) and 6e (Revised 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Procedure Code Titles), which also include the proposed DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assignments for these revised codes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
8. DRG Refinements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For several years, we have been analyzing major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refinements to the DRG classification system to compensate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals more equitably for treating severely ill Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patients. These refinements, generally referred to as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
severity of illness adjustments, would create DRGs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specifically for hospital discharges involving very ill 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patients who consume far more resources than do other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patients classified to the same DRGs in the current system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This approach has been taken by various other groups in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refining the DRG system, most notably the research done for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Yale, the changes incorporated by the State of New York into 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its all patient (AP) DRG system, and the all-patient refined 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(APR) DRGs, which are a joint effort of 3M/HIS and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
National Association of Children's Hospitals and Related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Institutions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our preliminary work on these refinements is finished, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and we have prepared a paper available to the public for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment that describes our proposed severity DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
classification system as well as the analysis upon which our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposal was formulated. Briefly, our approach was to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
develop a list of secondary diagnoses that have a major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect on the resources used by hospitals in treating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patients across DRGs and to evaluate the need to create DRGs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for major CCs on a DRG-by-DRG basis. The following is a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
description of our methodology: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Our first step, similar to the process used by Yale, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
was to collapse the current paired DRG groupings (DRGs with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and without CCs). We also evaluated the current DRGs to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determine if any of them could be combined. Based on this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis, we eliminated 24 DRGs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Our next step was to evaluate individual diagnosis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
codes to determine if the presence of the diagnosis as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
secondary condition resulted in increased resource use for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals across all DRGs. We analyzed how the presence of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the secondary diagnosis affected resource use compared to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other secondary diagnoses that have been classified as non-CC, CC, or major CC. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Although our final decisions of the CC designation of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
secondary diagnosis were primarily data driven, we did make 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some designations based on other factors. Specifically, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regardless of the data, we did not designate a diagnosis as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major if it is indicative of poor quality of care or is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prone to hospital upcoding. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00039 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;After finalizing the classification of secondary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diagnoses, we evaluated which collapsed DRGs should be split 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the basis of the presence of a major CC, other CCs, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
both. We developed criteria for this evaluation that allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a DRG to split only if the volume and difference in resource 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use is significantly different from the remainder of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cases in the DRG and there is a significant reduction in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variance. The collapsed DRGs can be split into three 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variations: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
_DRG With CC or Major CC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_DRG Without CC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_DRG With Major CC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_DRG Without Major CC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_DRG With Major CC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_DRG With CC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
_DRG Without CC 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
There are 120 DRGs that do not split at all. This final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
step resulted in the creation of 652 DRGs compared to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
491 DRGs we currently use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The paper we have prepared includes a listing of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revised DRGs as well as the relative weights that would have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been assigned to the DRGs using the FY 1992 MedPAR data and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a list of every diagnosis code in effect in FY 1993 with its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current and proposed CC assignment (non-CC, CC, or major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CC). In order to begin consultation with the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
industry and other interested organizations before formally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing our revised system, we will be distributing the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paper to many hospital-related associations and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organizations for their comment. In addition, requests for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the DRG severity refinement paper may be made to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following address: Division of Hospital Payment Policy, 1&hyph;H&hyph;1 East Low Rise, 6325 Security Boulevard,
Baltimore, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Maryland 21207, Attn: Nancy Edwards; or by calling (410) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
966&hyph;4532. Comments are due to HCFA by September 30, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Similar to our usual practice for proposed DRG changes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the prospective payment system, we are making available 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the public an FY 1992 Expanded Modified MedPAR File for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Severity that contains the discharge records we used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculate the severity DRG relative weights. Requests for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this file can be made by following the instructions set 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forth in section VIII.B of this preamble (Requests for Data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the Public). Also available are Severity versions of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the HCFA Medicare Case-Mix Index File, Table 5 DRG, and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
AOR/BOR File. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our plan is to incorporate comments and suggestions we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receive and to consider proposing the complete revised DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system as part of the FY 1996 prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed rule, which will be published in the spring of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1995. However, as the final rule published on September 1, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1992 (57 FR 39761) indicates, we would not propose to make 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant changes to the DRG classification system unless 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we are able either to improve our ability to predict coding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes by validating in advance the impact that potential 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DRG changes may have on coding behavior, or to make 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methodological changes to prevent building the inflationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effects of the coding changes into future program payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Besides the mandate of section 1886(d)(4)(C)(iii) of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Act, which provides that aggregate payments may not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected by DRG reclassification and recalibration changes, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we do not believe it is prudent policy to make changes for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which we cannot predict the effect on the case-mix index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and, thus, payments. Our goal is to refine our methodology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
so that we can fulfill, in the most appropriate manner, both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the statutory requirement to make appropriate DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
classification changes and to recalibrate DRG relative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
weights (as mandated by section 1886(d)(4)(C) of the Act) as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
well as to make DRG changes in a budget neutral manner. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One approach to this problem would be to maintain the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average case weight at 1.0 after recalibration, thereby 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eliminating the process of normalization. In other words, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
after recalibration, we would not scale the new relative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
weights upward to carry forward the cumulative effects of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
past case-mix increases. We would, instead, make an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment or include in the annual update factor a specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowance for any real case-mix change that occurred during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the previous year. This is a relatively simple and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
straightforward system for preventing the effects of year-to-year increase in the case-mix index from accumulating
in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the DRG weights. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition to the severity changes, we also intend to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improve the classification and relative weights of the DRGs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that apply to newborns, children, and maternity patients. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Medicare population does not include many of these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individuals. The original DRG classification system was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
developed from analysis of claims data representative of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total inpatient population. When we calculated the original 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare weights for the DRGs to which newborn, children and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maternity patients are classified, we used non-MedPAR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discharge records from Maryland and Michigan hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because there were either no MedPAR cases or too few cases 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assigned to these DRGs to provide a reasonably precise 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimate of the average cost of care. (See the September 1, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1983 prospective payment final rule with comment period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(48 FR 39768).) Since that time, because of the lack of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MedPAR data, these low-volume DRGs have not been analyzed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and refined, and the relative weights assigned to them may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
no longer be entirely reflective of the resources needed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
treat the patients. We again intend to rely on data bases 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outside the MedPAR file to supplement our data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Recalibration of DRG Weights 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We are proposing to use the same basic methodology for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FY 1995 recalibration as we did for FY 1994. (See the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
September 1, 1993 final rule (58 FR 46290).) That is, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would recalibrate the weights based on charge data for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare discharges. However, we would use the most current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
charge information available, the FY 1993 MedPAR file, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rather than the FY 1992 MedPAR file. The MedPAR file is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based on fully-coded diagnostic and surgical procedure data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for all Medicare inpatient hospital bills. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00040 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposed recalibrated DRG relative weights are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constructed from FY 1993 MedPAR data, received by HCFA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through December 1993, from all hospitals subject to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system and short-term acute care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals in waiver States. The FY 1993 MedPAR file 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes data for approximately 10.5 million Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discharges. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The methodology used to calculate the proposed DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relative weights from the FY 1993 MedPAR file is as follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;To the extent possible, all the claims were regrouped 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
using the proposed DRG classification revisions discussed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above in section II.B of this preamble. As noted in section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
II.B.4, due to the unavailability of revised GROUPER 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
software, we simulate most major classification changes to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approximate the placement of cases under the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification. However, there are some changes that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cannot be modeled. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Charges were standardized to remove the effects of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
differences in area wage levels, indirect medical education 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs, disproportionate share payments, and, for hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Alaska and Hawaii, the applicable cost-of-living 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The average standardized charge per DRG was calculated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by summing the standardized charges for all cases in the DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and dividing that amount by the number of cases classified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the DRG. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;We then eliminated statistical outliers using the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criterion as was used in computing the current weights. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
That is, all cases outside of 3.0 standard deviations from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the mean of the log distribution of charges per case for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each DRG were eliminated. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The average charge for each DRG was then recomputed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(excluding the statistical outliers) and divided by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national average standardized charge per case to determine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the relative weight. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;We established the relative weight for heart 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transplants (DRG 103) in a manner consistent with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methodology for all other DRGs except that the heart 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transplant cases that were used to establish the weight were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited to those Medicare-approved heart transplant centers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that have cases in the FY 1993 MedPAR file. Similarly, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited the liver transplant cases that were used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish the weight for DRG 480 (Liver Transplant) to those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals that are Medicare-approved liver transplant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
centers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Acquisition costs for kidney, heart, and liver 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transplants continue to be paid on a reasonable cost basis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Unlike other excluded costs, the acquisition costs are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concentrated in specific DRGs (DRG 302 (Kidney Transplant); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DRG 103 (Heart Transplant); and DRG 480 (Liver Transplant)). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because these costs are paid separately from the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment rate, it is necessary to make an adjustment to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevent the relative weights for these DRGs from including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the effect of the acquisition costs. Therefore, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subtracted the acquisition charges from the total charges on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each transplant bill that showed acquisition charges before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
computing the average charge for the DRG and before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eliminating statistical outliers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
When we recalibrated the DRG weights for previous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years, we set a threshold of 10 cases as the minimum number 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of cases required to compute a reasonable weight. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
propose to use that same case threshold in recalibrating the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DRG weights for FY 1995. Using the FY 1993 MedPAR data set, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
there are 35 DRGs that contain fewer than 10 cases. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
computed the weight for the 35 low-volume DRGs by adjusting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the original weights of these DRGs by the percentage change 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the average weight of the cases in the remaining DRGs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The weights developed according to the methodology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described above, using the proposed DRG classification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes, result in an average case weight that is different 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the average case weight before recalibration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, the new weights are normalized by an adjustment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factor, so that the average case weight after recalibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is equal to the average case weight before recalibration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This adjustment is intended to ensure that recalibration by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
itself neither increases nor decreases total payments under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the prospective payment system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 1886(d)(4)(C)(iii) of the Act requires that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning with FY 1991, reclassification and recalibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes be made in a manner that assures that the aggregate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments are neither greater than nor less than the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aggregate payments that would have been made without the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes. Although normalization is intended to achieve this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect, equating the average case weight after recalibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the average case weight before recalibration does not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessarily achieve budget neutrality with respect to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aggregate payments to hospitals because payment to hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is affected by factors other than average case weight. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, as discussed in section II.A.4.b. of the Addendum 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to this proposed rule, we are proposing to make a budget 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neutrality adjustment to assure the requirement of section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(d)(4)(C)(iii) of the Act is met. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00041 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
III. Proposed Changes to the Hospital Wage Index and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Possible Refinements to Labor Market Areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Background 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under the Medicare prospective payment system, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different payment rates are calculated for hospitals located 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in rural, urban, and large urban areas. For purposes of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standardized payment amount, section 1886(d)(2)(D) of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Social Security Act requires that we use Metropolitan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Statistical Areas (MSAs) as defined by the Office of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Management and Budget (OMB) to determine whether hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are located in rural, urban or large urban areas (areas with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a population over 1 million). However, section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(d)(3)(A) of the Act provides for the elimination of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
separate urban and rural standardized payment amounts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning in FY 1995. This change is discussed below in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section IV.C. of this proposed rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 1886(d)(3)(E) of the Act requires that, as part 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the methodology for determining prospective payments to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals, the Secretary shall adjust the standardized 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amounts ``for area differences in hospital wage levels by a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factor (established by the Secretary) reflecting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relative hospital wage level in the geographic area of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital compared to the national average hospital wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level.'' In accordance with the broad discretion conferred 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by this provision, we currently define hospital labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas based on the definitions of MSAs issued by OMB. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Additionally, as discussed below, we adjust the wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to take into account the geographic reclassification of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals in accordance with sections 1886(d)(8)(B) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(d)(10) of the Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 1886(d)(3)(E) of the Act also requires that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index be updated annually beginning October 1, 1993. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This section further provides that the Secretary base the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
update on a survey of wages and wage-related costs of short-term acute care hospitals. The survey should measure,
to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the extent feasible, the earnings and paid hours of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employment by occupational category and must exclude data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with respect to the wages and wage-related costs incurred in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
furnishing skilled nursing services. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For determining prospective payments to hospitals in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1994, the wage index is based on the data collected from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Medicare cost reports submitted by short-term acute care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals for cost reporting periods beginning in FY 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(that is, cost reporting periods beginning on or after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1989 and before October 1, 1990). The current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index includes wages and salaries paid by a hospital, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
home office salaries, fringe benefits, and certain contract 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor costs and hours. The current computation for the wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index excludes salaries and wages associated with non-hospital type services, such as skilled nursing facility

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services, home health agency services, or other subprovider 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
components that are not subject to the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As discussed in detail below, we are proposing to use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
updated wage data to construct the wage index as required by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 1886(d)(3)(E) of the Act. The FY 1995 wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be based on data for hospital cost reporting periods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning on or after October 1, 1990 and before October 1, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1991 (FY 1991). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. FY 1995 Wage Index Update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We propose to base the FY 1995 wage index, effective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for hospital discharges occurring on or after October 1, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1994 and before October 1, 1995, upon the data collected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the Medicare cost report (Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submitted by hospitals for cost reporting periods beginning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in FY 1991. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We propose to use all of the categories of data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
collected from Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II. Therefore, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FY 1995 wage index reflects the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Total short-term acute care hospital salaries and hours. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Home office costs and hours. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Fringe benefits associated with hospital and home 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
office salaries. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Direct patient care related contract labor cost and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hours. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The exclusion of salaries and hours for non-hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
type services such as skilled nursing facility services, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
home health services, or other sub-provider components that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are not subject to the prospective payment system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Verification of Wage Data from the Medicare Cost Report 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The data for the proposed FY 1995 wage index was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obtained from Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II, of the HCFA&hyph;2552 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submitted by short-term acute care hospitals for cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting periods beginning during FY 1991. The wage data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are reported electronically to HCFA through the Hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Cost Report Information System (HCRIS). Because of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantial deficiencies in the initial data reported by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals on the cost report (including missing data items 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such as excluded hours and total paid hours), we initiated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an intensive review of the wage data and made numerous edits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to ensure quality and accuracy. Medicare intermediaries 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were instructed to transmit any revisions through HCRIS by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
early January 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We subjected the revised cost report data to several 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
edit checks. Of the 5,294 providers in the data base, over 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1,400 providers had data elements that failed an edit; 53 of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these involved mathematical errors and have been resolved. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00042 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
The other edits involved data that appeared unusual and had 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be verified by the intermediary. Only 74 providers had 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data elements that were unresolved as of March 14, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Most of the unresolved data elements fall outside 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established edit parameters and require verification by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediary. None of the unresolved data elements failed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
critical edits (that is, edits indicating serious data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
errors that affect the wage index computation). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The wage file used to construct the proposed wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes data obtained in late January 1994 from the HCRIS 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
database and subsequent changes we received from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediaries through March 14, 1994. We have instructed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the intermediaries to complete their verification of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
questionable data elements and to transmit any changes to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the wage data, via HCRIS, no later than June 15, 1994. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expect that all outstanding data elements will be resolved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by that date and that we will be able to reflect the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
corrected data in the final rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the past, the hospital wage index data file was not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available to the public until mid-May. However, to allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals more time to evaluate the wage data used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
construct the proposed hospital wage index, on March 16, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1994, we made available to the public a diskette containing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the raw hospital wage data that were used to construct the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FY 1995 wage index. We advised State and national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital groups of the availability of the data. We also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
instructed all fiscal intermediaries to inform the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment hospitals they service that we would not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be sending out hospital-specific wage data as we had for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FY 1994 wage index, but that we would make the FY 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1991 data available on a diskette. The fiscal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediaries were instructed to advise hospitals of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
availability of the data either through their representative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital organizations or directly from HCFA using order 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forms provided to them. Additional details on the cost and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ordering of this file will be found below in section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VIII. B. of this proposed rule, Requests for Data from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Public. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, we note that Table 3c in the Addendum to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this proposed rule contains each hospital's inflated average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hourly wage used to construct the wage index values. By 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
backing out the applicable inflation factors (set forth 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below in section III.B.3.), a hospital can determine its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
uninflated average hourly wage as reflected in the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index. This table will also be included in the final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule. If a hospital believes, based on its review of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data contained in Table 3c, that its average hourly wage is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inconsistent with the data submitted on Worksheet S&hyph;3, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital should contact its intermediary. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Requests for Wage Data Corrections 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As noted above we will use cost report data from FY 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1991 (that is, cost reporting periods beginning on or after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1990 and before October 1, 1991) for the update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the wage index. As such, we believe hospitals have had 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ample time to ensure the accuracy of their FY 1991 wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data. Moreover, the ultimate responsibility for accurately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
completing the cost report rests with the hospital, which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must attest to the accuracy of the data at the time the cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report is filed. However, if a hospital believes that its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1991 wage data have been incorrectly reported, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital must submit corrections along with complete 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supporting documentation to its intermediary in time to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allow for review, verification and transmission of the data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before the development of the final wage index. To allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sufficient time to process any changes, a hospital must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submit requests for corrections to its fiscal intermediary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by May 15, 1994. The request should include all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
documentation necessary to support the requested change. To 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be reflected in the final wage index, any wage data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
corrections must be reviewed by the intermediary and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transmitted to HCFA via HCRIS on or before June 15, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00043 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This deadline is necessary to allow sufficient time to download and edit the data so that the final wage index calculation
can be completed for development of the final prospective payment rates to be published by September 1, 1994. We cannot
guarantee that corrections transmitted to HCFA after June 15, 1994, will be reflected in the final wage index. Therefore,
we suggest that hospitals wishing to submit corrected data do so as soon as possible and follow up with their intermediaries
to ensure inclusion of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
corrected data in the final FY 1995 wage index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
After reviewing requested changes submitted by hospitals, intermediaries will transmit any revised cost reports
to HCRIS and will forward to the hospitals a copy of the revised Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II. If requested changes are
not accepted, fiscal intermediaries will notify hospitals in writing of reasons why the changes were not accepted.
This procedure will ensure that hospitals have an opportunity to verify the data that will be used to construct their
wage index values. We believe that fiscal intermediaries are in the best position to make evaluations regarding the
appropriateness of a particular cost and whether it should be included in the wage index data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, in the event that a hospital disagrees with the intermediary's resolution of a requested change, the hospital
may contact HCFA to attempt to resolve the dispute. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We have created the above-described process to resolve all substantive wage data correction disputes before we finalize
the raw wage data for the FY 1995 payment rates, Accordingly, hospitals that do not meet the procedural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadlines described above will not be afforded a later opportunity to submit wage corrections or to dispute the intermediary's
decision with respect to requested changes. We note that we intend to make a diskette available in mid-August that
will contain the finalized raw wage data that will be used to construct the wage index values in the final rule. As with
the diskette made available in March 1994, HCFA will make the August diskette available to hospital associations
and the public. This August diskette, however, is being made available only for the limited purpose of identifying
any potential errors made by HCFA or the intermediary in the entry of the finalized wage data, not for the initiation
of new wage data correction requests. Hospitals are encouraged to review their hospital wage data promptly after
the release of the second diskette. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If after reviewing the August diskette, a hospital believes that its wage data is incorrect due to a fiscal intermediary
or HCFA error, it should send a letter to both its fiscal intermediary and HCFA. The letters to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediary and HCFA should outline why the hospital believes an error exists. These requests must be 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
received
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 by HCFA no later than September 23. Requests should be sent to: Charles R. Booth, Director; Office of Payment Policy;
181 East High Rise; 6325 Security Boulevard; Baltimore, Maryland 21207. The intermediary will review requests upon
receipt and, if it is determined that an intermediary or HCFA error exists, the fiscal intermediary will notify HCFA
immediately. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As indicated above, after mid-August, we will make changes to the hospital wage data only in those very limited situations
involving an error by the intermediary or HCFA that the hospital could not have known about before its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review of the August diskette. Specifically, neither the intermediary nor HCFA will accept the following types of
requests in conjunction with this mid-August process: requests for wage data correction that were submitted too

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
late to be included in the data transmitted to the HCRIS system on or before June 15, 1994, requests for correction of
errors made by the hospital not identified during the hospital's review of the March 1994 data, or requests to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revisit factual determinations or policy interpretations made by the intermediary or HCFA during the wage data correction
process. Verified corrections to the wage index made as a result of an intermediary or HCFA error received timely (that
is, by September 23, 1994) will be effective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We continue to believe, as outlined in the final rule published September 1, 1992 (57 FR 39765), that midyear corrections
should not be made in most cases. We believe the wage data correction process described above provides 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals with sufficient opportunity to bring errors made during the preparation of Worksheet S&hyph;3 to the intermediary's
attention. Moreover, because hospitals will have access to the raw wage data in mid-August, they will have the opportunity
to detect any ministerial data tabulation errors made by the intermediary or HCFA before the implementation of the
prospective payment rates on October 1. We believe that if hospitals avail themselves of this opportunity, the wage
index implemented on October 1 should be free of such errors. Nevertheless, in the unlikely event that such errors
should occur, we do not believe a hospital should be disadvantaged because actions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
taken by its intermediary or HCFA in the tabulation of its data resulted in an error of which the hospital could not have
been aware. Since the current regulations do not provide for midyear corrections in these situations, we are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing to revise the regulations to specify that the Secretary retains the right to make midyear changes to the
wage index under very limited circumstances. Again, we believe that it is appropriate to reserve the right to make
midyear changes to the wage index in those limited circumstances where the hospital can show that an error was made,
and the hospital could not have known about, or sought to correct, the error by September 23, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00044 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Specifically, we propose to revise &sect;412.63(s)(2) to provide that the Secretary may make midyear corrections
to the wage index only in those limited circumstances where a hospital can show: (1) That the intermediary or HCFA made
an error in tabulating its data, and (2) that the hospital could not have known about the error, or did not have an opportunity
to correct the error, by September 23, 1994. As indicated earlier, since a hospital will have the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
opportunity to verify its data and the intermediary will notify the hospital of any changes, we do not foresee any specific
circumstances under which midyear corrections would be made. However, should a midyear correction be necessary,
the wage index change for the affected area will be made prospectively from the date the correction is made. If midyear
corrections are made, we will evaluate their impact to determine if a budget neutrality adjustment to the program
payments should be made at the beginning of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following fiscal year in accordance with &sect;412.63(s)(4). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Computation of the Wage Index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As noted above, we are proposing to base the FY 1995 wage index on wage data reported on the FY 1991 cost report. The wage
index would be based on data from 5,294 hospitals paid under the prospective payment system and short-term acute care
hospitals in waiver States. The method used to compute the proposed wage index is as follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 1_We gathered data from each of the non-Federal short-term acute care hospitals for which data were reported
on the Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II of the Medicare cost report for the hospital's cost reporting periods beginning
on or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
after October 1, 1990, and before October 1, 1991. Each hospital was assigned to its appropriate urban or rural area
prior to any reclassifications under sections 1886(d)(8) or 1886(d)(10) of the Act. In addition, we included data
from a few hospitals that had cost reporting periods beginning in September 1990 and had reported a cost reporting
period exceeding 52 weeks. The data were included because no other data from these hospitals would be available for
the cost reporting period described above, and particular labor market areas might be affected due to the omission
of these hospitals. However, we generally describe this wage data as FY 1991 data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 2_For each hospital, we subtracted the excluded salaries (that is, direct salaries attributable to skilled
nursing facility services, home health services, and other sub-provider components not subject to the prospective

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system) from gross hospital salaries to determine net hospital salaries. To the net hospital salaries, we
added hospital contract labor costs, hospital fringe benefits, and any home office salaries and fringe benefits
reported by the hospital to determine total salaries plus fringe benefits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 3_For each hospital, we inflated or deflated, as appropriate, the total salaries plus fringe benefits resulting
from Step 2 to a common period to determine total adjusted salaries. To make the wage inflation adjustment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we used the percentage change in average hourly earnings for each 30-day increment from October 14, 1990 through September
15, 1992, for hospital industry workers from S.I.C. 806, Bureau of Labor Statistics Employment and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Earnings Bulletin. The annual inflation rates used were 5.6 percent for FY 1990 and FY 1991 and 4.8 percent for FY 1992.
The inflation factors used to inflate the hospital's data were based on the midpoint of the cost reporting period as
indicated below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



