<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=4 -->
For example, the midpoint of a cost reporting period beginning January 1, 1991 and ending December 31, 1991 is June
30, 1991. An inflation adjustment factor of 1.033713 would be applied to the wages of a hospital with such a cost reporting
period. In addition, for the data for any cost reporting period that began in FY 1991 and covers a period of less than
360 days or greater than 370 days, we annualized the data to reflect a 1-year cost report. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Annualization is accomplished by dividing the data by the number of days in the cost report and then multiplying the
results by 365. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Step 4_For each hospital, we subtracted the reported excluded hours from the gross hospital hours to determine net
hospital hours. We increased the net hours by the addition of any reported contract labor hours and home office hours
to determine total hours. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 5_As part of our editing process, we deleted data for 69 hospitals that are no longer participating in the Medicare
program or that are in bankruptcy status, and for which we lacked sufficient documentation to verify data that failed
edits. We retained the data for other hospitals that are no longer participating in the Medicare program because these
hospitals contributed to the relative wage levels in their labor market areas during their FY 1991 cost reporting
period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 6_Within each urban or rural labor market area we added the total adjusted salaries plus fringe benefits obtained
in Step 3 for all hospitals in that area to determine the total adjusted salaries plus fringe benefits for the labor
market area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 7_We divided the total adjusted salaries plus fringe benefits obtained in Step 6 by the sum of the total hours (from
Step 4) for all hospitals in each labor market area to determine an average hourly wage for the area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 8_We added the total adjusted salaries plus fringe benefits obtained in Step 3 for all hospitals in the nation
and then divided the sum by the national sum of total hours from Step 4 to arrive at a national average hourly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage. Using the data as described above, the national average hourly wage is $18.1808. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Step 9_For each urban or rural labor market area, we calculated the hospital wage index value by dividing the area average
hourly wage obtained in Step 7 by the national average hourly wage computed in Step 8. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=4 -->
C. Changes In the Reporting of Hospital Wage Index Data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Currently, the data used to develop the wage index are submitted by hospitals on the Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II of
the Medicare cost report. We developed this worksheet as part of the FY 1990 cost reports, and we used the worksheet
to calculate the wage index for FY 1994. The worksheet has been evaluated by HCFA and industry representatives to ensure
that this data collection mechanism captures relative wage costs as accurately as possible and to determine whether
any refinements are appropriate. The Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Technical Advisory Group (MTAG) established a task force to study and recommend changes to the cost reporting form
used to collect wage index data. The task force was comprised of hospital, intermediary, and HCFA representatives.
The MTAG task force recommended, and we are proposing, three major changes to the Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II; the
provider cost report questionnaire (HCFA 339); and accompanying instructions as discussed below. We note that the
proposed changes outlined below are effective for cost reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
periods beginning on or after October 1, 1994. Therefore the proposed changes will not affect the proposed FY 1995
wage index, which is based on FY 1991 wage data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
1. The Elimination of Part A Physicians' and CRNA Salaries 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Currently, a hospital that directly employs and pays the salary of a physician can include the Part A portion of the
physician's salary in total salaries reported on Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II. (The Part B portion of physician 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
salaries has always been excluded from the wage index.) However, if a hospital contracts for physician services,
it is not permitted to include the Part A physician services as contract labor because we consider Part A physician
services to be administrative, not direct patient-care related (to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
included in the computation of the wage index, contract labor must be directly related to patient care). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Not all hospitals directly employ physicians. There are currently five States in which State laws specifically restrict
hospitals from directly hiring physicians. Hospitals in these States are forced to contract out for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physicians. The inability of those hospitals that contract for physician services to include the Part A portion of
the services as contract labor has been perceived as inequitable. In States where hospitals may directly employ 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physicians, the hospitals may include some of these characteristically high wages in the wage data, while in States
where hospitals must contract for their physician services, these contract wages cannot be included in their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We agree with the MTAG task force recommendation and are proposing to exclude all Part A physician costs on the Worksheet
S&hyph;3, Part II, regardless of whether the physician is a hospital employee or contractor. For purposes of this
exclusion, physician's salaries are defined as salaries applicable to positions that require a licensed physician,
such as a medical director of a department. Salaries for physicians employed in other positions, such as hospital
administrator, that do not require a physician would not be excluded.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since this change would apply equally to hospitals that are permitted to employ physicians and to those that must contract
for physician services, we believe this proposal will promote payment equity and provide more uniformity in the wage
data across areas. This action should not require any additional reporting burden since these Part A physician salaries
already are reported on the cost report (Worksheet A&hyph;8&hyph;2). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe that this proposal to eliminate Part A physicians' salaries is more appropriate than the alternative of
allowing all Part A physician costs (whether salaried or under contract) for three reasons. First, physician costs
are not driven by normal labor market situations and, in many cases, hospitals must hire physicians from outside of
their recruiting areas. Second, many hospitals have indicated difficulty in accurately determining the hours for
the physicians attributable to Part A services, especially for those under contract. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00047 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=4 -->
Third, we have found that some hospitals that employ 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physicians are not appropriately eliminating Part B 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physician salaries from the total salaries reported for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index as required in the cost reporting instructions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accordingly, we are proposing that hospitals exclude all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physicians' salaries (both Part A and Part B related) from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the wage data reported on the Medicare cost report. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addition, we are proposing that the salaries for teaching 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physicians also be excluded, since payment for these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services does not fall under the prospective payment system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Regarding Part A CRNA (certified registered nurse 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
anesthetist) costs, we propose to require hospitals to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exclude these salaries from the wage data reported on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost report. These Part A services are currently paid on a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``pass-through'' basis, outside the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system. Therefore, we believe it is appropriate to exclude 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these costs from the wage index. Moreover, this Part A 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pass-through provision is applicable to a limited number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals (small rural hospitals). All other hospitals are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paid for CRNA services under Part B of the Medicare program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, in order to ensure consistency across areas, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believe that no CRNA costs should be reflected in the wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index computation. CRNA services are generally excluded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the wage index as Part B services. Since the Part A 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portion was granted as a pass through for certain rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals, it is currently included in wage index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We note that these changes are effective for cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting periods beginning on or after October 1, 1994 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will not affect the current reporting of physicians and CRNA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
salaries. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
2. Management Contracts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
The second major proposed change concerns the inclusion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of certain management contracts in the hospital wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data. Before FY 1994, the wage index did not include any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs associated with contract services. However, many 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals indicated that they were inappropriately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disadvantaged because they were forced to contract out for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nurses and technicians due to shortages of these services in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their areas. To alleviate this problem, we revised the cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report to collect the data associated with any direct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient care service contract (that is, nursing, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therapeutic, etc.). We specifically excluded any Part B 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services, Part A physician services, management contracts, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or any contract for services not directly involved with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient care. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The hospital industry has expressed concern that we do 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not currently recognize the cost of certain contract 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
management services. In particular, many rural hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that are either unable to recruit or cannot afford top 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
managers such as hospital administrators must contract for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the services of these individuals. Therefore, we believe it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is appropriate to include the costs of certain management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contracts in the wage index. We propose to expand the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition of contract services reported on the Worksheet
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=4 -->
S&hyph;3 to include the personnel cost associated with contracts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for any personnel hired in the top four positions within the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital. Allowable contract management services would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited to the personnel costs for those individuals who are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
working at the hospital facility in the capacity of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Chief Executive Officer (CEO)/Hospital Administrator, Chief 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Operating Officer (COO), Chief Financial Officer (CFO), or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Nursing Administrator. The exact titles assigned to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individuals may vary but the individuals should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performing essentially the same duties as customarily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assigned these management positions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
The hospitals (via HCFA&hyph;339 form) would be required to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide the fiscal intermediary with complete details on all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
direct patient care related contracts and the description 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and aggregate totals for all management contracts. Because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the difficulty in accurately determining hours and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
isolating wage related costs for the other types of contract 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services, the wage data would continue to exclude all other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
non-patient care contract services except those limited 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
management contracts discussed above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since the current cost report does not provide for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
collection of management contract data, this revised 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition would not be effective until cost reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
periods beginning on or after October 1, 1994. To provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent reporting of data, hospitals must continue to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exclude all management contracts until the FY 1995 data is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reported. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
3. Reporting of Wage-Related Costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Since we began including fringe benefits in the wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index, we have been concerned with the inconsistent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting of fringe benefits, whether because of a lack of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provider proficiency in identifying fringe benefit costs or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
varying interpretations across fiscal intermediaries of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition for fringe benefits in PRM&hyph;I, &sect;2144.1. Although 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we have attempted to promote consistent reporting of fringe 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefits by providing fiscal intermediaries with general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
guidelines to be used in determining allowable fringe 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefit costs, the intermediaries must necessarily make 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
judgments as to whether certain costs qualify as a fringe 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefit, and inconsistencies persist. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Last summer, HCFA conducted a survey to determine what 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs the health care industry thought should be recognized 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as fringe benefit costs. The survey consisted of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
questionnaire with a yes/no response column and a column for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comments. We received 238 responses from hospitals, fiscal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediaries, state hospital associations, national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital associations, state agencies, and bureaus and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offices within HCFA. The results of those surveys were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
examined closely by the MTAG task force. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Based on the recommendations of the task force, we are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing several changes that we believe will promote more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equitable and consistent reporting of wage-related costs for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all hospitals. Where the term ``fringe benefit'' has been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used in the past, we will now refer to these costs as ``wage- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
related costs'' for wage index purposes. We believe that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this change in terminology will eliminate the confusion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regarding those wage-related costs that we will allow to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incorporated in the wage index versus the definition of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fringe benefits required by Medicare principles for cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reimbursement purposes. Accordingly, we are proposing to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revise the Worksheet S&hyph;3, Part II to capture wage-related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs in three parts. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00048 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Wage-Related Costs (Core).
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 For most hospitals, the wage-related costs used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
develop the wage index would be limited to ``core'' wage-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
related costs. The list of ``core'' wage-related costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes all commonly recognized costs (which are similar to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fringe benefits used for cost reimbursement purposes) that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contribute significantly to the wage costs of a hospital and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that are readily identifiable on the hospital records. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These costs will also be listed on the revised HCFA&hyph;339 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
form, and hospitals will be required to provide the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediary with a detailed description of the wage-related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs in Exhibit 7. This description will allow the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediary to review the appropriateness of each wage-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
related cost. We believe that this core list includes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
virtually all significant wage-related costs, including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those costs that are required by statute. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To develop the list of core wage-related costs, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MTAG task force established a number of specific criteria. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To be considered a core wage-related cost, one or more of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the following criteria must be met: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The wage-related cost is provided at a significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
financial cost to the employer. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The wage-related cost is of a type and nature that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would generally be offered as a fringe benefit by most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The perceived value of this wage-related cost is of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such importance that it would influence an individual's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employment decisions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The wage-related cost is a mandatory requirement under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal or State law (for example FICA, Federal and State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unemployment, etc.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Fees paid to external organizations that are directly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated with the core wage-related costs may be included 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as part of the wage-related cost (for example, actuarial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fees, claim administration fees, IRS form preparation fees, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
etc.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The following is the proposed list of core wage-related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=3 -->
(1) Retirement Costs: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
401(k) employer contributions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Tax sheltered annuity (TSA) employer contributions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Qualified and non-qualified pension plan cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Prior year pension service cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Plan Administration Costs (Paid to external 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organization): 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
401(k)/TSA plan administration fees 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Legal/accounting/management fees_pension plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Employee managed care program administration fees 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Health and Insurance Costs: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Health insurance (purchased or self-funded) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Prescription drug plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Dental, hearing, vision plans 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
