<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
F. Occupational Mix Adjustment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In its March 1, 1994 report, ProPAC recommended that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Secretary develop methods to collect more timely 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital wage data by occupational categories and to adjust 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the HCFA wage index for occupational mix (Recommendation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
15). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The HCFA wage index reflects variations in the cost of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor; that is, it includes the effects of variations in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mix of occupations as well as the price of labor. ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that the wage index should reflect only variations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the price of labor, which are beyond the hospital's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control and are not otherwise accounted for by adjustments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the prospective payment system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Previous ProPAC studies on the effect of adjusting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index for occupational mix using data collected from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California hospitals found that a wage index adjusted for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occupational mix would redistribute funds from urban to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rural hospitals. Within urban areas, the occupational mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment would redistribute funds from large to small 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals. Within rural areas, the occupational mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment would increase the wage index values of all bed-size groups. ProPAC also reanalyzed the California data
to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
examine the impact of an occupational mix adjustment on wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index values based on hospital-specific labor market areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ProPAC currently believes that the implementation of revised 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor areas, including nearest neighbor labor markets, would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantially improve payment equity among hospitals if an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occupational adjustment was developed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are not convinced that an occupational mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment would improve the accuracy of the wage index, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we have discussed most recently in the August 30, 1991 final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule (56 FR 43222). Currently, the wage index measures the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
overall costs of labor. If the wage index were to measure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the price of labor, then a set of occupational weights would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have to be developed to determine a standard occupational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mix. Hospitals would not be compensated for a mix of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employees above the standard, while hospitals with a mix of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employees below the standard would be overcompensated, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relative to their cost of labor. If we were to adopt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital-specific labor market areas in conjunction with an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occupational mix adjustment, one concern would be the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application of occupational weights to every prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment hospital in the country. Application of one set of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occupational weights to every prospective payment hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would conflict with the intended purpose of hospital-specific labor markets, which is to make the wage index more

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsive to local conditions. We cannot envision a single 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
set of occupational weights that could be applied to all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment hospitals in an equitable manner. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Last year, ProPAC suggested that we convene a working 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
group made up of representatives from HCFA and the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
industry to try to improve the timeliness and accuracy of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the wage data. HCFA assembled such a discussion work group 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and presented ProPAC comments concerning the equity of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopting a method to collect data to assist in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development of an occupational mix. The discussion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
workgroup's consensus was that the data required to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement the proposal is not currently available and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
likelihood of obtaining such data would be minimal. There 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seems to be little support among hospital industry 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representatives for developing a system that in their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
opinion clearly creates additional reporting burdens with an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unproven or minimal impact on the distribution of payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, at this time we are not proposing any changes to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the collection of data to be used in developing an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occupational mix adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00053 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
G. Research on Refinements to Labor Market Areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Background 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Almost from the beginning of the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system, we have received comments from hospitals and ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
objecting to the use of MSA-based labor market areas to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
construct the wage index. Common concerns have been that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Statewide rural areas are too large to distinguish 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
differences in labor market conditions, and that the MSA-based wage index does not reflect the fact that hospitals
in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the center cities (the ``urban core'') pay higher wages than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suburban hospitals. Some commenters have argued that rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor market areas should be subdivided into two smaller 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
groups based on county populations and that MSA-based labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas should be divided into urban core and suburban 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ring labor markets to distinguish the differences in labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market conditions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In light of these concerns, we have continued to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
examine a variety of options for revising wage index labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas. In this proposed rule, we discuss in detail 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issues raised by commenters concerning ProPAC's ``nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbor'' recommendation, our research and analysis on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative labor market areas, and the possibility of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowing all hospitals in a State to opt out of the current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor market system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Discussion of Comments Concerning ProPAC's Nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Neighbor Recommendation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In its March 1, 1993 report, ProPAC recommended that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Secretary substantially revise the hospital wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the prospective payment system for FY 1994. In its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
March 1, 1994 report, ProPAC made the same recommendation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Recommendation 16). Specifically, ProPAC recommends that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we develop hospital-specific labor market areas based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geographic proximity, using each hospital's ``nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors'' for purposes of a revised wage index. As in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1993, ProPAC has also recommended that Congress repeal the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current statutory provisions relating to geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification for the wage index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In ProPAC's 1993 report, the Commission discussed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishing each hospital's labor market area based on its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
10 nearest neighbors within a 50-mile radius. In its 1994 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report, ProPAC examined defining labor markets for each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital by including the nearest 15 hospitals within a 20-mile radius, and extending the radius up to 30 or 35 miles

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
when needed in order to include a total of at least 3 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In our May 26, 1993 proposed rule (58 FR 30242), we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stated that we believed, and ProPAC agreed, that legislation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be required before we could use hospital-specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor market areas in constructing the wage index due to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statutory requirements regarding hospital reclassifications 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for wage index purposes. We also stated that in addition to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the statutory constraints, we did not believe that it would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be feasible or advisable to attempt to implement ProPAC's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommendation in FY 1994. Although we acknowledged that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ProPAC's recommendation might have promise, we indicated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that careful analysis of the impact of such a proposal on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals was necessary before proposing to adopt such a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant change. In addition, we stated there were also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a number of administrative issues that must be carefully 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered before ProPAC's proposal could be implemented, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including the possible development of an exceptions or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appeals process to resolve disputes concerning the labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas. We agreed with ProPAC that if we adopted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital-specific labor market areas using its methodology, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the MGCRB as it is currently constituted might no longer be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary. We also stated that we would not recommend that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Congress repeal the MGCRB provisions of the Act until we had 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyzed fully the implications of adopting hospital-specific labor market areas or some other revisions to labor

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas. We noted that after our analysis was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
complete, we would decide whether to recommend that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mission of the MGCRB be altered to make it responsible for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resolving disputes concerning hospital-specific labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To facilitate a thorough analysis of ProPAC's proposal, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we published in the FY 1994 proposed rule hospital-specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index values using ProPAC's data on hospital locations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the new FY 1990 hospital wage data (58 FR 30457). The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tables indicated what each hospital's wage index value would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be if the wage index were based on the wage data for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital and its 10 nearest neighbors up to a radius of 50 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air miles. The tables also showed which hospitals were in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each hospital's labor market area based on ProPAC's data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
base. Subsequently, we discovered a technical error in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index values and republished a revised wage index value 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for each hospital in a correction notice (June 29, 1993, 58 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FR 34742). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00054 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We solicited public comments on ProPAC's proposal and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggestions concerning the development of an exceptions or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appeals process, if appropriate, for possible implementation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in FY 1995. We also solicited comments on a number of other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issues such as the feasibility of using road miles instead 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of air miles, a process to verify hospital locations, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
what the process should be to establish a wage index for new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals. We stated that we planned to continue our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis of alternatives based on the current MSA based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system that have appeared promising in the past, and that we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
planned to evaluate all potential labor market revisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
using the same basic criteria. Comments on these issues 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were due to HCFA by August 31, 1993. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In both the proposed and final rules for FY 1994, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stated our intention to analyze the comments we received as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
well as to conduct the research we outlined. We also stated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that we would continue to consult with ProPAC and study any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional information they developed. We stated our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intention to publish a proposal for formal public comment in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FY 1995 proposed rule if hospital-specific labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas based on the nearest neighbor concept proved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promising. We indicated that we would include information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
describing all aspects of our proposal, and consider all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comments, before issuing a final rule for implementation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In response to the May 26, 1993 proposed rule, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
received a total of 266 comments on the nearest neighbor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index proposal, including 28 from national, State, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local organizations representing hospitals. In all, 33 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commenters were in favor of the nearest neighbors proposal, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
128 were opposed and 105 were in support if changes were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
made to satisfy their concerns. The areas of major concern 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
raised by the commenters were the redistribution of Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments that would occur if hospital-specific labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas were adopted, how the boundaries should be drawn 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
around hospitals to define labor market areas, and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nature and extent of an exceptions process. Several 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commenters also had reservations about whether a hospital's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nearest neighbors were reflective of its labor market area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We discuss these and other issues below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Redistribution of Medicare Payments
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Many commenters 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were concerned about whether instituting hospital-specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor market areas would lead to major shifts in Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments at the same time that hospitals face an uncertain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
financial future because of the impact of health care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reform. One state hospital association noted that adoption 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the nearest neighbors proposal would cause a major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
redistribution of payments among rural hospitals, and that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
many hospitals that would be adversely affected were already 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in precarious financial condition. Another state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
association commented that the effects of the proposal on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the hospitals in its State would vary greatly, with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantially increased payments to some hospitals and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decreased payments to others. Many commenters suggested 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that if we were to adopt the nearest neighbors proposal, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some type of phase-in over several years would be essential. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Definition of Labor Market Areas
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Several commenters 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stated that hospital characteristics such as size, services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offered, and case mix are a more reliable determinant of who 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital competes with for labor than who its nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors are. One state association stated that its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis of the wage index computed under the nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors proposal demonstrates that geographic proximity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
does not provide an accurate measure of the amount a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital must pay to compete for labor. Since there are a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wide range of options for defining hospital-specific labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas, many of the commenters suggested variations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending on their perception of what new labor market areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should achieve. Some favored changing the size of labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas so as to increase payment to a particular 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital or type of hospital, while others favored defining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor markets in a way that would decrease the payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shifts that would occur under the options. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to minimize the payment changes that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
result from the wage index, some groups supported including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a larger number of hospitals in each labor market, while 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
others supported limiting the size of labor market areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One state association asserted that both nearest neighbor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and fixed boundary labor market alternatives are problematic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because neither method allows a sufficient amount of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
flexibility for determining labor market areas. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
association believes that it would be more accurate if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
direction and distance of worker commuting patterns were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incorporated into the determination of a hospital's nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors. Another association stated that it may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difficult to identify a uniform approach that is equitable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for all areas of the country, and urged HCFA to consider 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
using different methodologies in different regions as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate. A national association stated that while the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nearest neighbors proposal has conceptual appeal, whether 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the association would support a nearest neighbor proposal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ultimately would depend on where HCFA draws the line around 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00055 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Exceptions Process
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Most of the commenters agreed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that some type of exceptions process would be needed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regardless of which system for defining hospital labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas is developed. Several commenters stressed the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
need for an exceptions process to address geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
anomalies; cases in which air miles are not equivalent to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
road miles; and cases in which a hospital is grouped with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unlike hospitals, such as a rural referral center (RRC) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
grouped with much smaller primary care hospitals or a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tertiary care hospital in a single hospital MSA that is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
grouped with much smaller rural hospitals. Another 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commenter supported giving a hospital the right to appeal to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
add or delete hospitals from its labor market area if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
target hospital can demonstrate substantial labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
competition (or a lack of it) with a specific hospital. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Some commenters supported special exception criteria for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RRCs. One group believes that the nearest neighbors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposal should be modified to accommodate RRCs that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise would have been reclassified, that have only other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rural hospitals in their nearest neighbors group, or that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be significantly harmed by the proposal. Another 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
group believes that RRCs should be excluded from a nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors wage index and that they should be allowed to use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
either their own hospital-specific wage index or be allowed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to reclassify into the nearest MSA using the existing MGCRB 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Multi-campus hospitals
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_We received a wide range of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comments concerning multi-campus hospitals. Some commenters 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggested that the hospital's location should be its main 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address listed on the cost report. Other commenters 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggested that the facility with the largest concentration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of employees be defined as the hospital's location, or that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital should be allowed to pick which campus would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered its location for labor market area purposes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Hospital Location Verification
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Some commenters 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggested that we publish each hospital's longitude and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
latitude and require them to submit documentation to verify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any requests for corrections to this data. Others suggested 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that we use the Global Positioning System to verify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
location. Some commenters suggested that the hospital's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emergency room entrance should be used as the hospital's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
location, others suggested the main employee entrance, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
still others suggested that a hospital be allowed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designate any point on its campus as its location. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
State Boundaries
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Some commenters stated that labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas should not be constructed across State lines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because cost of living and labor costs can vary greatly from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State to State due to differences in State tax structures, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs of health insurance and other employee benefit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs dependent on State law. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Blend/Floor
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_Some commenters suggested that a nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors wage index should not be adopted unless a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's own wage data were weighted more heavily than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those of its neighbors. Other commenters suggested that we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
institute a floor below which a hospital's wage index could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not fall or that we blend the current wage index with any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index based on revised labor market definitions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Analysis of Alternative Labor Market Areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We agree with commenters, and with ProPAC, that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improvements to the current labor market system should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
explored. Below, we discuss the feasibility of several 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative methodologies for establishing labor markets, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including revised versions of the current MSA-based system, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
various hospital-specific methodologies, as well as an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative that combines the hospital-specific and MSA-based methodologies. In evaluating each of the alternatives

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presented, we considered the major issues raised by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commenters, ProPAC's recommendations, as well as our own 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
internal research and analysis. ProPAC's data on air-mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
distances between hospitals were used to develop wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indexes based on hospital-specific systems. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We note that in our May 26, 1993 proposed rule (58 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
30244), we indicated that we would hold a meeting with a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
working group consisting of hospital industry 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representatives to review potential revisions to labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas, and other issues related to the wage index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On November 19, 1993, HCFA staff met with 17 representatives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of State and national hospital associations to discuss 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
options for redefining hospital labor market areas and other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issues related to the current wage index. A ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representative also attended and presented information on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the status of the Commission's current research. HCFA staff 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presented information on each of the options under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consideration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The group expressed no preference for any of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital-specific or MSA-based options with the possible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exception of a hospital-specific wage index based in large 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part on the hospital's own wages. The group was extremely 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concerned about the redistribution of dollars that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occur if nearest neighbors labor market areas were adopted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the current budgetary climate. It was also concerned 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
about how an exceptions process would function under any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revised labor market scheme, fearing that it would be too 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subjective and that it would be impossible to predict how 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
many hospitals would be reclassified. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. MSA-based and Hospital-Specific Alternatives.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In our labor market research, we reviewed a large 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of options: nine hospital-specific wage indexes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based on the nearest neighbor and fixed radius approaches 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and seven MSA-based indexes involving ways of subdividing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MSAs or statewide rural areas. Following is a description 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the options we reviewed. We have assigned each option a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
name to facilitate discussion and for use in tables that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
follow later in this section. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00056 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
Hospital-Specific Alternatives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
10 Nearest Neighbors_A wage index based on each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's own wage data and that of its 10 nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors, or all hospitals within a distance of 50 miles, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
if there are fewer than 10 other hospitals within 50 miles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One hundred ninety-five hospitals had no neighbor within 50 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles. These hospitals were assigned their own relative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage level, which is computed by comparing its own wages to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the national average hourly wage. The same 195 hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also were assigned their ``own wage index'' for the 15 Nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Neighbors and 20 Nearest Neighbors wage indexes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
10 Nearest Neighbors/Minimum of 2_A wage index based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each hospital's own wage data and that of its 10 nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors within 20 miles (with a minimum of at least 2 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nearest neighbors within 20 miles or all hospitals within 35 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
15 Nearest Neighbors_A wage index based on each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's own wage data and that of its 15 nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors, or all hospitals within a distance of 50 miles if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
there are fewer than 15 other hospitals within 50 miles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
15 Nearest Neighbors/Minimum of 2_A wage index based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each hospital's own wage data and that of its 15 nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors within 20 miles (with a minimum of at least 2 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nearest neighbors within 20 miles or all hospitals within 35 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
20 Nearest Neighbors_A wage index based on each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's own wage data and that of its 20 nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors, or all hospitals within 50 miles if there are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fewer than 20 other hospitals within 50 miles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
20-Mile Radius_A wage index based on each hospital's own 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage data and that of all its neighbors within a 20-mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
radius. The 887 hospitals having no neighbor within 20 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles were assigned their own relative wage level. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
25-Mile Radius_A wage index based on each hospital's own 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage data and that of all its neighbors within a 25-mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
radius. The 522 hospitals having no neighbor within 25 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles were assigned their own relative wage level. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
30-Mile Radius_A wage index based on each hospital's own 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage data and that of all its neighbors within a 30-mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
radius. The 391 hospitals having no neighbor within 30 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles were assigned their own relative wage level. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
35-Mile Radius_A wage index based on each hospital's own 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage data and that of all its neighbors within a 35-mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
radius. The 301 hospitals having no neighbor within 35 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles were assigned their own relative wage level. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
MSA-Based Alternatives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
These alternatives represent various subdivisions (by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
counties) of the current MSA/rural labor market area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definitions. As such, all counties in each subdivided 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
category need not be contiguous. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Central-Outlying_A wage index in which each MSA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
divided into 2 areas based on its central and outlying 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
counties. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rural-Population_A wage index in which each Statewide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rural area is divided into 2 distinct labor market areas, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one including all counties with populations greater than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
25,000 and one made up of counties with populations less 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than 25,000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rural-Density_A wage index in which each Statewide rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area is divided into 2 areas if it contains counties with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
population densities greater and less than 35 persons per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
square mile. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rural-Population/Density_A wage index in which each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Statewide rural area is divided into as many as 4 areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending on whether populations are greater or less than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
25,000 person and population densities are greater or less 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than 35 persons per square mile. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rural-Adjacent_A wage index in which each Statewide rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area is divided into 2 areas, one including all counties 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjacent to an MSA and the other made up of counties not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjacent to an MSA. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rural-Population/Adjacent_A wage index in which each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Statewide rural area is divided into as many as 4 areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending on the adjacent/non-adjacent status of its non-MSA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
counties and whether county populations are greater or less 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than 25,000 persons. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00057 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Rural-Density/Adjacent_A wage index in which each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Statewide rural area is divided into as many as 4 areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending on the adjacent/non-adjacent status of its non-MSA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
counties and whether county population densities are greater 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or less than 35 persons per square mile. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As a basis of comparison for these alternatives, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also reviewed three variations of the current MSA-based wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
Current MSA-based Indexes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Geographic_The FY 1994 hospital wage index without the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effects of geographic reclassification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reclassification_The actual FY 1994 hospital wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
after reclassification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Estimated FY 1995_The FY 1994 hospital wage index revised 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to include an estimate of the effects of geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification in FY 1995 (based on FY 1990 data). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We used three basic criteria to analyze each of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternatives: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Wage Conformity Within Labor Markets.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criterion assesses the extent to which a wage index conforms 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to a hospital's own relative wage level. It also evaluates 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the extent to which a hospital's wages are aberrant for its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor market area (that is, are more than one standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deviation above the labor market wage index); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Wage Index Conformity Across Labor Markets (Boundary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Problems).
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This criterion measures the extent to which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new labor market areas reflect uniform labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions as indicated by the degree of similarity in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital wage rates across labor market areas. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
boundaries should minimize inequitable treatment, that is, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cases in which hospitals facing similar labor costs are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
grouped into different labor markets. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Distributional Equity Improvement.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This criterion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
examines the impact of a labor market option to determine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its effects on the distribution of hospital payments, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the extent to which the hospitals that would gain and lose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under various proposals are already doing well or poorly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the current system. Redistribution of payments should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fairly compensate both high-wage hospitals and low-wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
None of the options we initially reviewed were a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant improvement over the current reclassified wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index in terms of wage conformity within labor markets, wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conformity across labor markets, or distributional equity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improvement. The following tables summarize our major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
results: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
Wage Conformity Within Labor Markets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Table A displays the number of hospitals for which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difference between the area wage index value for a given 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative and the hospital's own relative wage index value 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(determined by dividing the hospital's average hourly wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the national average hourly wage) is greater than .08 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
either positive or negative. A .08 difference in the wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index was selected as it represents approximately a $200 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difference in payments per case. For each wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
option, the Lose and Gain columns show the number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals for whom the difference represents a given 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decrease or increase in wage index value. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Table B displays the number of hospitals whose wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index values are more than 108 percent above their labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market wage index value (that is, one standard deviation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
greater). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
