<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00062 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
As demonstrated by the above tables, our analyses showed that none of the MSA-based options consistently outperformed
the current estimated FY 1995 post-reclassified wage index in terms of wage conformity within labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas and wage conformity across labor market areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to wage conformity within labor market areas, the results in Table A demonstrate that the 20-mile radius
hospital-specific option, with 549 losing hospitals, best reduces the number of hospitals with wage index values

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more than .08 below their own wage level. The FY 1995 post-reclassified wage index was second best, with 590 hospitals
with wage index values more than .08 below their own wage level. The 20-mile radius approach also produced the fewest
number of hospitals (1014) with wage index values more than .08 above their own wage levels. However, as demonstrated
in Table B, the estimated FY 1995 post-reclassified wage index outperformed the other labor market options, with
only 559 hospitals whose own average hourly wages were 108 percent or more of their respective labor market area's
average hourly wage. Therefore, the 20-mile radius and the estimated FY 1995 post-reclassified wage indexes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrated somewhat better outcomes for wage conformity within labor market areas than any of the other options
we studied. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to wage conformity across labor market areas (``boundary problems''), as indicated in Table C, the estimated
FY 1995 post-reclassified wage index generated the fewest hospitals having boundary problems (662), with the central-outlying
MSA option having the second lowest number of such hospitals (712). All of the nearest neighbor options, including
the 20-mile radius option (with 1,041 hospitals), had the effect of significantly increasing the number of boundary
problems that would occur, although the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
problems were of a smaller magnitude. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Overall, none of the MSA-based options or the nearest neighbor options consistently outperformed the current estimated
FY 1995 post-reclassification wage index. While the 20-mile radius option performed well in terms of wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conformity within labor market areas, it performed significantly worse than the current system with respect to wage
conformity across labor market areas. Additionally, with respect to the distributional effects of all the options,
none demonstrated any measurable improvement in payment equity across hospital groups. Therefore, we are not presenting
any tables concerning the distributional effect in this discussion. We have decided not to propose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes to labor market areas for FY 1995 because we believe that neither revisions to the current MSA-based system
or the nearest neighbors labor market options that we studied constitute a clearly demonstrable improvement over
the current system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. Options for Future Wage Index Refinements Using Combined 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MSA/Hospital-Specific Approach.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 While none of the alternative labor market areas that we studied, whether based on current MSA definitions or the ``nearest
neighbor'' approach, provided a distinct improvement over the current reclassification wage index, we believe
a classification system that uses a combination of both approaches has considerable potential for improving the
wage index. Discussed below are some options for further study that we believe may offer a viable alternative to the
current system by taking into consideration all of the varying factors that affect a hospital's labor market. Each
of these options is based on a weighted average of each hospital's own average hourly wages and the average hourly wages
of a group of other hospitals. The following concepts and assumptions underlie the wage index options that we are now
analyzing: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Empirically defining labor market areas in a highly accurate, definitive manner is extremely difficult,
if not impossible. MSAs and statewide rural areas may be reasonably accurate in many cases, but in other cases, these

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas may be larger than a hospital's ``true'' labor market area. None of the methodologies we studied for subdividing
MSAs and/or statewide rural areas or for designing nearest neighbor labor market areas proved to have been demonstrably
more accurate than the current system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00063 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;Both a hospital's own wage data and that of other hospitals in its vicinity may contain relevant information
about the level of wages in the hospital's true labor market. In particular, a hospital's own data may reflect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific circumstances of the hospital's labor market that we have yet to discover in our analysis of the data, but
that reflect geographic variation that Medicare would want to capture in the wage index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;No matter how we determine labor market areas, both a hospital's own wage data and that of other hospitals assigned
to its labor market area may contain any number of spurious sources of variation, including data errors, that should
not be captured in the wage index. The larger the number of hospitals whose data are averaged, the more the effects of
spurious factors are reduced. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To address all of these considerations, we have designed for comment and future study an approach that uses the current
MSA system but generally gives a hospital's own wages a higher weight than under the current system. (We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have assigned each hospital's own wages a weight sufficient to ensure that in no case would the weight of a hospital's
own wages be reduced below its current level.) We believe that a hospital's own wages, to some degree, reflect its specific
labor market conditions. Hence, a hospital's wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index would be more representative of these labor market conditions if a higher weight was assigned to its own wages
than typically occurs in large MSA or statewide rural area labor markets. If this approach results in situations in
which a hospital's wage level continues to be significantly higher than the combined average hourly wage used to construct
its wage index, we would provide an exceptions mechanism whereby a hospital's wage index would be derived entirely
or in part from its ``nearest neighbor'' labor market area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are considering two alternative wage indexes based on this approach. The first of two wage indexes we considered
places a 25 percent (.25) weight on each hospital's own average hourly wage and a 75 percent weight (.75) on the average
hourly wage of the other hospitals in each hospital's MSA or statewide rural area. However, if a hospital's data represent
more than 25 percent of the hours in the MSA or statewide rural area in which it is located, then, in calculating the hospital's
wage index, that higher percent was used instead of the 25 percent weight. The resulting weighted average hourly wage
is divided by the national average hourly wage to obtain each hospital's wage index value. For purposes of discussion,
this wage index is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
denoted as ``Minimum 25'' or ``M25''. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The second wage index differs from the first alternative only in that a minimum weight of .50 is given the hospital's
own average hourly wage, instead of a minimum 25 percent. This wage index is denoted as ``Minimum 50'' or ``M50''. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, we recognize that in some cases a hospital's immediate labor market area as defined under a ``nearest neighbor''
approach may be more representative of its true labor market area than an MSA-based labor market area. To address such
situations, we have developed a mechanism that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would essentially provide a hospital with an alternative wage index derived entirely or in part from its nearest neighbors
labor market. Currently, we are examining two methods for reclassification, a ``simple'' method and a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``refined'' method. Both methods utilize the two wage indexes just introduced, and like the current MGCRB reclassification
system, also require a hospital's own wages to exceed certain thresholds to meet eligibility. These thresholds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are as follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The first threshold would be used to identify hospitals whose own wage levels are still aberrant when using the Minimum
25 or Minimum 50 wage indexes. We set this threshold at the mean plus 1.0 standard deviation of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ratio of each hospital's own average hourly wage to the weighted sum of its own average hourly wage plus the average
hourly wage of the other hospitals in the MSA or Statewide rural area in which the hospital is located. For the M25 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index, the threshold would be 1.07. That is, a hospital's average hourly wage would have to equal at least 107 percent
of the weighted average hourly wage used to develop its wage index in order to qualify for reclassification. For the
M50 wage index, the threshold would be 1.05. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The second threshold, which would be used to identify hospitals whose wages are comparable to the hospitals
in the immediate (that is, nearest neighbor) labor market area, was set at 83 percent, which equals the mean minus 1
standard deviation of the ratio of each hospital's average hourly wage to the average hourly wage of its nearest 10
neighbors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under the ``simple'' reclassification methodology, there would be only a single adjustment available to a hospital
that demonstrates that its wages are aberrant within its labor market area (that is, it meets both thresholds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed above). The basis for the adjustment is evidence that the average hourly wage of the other hospitals in its
MSA or statewide rural area is not representative of the average hourly wage in the hospital's actual labor market.
A common reason that the MSA's average hourly wage may not be representative of a particular hospital's actual wage
costs is that the MSA is too large and comprises multiple labor markets. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To qualify for reclassification under this method, a hospital would have to meet a third criterion. The average hourly
wage of a hospital's nearest 10 neighbors (with a minimum of at least 2 neighbor hospitals within 20 miles or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all hospitals within 35 miles) would have to exceed the average hourly wage of the other hospitals in its MSA or statewide
rural area. If this is the case, then the average hourly wage of the hospital's nearest 10 neighbors would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substituted for the MSA or statewide rural average hourly wage in calculating the numerator of the hospital's wage
index. Under this reclassification scheme a hospital's own wages would be given the same weight as in the MSA-based
alternative wage index (M25 or M50). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00064 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The ``refined'' reclassification methodology would add a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
second basis for adjusting a reclassified hospital's wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index. This was developed to assist hospitals whose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
situation warrants a different nearest neighbor approach. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For example, it is possible that a hospital's data could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
represent more than 25 percent, or even 50 percent, of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total hours when combined with its 10 nearest neighbors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, the same hospital's data very likely might 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
represent a much lower percentage of the total hours in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MSA or statewide rural area in which it is located. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refined method would allow a hospital to use not only the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average hourly wage of its 10 nearest neighbors, but also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its hours weight when combined with its 10 nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors. So, in addition to the test of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representativeness of the other hospitals' average hourly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage described above, there would be a test of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriateness of substituting its hours weight from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's immediate labor market for the weight that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise be used (.25, .50, or its MSA/statewide rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hours weight). A reclassified hospital's hour weight 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reflected in the wage index would be adjusted under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method if two criteria are met. First, a hospital's share 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the total hours in its nearest 10 neighbors ``labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market'' would have to exceed its hour weight in its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MSA/statewide rural labor market. Second, the use of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nearest neighbors' hours weight would have to increase the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average hourly wage that comprises the numerator of its wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index value. If both criteria were met, then the nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors' weight would be substituted for the hours weight 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that would have been used were the hospital not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassified. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This refined reclassification methodology would allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for three different types of reclassification groups under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which a hospital's wage index would be calculated by using 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one of the following: (1) The nearest neighbors' average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hourly wage and the MSA hour weight; (2) the nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neighbors' hours weight and the MSA-based average hourly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage; or (3) both the nearest neighbors' weight and average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hourly wage. The following tables summarize our findings 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under each of the options we examined in comparison with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current labor market system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Table E displays the number of hospitals for which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difference between the area wage index value for a given 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative and the hospital's own relative wage index value 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(determined by dividing the hospital's average hourly wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the national average hourly wage) is greater than .08, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
either positive or negative. For each wage index option, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Lose and Gain columns show the number of hospitals for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whom the difference represents a given decrease or increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in wage index value. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Table F presents the assessment of wage conformity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
across labor markets. We determined that a ``boundary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
problem'' existed for a target hospital (that is, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital whose wage index value is being calculated) if_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Among nearby hospitals, one or more other hospitals' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index value is at least .04 greater than that of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
target hospital, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The hospital with the lower wage index value pays 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
higher wages than the hospital with the higher wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
value. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``Nearby hospitals'' were defined as the 20 nearest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals or if a hospital does not have 20 other hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within a 35 mile radius, all hospitals within a 35 mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
radius. Table F shows the number of hospitals with one or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more boundary problems and the average size of the wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index difference for those boundary problems. An average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index difference of .12 corresponds to a payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difference of about $300 per case. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

