<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
As demonstrated in the above tables, we believe these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
options for refining the wage index show promising results. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
All four of the wage index options significantly reduce the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of boundary problems, as seen in Table F. Not only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would the number of boundary problems be reduced, but the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
boundary problems that do remain would be smaller, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
especially for the M50 wage indexes where the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difference is 0.067 for both the simple and refined 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification methods. As indicated in Table E, these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index options produce wage index values that would more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
closely resemble a hospital's own wage level, as was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected. Table G demonstrates that the new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification methods would likely affect a larger number 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of hospitals than the current system. Whereas in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current system, we estimated that only 362 hospitals qualify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the wage index reclassification, under the M25 wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index the simpler reclassification method would lead to 411 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals being adjusted to their nearest neighbors' average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hourly wage. The refined reclassification method under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
M25 wage index would make it possible for 250 hospitals to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use the nearest neighbors' average hourly wage, 161 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals to use both the nearest neighbors' average hourly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage and hours weight, and 110 hospitals to use just the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nearest neighbors' hours weight. We show the effects the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
four new options would have when compared to the estimated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1995 post-reclassified system against the current 1994 pre-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassified values in Table H. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We plan to continue our evaluation of these new wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index options in the coming months and are soliciting public 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment. We will continue to accept comments on these wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index refinements until August 31, 1994. We will respond to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these comments in the FY 1996 prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed rule. Comments on this issue should be sent to: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Lana Price, Director; Division of Hospital Payment Policy; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1&hyph;H&hyph;1 East Low Rise; 6325 Security Boulevard; Baltimore, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Maryland 21207. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While we believe the alternatives discussed above offer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
much promise with respect to refining the hospital wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index, we do not believe that sections 1886(d)(2), (3) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(8) of the Act authorize a hospital-specific wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scheme that combines each hospital's own wages and the wages 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of other hospitals in its MSA or Statewide rural area using 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different hour weights. Moreover, the MGCRB provisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under section 1886(d)(10) of the Act contemplate wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassifications from one area to another that cannot be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonably implemented consistent with these alternatives. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, before we could implement these alternatives, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
legislative changes would be necessary to allow the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Secretary greater flexibility in refining the hospital wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index. Again, we invite public comments on these issues by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
August 31, 1994, so that we may consider them in our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continuing evaluation of wage index refinements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00068 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=4 -->
H. State Labor Market Option 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
In addition to the alternatives discussed above, we are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also considering a State labor market option (SLMO) under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which hospitals would be able to voluntarily design labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas within their own State boundaries. Although we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are not formally proposing the SLMO at this time, we welcome 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comments on this option. Under the SLMO, an application to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
design labor market areas could be submitted by a group of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals or any entity that represents hospitals that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be affected under the SLMO (for example, a state hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
association, the Office of the Governor, etc.). The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application would have to include a statement signed by each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's chief executive or chief financial officer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supporting participation in the SLMO. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The SLMO is being considered on the premise that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals within a State may, in some circumstances, have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
better information than HCFA concerning local labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
competition between hospitals. Accordingly, we are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considering the SLMO along with the alternatives discussed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above, which we believe would require legislative changes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Absent changes to the statute, we believe that the SLMO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would offer opportunities for hospitals to design labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas that are more reflective of local wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions than the current system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
1. Criteria for Participating in a SLMO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Below we describe some of the criteria we would apply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to determine whether labor market areas under the SLMO could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be approved. (We have not fully designed or specified the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limitations for creating labor market areas under the SLMO, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and we are interested in any comments from the public for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
developing criteria under which a SLMO would be approved.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, we envision that hospitals participating in a SLMO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
could design labor market areas under the following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restrictions: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;We are considering limiting approval of labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas under the SLMO to cases in which there is unanimous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
support among hospitals. (We may also consider use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``overwhelming support'' as a criterion rather than unanimous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
support. We likely would specify a percentage of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participating hospitals that must support the SLMO labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market areas (for example, 75 percent). Such a criterion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may also specify that to be approved, the SLMO labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area must be supported by each hospital that would lose more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than 5 percent of payments relative to the current system.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Aggregate payments to hospitals participating in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SLMO could be no higher than they otherwise would have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the absence of the SLMO. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;All hospitals that participate in the SLMO must sign an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application for it to become effective. Although the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application could come from a group of hospitals or its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representatives, each participating hospital must sign the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application and agree to its provisions. In addition, each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participating hospital must indicate in the application (or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a separate agreement) that: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The participating hospital waives its rights to any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index it would otherwise receive absent the SLMO, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including a wage index it might receive through geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The hospital understands how its participation in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SLMO would affect its prospective payments, including an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
understanding that the hospital's wage index could be higher 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or lower under the SLMO in comparison to its wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under labor market areas in the absence of the SLMO. (The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SLMO application would only have to specify that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital understands the effect of HCFA regulations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regarding SLMOs and that there may be a reduction of their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index as a result of participation. The hospital would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not have to indicate the precise level of its wage index in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the SLMO application.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Labor market areas under the SLMO would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
automatically renewed each year for the purpose of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculating the wage index until one or more participating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals withdraws its support for the SLMO. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Labor market areas under the SLMO generally would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prohibited from crossing State boundaries. The only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exception would be that hospitals from a bordering State may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be included in a SLMO labor market area if they are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently located in an MSA that crosses a State boundary. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In this case, the wage index would be calculated as if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals in the bordering State were included under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SLMO labor market area. For hospitals in the bordering 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State, the wage index would continue to be calculated as it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is under the current system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00069 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Section 1886(d)(3)(E) of the Act requires that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Secretary establish the wage index for the purposes of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjusting payment rates for hospital inpatient services to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reflect wages in a geographic area relative to the national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average. For this reason, we are considering establishing a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criterion that labor market areas under the SLMO must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include all hospitals within a contiguous area or must have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
defined geographic boundaries or distance parameters. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, we are concerned that such a requirement may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevent hospitals in noncontiguous areas within a State that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
share similar demographic features from being grouped in one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor market area under the SLMO. We are also concerned 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that hospitals may be able to meet the contiguity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement but design an area with an illogical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
configuration that may not represent a labor market area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For example, we do not believe it would be appropriate to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve an application for a new labor market area that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
groups 2 hospitals 100 miles apart but excludes the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals located in between. We are also concerned about 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approving a SLMO application under which the wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be based only on hospital-specific wages. Such an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application would not reflect local market conditions based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on contiguous geographic areas. Because of these concerns, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we are soliciting public comments regarding appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria for approving labor market areas under the SLMO. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
2. Calculation of Budget Neutrality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
As noted above, we would require payments to hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the SLMO to be budget neutral in relation to payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the absence of the SLMO. We are considering alternatives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for applying a budget neutrality adjustment. Because the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
budget neutrality calculation for the SLMO labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas would be made after the effects of reclassification, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we expect that hospitals under a SLMO will continue to apply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for reclassification as they do under the current system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Such reclassifications would be recognized both for purposes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of computing the budget neutrality adjustment and computing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the wage index values applicable to reclassified hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We envision two options for applying the budget neutrality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Adjusting the wage index for the SLMO labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under this option, there would be a single 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment to the wage index that would affect all hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participating in the SLMO in equal proportions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Adjusting the wage index for selected hospitals within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the SLMO labor market areas. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under this option, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application to create labor market areas under SLMO would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specify how the budget neutrality adjustment would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applied. For example, it could be applied equally to all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SLMO hospitals or it could be limited to selected hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If the SLMO application does not specify how to apply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the budget neutrality adjustment, the adjustment to the wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index would have a proportional effect on payments to all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals participating in a SLMO labor market area. (As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indicated above, reclassification granted to hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participating in a SLMO would be recognized in computing the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index values applicable to non-SLMO reclassified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals under section 1886(d)(8)(C) of the Act.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Effect on Hospitals Not Participating in the SLMO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Hospitals that are not part of labor market areas under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the SLMO would continue to have their wage index calculated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the current labor market areas_including the effects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of reclassification. The wage index for these hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would continue to reflect the average hourly wage in their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current labor market area relative to the national average. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If a non-SLMO hospital were reclassified to an MSA that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes hospitals that are part of a SLMO labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area, the wage index for the non-SLMO hospital would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculated as if the SLMO did not exist. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We note that hospitals in SLMO States that are not part 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of a redesigned labor market area would not necessarily have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be part of the budget neutrality adjustment and their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
support for the SLMO would not be needed. However, if they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
chose to be part of the budget neutrality adjustment, their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
support for the SLMO would be needed even if they were not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
included in a redesigned labor market areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Process for Submitting a SLMO Proposal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We are considering establishing the process described 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below for establishing labor market areas under the SLMO. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given that hospitals may apply both for geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification by the MGCRB and for a revised labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area under the SLMO in the same year, our time frames for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SLMO decisions may parallel the time frames used for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification decisions by the MGCRB. However, we expect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that approval or rejection of SLMO applications would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
made by HCFA's Bureau of Policy Development. The potential 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process would consist of the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) The initial SLMO application would be made to HCFA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and would include a statement from each participating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital attesting to their support for labor market areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the SLMO. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) We envision providing notification to applicants 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of approval of the SLMO labor market areas before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
publication of our annual prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed rule (usually published in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
May of each year). For hospitals whose SLMO application has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been denied, we are considering providing an opportunity to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submit additional information in support of a SLMO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application. This information would be used by HCFA in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reconsidering a denial before publication of the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00070 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(3) Approval of SLMO labor market areas and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resulting proposed wage indices would be announced in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed rule. The proposed rule would include the wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index under the current labor market areas (including the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effects of reclassification) and under the SLMO labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Hospitals participating in the SLMO would be able 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to withdraw support for the SLMO during the 60-day comment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period following publication of the proposed rule. If one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or more hospitals withdraws support for a labor market area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the SLMO, the SLMO would not go into effect in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following fiscal year. To withdraw from the SLMO, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participating hospital would submit a letter to HCFA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
referencing the appropriate proposed rule and stating its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participation in the SLMO labor market area is being 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
withdrawn. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(5) Absent any withdrawal of support for labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas under the SLMO, HCFA would announce labor market areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and wage indices under the SLMO in the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system final rule (published by September 1 of each year). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(6) The labor market areas under the SLMO would be in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect for at least 1 Federal fiscal year following approval 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by HCFA. The SLMO labor market areas would be automatically 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
renewed for following years unless a participating hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
informed HCFA in writing of its decision to withdraw. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5. Special Issues Affecting the SLMO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We are considering the following policies that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address the effects of special situations on the SLMO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concept. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
New Hospitals_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
If a new hospital is established in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SLMO labor market area after its approval, the wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the new hospital would be determined as it is under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current system for the remainder of the fiscal year. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order for the SLMO labor market area to be renewed for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following fiscal year, the new hospital would have to inform 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
HCFA in writing of its support for the SLMO labor market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Ownership Changes and Hospital Mergers
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_If there is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an ownership change or hospital merger during the period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the SLMO labor market area is in effect, the SLMO would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
remain in effect for the remainder of the fiscal year. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order for the SLMO labor market area to be renewed for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following fiscal year, the new ownership would have to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inform HCFA in writing of its support for the SLMO labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We will accept comments on the SLMO until August 31, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1994. These comments should be sent directly to: Lana 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Price, Director; Division of Hospital Payment Policy; 1&hyph;H&hyph;1 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
East Low Rise; 6325 Security Boulevard; Baltimore, Maryland 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
21207. Responses to these comments will appear in the FY 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1996 prospective payment system proposed rule.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
I. Conclusion
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Since the inception of the prospective payment system, we have repeatedly solicited comments concerning how labor
market areas used to construct the wage index should be defined. In recent years, we have also solicited comments concerning
specific proposals to modify or replace our current use of MSAs. For example, we have offered for public comment the
reclassification criteria used by the MGCRB and alternatives to the MSA-based system such as ProPAC's nearest neighbor
proposal and the options outlined in the present rule. In essence, therefore, we have been engaged in a continuing
dialogue with the public concerning whether it is appropriate to use MSAs to define labor market areas to construct
the wage index.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On June 30, 1993, the Office of Management and Budget announced the revised definitions for Metropolitan Areas (MA)
to reflect the MA standards published on March 30, 1990, and the demographic data drawn from the 1990 Decennial Census.
In that bulletin, OMB stated that in cases where an agency is publishing for comment a proposed regulation that would
use the MA definitions for a nonstatistical purpose, the agency should seek public comment on the proposed use of the
MA definitions. As indicated above, we are not proposing the options outlined in sections III G and H at this time. However,
consistent with OMB's bulletin and as part of our continuing review of the definitions used to define labor market
areas for wage index purposes, we are soliciting comments on the use of MSA definitions as incorporated in the options
described in this document.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00071 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
IV. Other Proposed Changes to the Prospective Payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
System for Inpatient Operating Costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Definition of and Payment for Transfer Cases (&sect;412.4) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The prospective payment system distinguishes between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``discharges'', situations in which a patient leaves an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acute-care hospital after receiving complete treatment, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``transfers'', situations in which the patient is transferred 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to another acute-care hospital for related care. If a full 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DRG payment were made to each hospital involved in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transfer situation irrespective of the length of time the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient spent in the ``sending'' hospital prior to transfer, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this would create a strong incentive to increase transfers, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thereby unnecessarily endangering patients' health. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, the regulations at &sect;412.4(d) provide that, in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transfer situation, full payment is made to the final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discharging hospital and each transferring hospital is paid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a per diem rate for each day of the stay, not to exceed the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
full DRG payment that would have been made if the patient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
had been discharged without being transferred. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Currently, the per diem rate paid to a transferring 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital is determined by dividing the full DRG payment that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would have been paid in a nontransfer situation by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geometric mean length-of-stay for the DRG into which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
case falls. Transferring hospitals are also eligible for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlier payments for cases that meet the cost outlier 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria established for all other cases (nontransfer and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transfer cases alike) classified to the DRG. They are not, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
however, eligible for day outlier payments. Two exceptions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the transfer payment policy are transfer cases classified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into DRG 385 (Neonates, Died or Transferred to Another Acute 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Care Facility) or DRG 456 (Burns, Transferred to Another 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Acute Care Facility), which are not paid on a per diem basis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
but instead receive the full DRG payment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Definition of a Transfer Case 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under current policy, cases that are transferred from an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acute-care area paid under the prospective payment system to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital or unit excluded from the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system are considered to be discharges (as opposed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transfers) from the acute-care hospital. As a discharge, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment for the case is the full DRG amount. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The rationale for this policy was set forth in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
initial hospital inpatient prospective payment interim final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule with comment period, which was published on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
September 1, 1983. In that document, we stated that: ``*&blank;*&blank;* 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Hospitals and units excluded from the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system are organized for treatment of conditions distinctly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unlike treatment encountered in short-term acute care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities. Therefore, the services obtained in excluded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities would not be the same services obtained in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transferring hospitals (that is, paid under the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system), and payment to both facilities would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate.'' (See 48 FR 39759.) Thus, since the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment hospital was assumed to be providing a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
full course of acute inpatient care before transfer to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excluded hospital or unit for additional care and treatment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we believed it was appropriate to pay the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment hospital the full DRG amount. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For the most part, inpatient hospital services furnished 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by hospitals and hospital units excluded from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system are paid on a reasonable cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basis, subject to a cost per discharge limit. This payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
does not vary by source of admission or discharge 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
destination. Under &sect;&sect;412.22 through 412.29, the following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
types of facilities are identified as being excluded from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the prospective payment system: psychiatric hospitals and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
distinct part units; rehabilitation hospitals and distinct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part units; long-term care hospitals; cancer hospitals; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
children's hospitals; hospitals outside the 50 States, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
District of Columbia, or Puerto Rico; Veterans 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Administration and other Federal hospitals; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonparticipating hospitals furnishing emergency services to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare beneficiaries. Also listed in &sect;412.22(c) (2) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) are hospitals paid under approved State cost control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
systems and hospitals reimbursed in accordance with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorized demonstration projects. Under &sect;412.4(b)(3)(i), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patients moving from an acute-care hospital paid under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system to hospitals within either of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these categories are currently considered transfers, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therefore they are not referred to in the following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussion. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since implementation of the prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
over 10 years ago the distinctions between the types of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services provided in acute-care hospitals and excluded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals and hospital units have become less clear. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Acute-care hospitals may have an incentive to reduce patient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lengths of stay, and thus patient costs, in order to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maximize profits since payment is no longer linked to actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs. This has led to a steady increase in the numbers of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excluded hospitals and units, as well as postacute-care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities, as acute-care hospitals look to discharge 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patients whom heretofore they were treating until the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patients were ready to be discharged to their homes. (See 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Langenbruner et al., 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Health Care Financing Review, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Spring 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1989.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As part of a study of Medicare transfer cases funded by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
HCFA (``Transfers of Medicare Hospital Patients under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Prospective Payment System'', PM&hyph;191&hyph;HCFA, January 1994), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RAND examined trends in transfer episodes from FY 1987 to FY 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1991. RAND's analysis found that, from 1987 to 1991, while 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transfers from one prospective payment system setting to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
another increased 4.1 percent, transfers from a prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system setting to excluded rehabilitation units and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excluded psychiatric units increased 18.9 percent and 7.0 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent, respectively. We believe that this growth in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transfers to excluded hospital units is an indication that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the distinction in the types of services provided in short-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
term acute-care settings and excluded settings is not as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant as it was in 1983. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00072 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In addition, in section VI.A. of this preamble, we are proposing revised criteria for determining whether an entity
qualifies for exclusion from the prospective payment system as a separate long-term care hospital. This proposed
change is in response to situations where hospitals are manipulating the process for recognition as a separate Medicare
provider in order to circumvent the prospective payment system exclusion rules. For example, some hospitals have
begun to organize themselves under what they themselves refer to as the ``hospital within a hospital'' model. The
effect of such a reorganization is to grant an exclusion to what is, for all practical purposes, a long-term care hospital
unit, even though section 1886(d)(1)(B) of the Act limits the exclusion from the prospective payment system for hospital
units to rehabilitation and psychiatric units. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe that the motivation for such arrangements is primarily financial and may lead to inappropriate transfers
that are not necessarily in the best interests of patients' health and well-being. We strongly believe that patients
should be treated in settings that are best suited to provide the levels of care the patients need. Decisions regarding
the most appropriate setting should be based on clinical criteria. To help ensure that this occurs, to the greatest
extent practicable, financial incentives should be neutral regarding the setting wherein a patient is treated.
This means matching payments as nearly as possible to the resources required to treat the patient. We believe our current
definition of transfer cases provides financial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentives on the acute-care side to transfer patients to excluded hospitals and units in order to reduce lengths
of stay and at the same time receive payment at the full DRG amount. These incentives exist whether the acute-care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital is transferring patients to its hospital-based excluded units or to other excluded hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For these reasons, we are proposing to change our definition of a transfer case by adding new &sect;412.4(b)(4) to
include cases transferred from an acute-care setting paid under the prospective payment system to an excluded hospital

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or unit. We believe that this policy would more appropriately pay those prospective payment hospitals that are transferring
patients to an excluded hospital or unit before completion of a course of treatment. This policy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would extend to cases transferred to all excluded hospitals and units, Veterans Administration hospitals, other
Federal hospitals, and hospitals not participating in Medicare. Discharges to non-hospital settings, such as skilled
nursing facilities and intermediate care facilities, would still be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paid as discharges. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Payment for Transfer Cases 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Since the inception of the prospective payment system, there has been concern that a flat per diem payment rate for
transfer cases fails to account for the likelihood that the beginning of a patient's hospitalization is the most resource
intensive portion of the stay. Comments received in response to the September 1, 1983 interim final rule recommended
that the transferring hospital should receive either the full DRG amount or should be paid on a sliding scale to reflect
the higher costs of the first few days of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient's stay. Our response at that time was that little or no data were provided in support of this position (49 FR
245, January 3, 1984). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Last year, in the May 26, 1993 proposed rule, we responded to a ProPAC recommendation concerning payment of transfer
cases and described the preliminary results of the analysis undertaken by RAND to evaluate the adequacy of our transfer
payment policy (58 FR 30245). RAND found that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
among cases transferred prior to reaching the mean length-of-stay, 1-day stays cost 2.096 times the per diem payment
amount for cases in nonsurgical DRGs (based on the geometric mean length of stay) and 2.576 times the per diem for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
surgical DRGs. Among nonsurgical transfer cases, the costs of 2-day stays are about 1.215 times the per diem payment
amount, and cases transferred after 2 days cost about 10 percent more than the applicable per diem amount. Among 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
surgical cases, the costs of stays of 2 or more days are actually about 7 percent below the applicable per diem amount.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To evaluate the impact of replacing the flat per diem methodology with one designed to reflect the observed relationship
between costs and the first few days of hospitalization, RAND simulated a transfer payment methodology that multiplies
the per diem amounts by the coefficients referred to above. The improvement in payment-to-cost ratios for per diem
transfer cases was significant, from 0.7224 under current policy to 0.9722 using the scaled per diem. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We did not propose a change to the transfer payment policy last year, however, due to concerns over the specification
of the transfer payment formula. First, the coefficients used to graduate the per diem amount are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dependent on the specification of the model and the data employed. Ensuring the continued validity of the per diem
weighting factors would require frequent reestimation as other payment parameters change and more recent data become
available. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Second, we were concerned that weighting the per diem amounts by the coefficients directly from the regression may
overstate the precision of the estimates of costs per day prior to transfer. Because available data do not attribute
charges or costs to a particular day, the estimated costs for each additional day reflect the incrementally higher
costs per case compared to transfers occurring 1 day earlier. If, in transfers occurring after 3 or more days, for example,
costs per day were more evenly distributed than the graduated per diem payments, the coefficients in the regression
might not reflect the costs of additional days. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00073 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This leads to a third concern, that the graduation of the scaled per diem amounts may generate inappropriate incentives
to transfer patients as soon as possible, that is, before the point at which costs equal payments. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentive could arise, for example, for cases in which payments early in a patient's stay are greater than costs (that
is, cases with more even costs throughout the stay rather than high costs in the first day or two). In such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cases, the longer the patient stays in the hospital, the more likely that total costs will approach total payments.
Under this scenario, a hospital seeking to maximize its profit could do so by transferring the patient as soon as possible.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to address these concerns and to pay hospitals more appropriately for the treatment they furnish to patients
before transfer, we are proposing at &sect;412.4(d)(1) to pay transfers twice the per diem amount for the first day

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of any transfer stay plus the per diem amount for each of the remaining days prior to transfer, up to the full DRG amount.
We believe this method will improve the payment equity for transfer cases in a straightforward way while 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alleviating our concerns about a steeply graduated per diem based directly on regression coefficients. We are proposing
that this change be applied uniformly for both medical and surgical transfer cases; although surgical transfer cases
appear to be more costly, on average, the first day, they are relatively less costly for the second day and beyond. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Using the graduated per diem methodology we are proposing, RAND estimated the payment-to-cost ratio of transfer
cases that were transferred prior to reaching the geometric mean length of stay would be 0.9321. While this is somewhat
less than the payment-to-cost ratio for nontransfer cases (0.9645), it represents a significant improvement over
the current ratio for these cases (0.7224). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since publication of the September 1, 1993 final rule, RAND has completed the second and final phase of its study. This
phase of the study focused on the receiving hospitals and how they fare under our current transfer payment policy.
Their findings in this regard were more ambiguous than in the case of sending hospitals. RAND performed regressions
on transfer cases in receiving hospitals to determine whether these cases were more expensive than nontransfer cases
within the same DRGs. They found no consistent relationship. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Over 60 percent of all transfer cases were assigned DRGs from MDC 5 (Diseases and Disorders of the Circulatory System).
According to RAND, ``*&blank;*&blank;* for the surgical DRGs in MDC 5 (DRGs 104 through 116), the costs of transfer
cases relative to nontransfer cases at the same hospital range from almost the same (in DRG 106, coefficient = 0.007)
to about 11 percent greater (DRG 105, coefficient = 0.105). In the medical DRGs in MDC 5, the range is even wider, with
transfers received into DRG 121 and 122 actually costing less than other cases in the same DRG at the same hospital and
transfers received in DRG 127 costing 37 percent more.'' (See RAND page 40.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Based on these results, RAND did not recommend and we are not proposing any change in the payments for the receiving
hospital in a transfer episode. That is, if the receiving hospital is also the final discharging hospital, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
they will be paid the full DRG amount plus any outlier payments they are eligible to receive (under either the day or
cost outlier thresholds). If the patient is transferred again prior to discharge, then, under the change we are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing, the sending hospital in this second transfer episode would be paid using the graduated per diem methodology,
rather than the flat per diem rate they currently receive. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Review of DRG Assignments (&sect;412.60) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under the provisions of &sect;412.60(d), a hospital has 60 days from the date of the notice of the initial DRG assignment
of a claim to request review of that assignment. The hospital may submit additional information as part of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its request. The intermediary reviews the request and any additional information and decides if a change in the assignment
is appropriate. Any change by the intermediary to a higher-weighted DRG must be reviewed by the hospital's Peer Review
Organization (PRO) to determine if the request and the change are appropriate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under the first PRO contract cycle, this review was conducted on a postpayment basis. However, the second PRO contract,
effective July 1, 1986, required that this review be conducted on a prepayment basis. Therefore, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;412.60(d)(2) currently provides that the intermediary must request that the PRO review any change in assignment
to a higher-weighted DRG. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00074 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under the fourth contract cycle, the PROs are no longer required to review these cases on a prepayment basis. Rather,
the cases are flagged and the PRO conducts a 100 percent review after payment. Therefore, we are proposing to revise
&sect;412.60(d)(2) to conform the regulations to current practice. At the same time, we would revise an incorrect
cross-reference to the regulations that govern the PROs' review of these DRG reassignments. The correct cross-reference
is &sect;466.71(c)(2). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. National Average Standardized Amounts for FY 1995 (&sect;412.63) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 1886(d)(3)(A) of the Act directs the Secretary to compute national average standardized amounts for use
in determining payments for inpatient operating services, updated annually by the applicable percentage increase
set 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forth under section 1886(b)(3)(B) of the Act. Under section 1886(d)(3)(A)(ii) of the Act, for FYs 1988 through 1994,
the Secretary has computed separate national average standardized amounts for large urban, other urban and rural

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. However, section 1886(d)(3)(A)(iii) of the Act requires that, for discharges occurring after October 1,
1994, the average standardized amounts for hospitals located in a rural area shall be equal to the average standardized
amount for hospitals located in an other urban area. We note that hospitals located in a rural area will continue to
be considered rural for all other payment purposes, such as the disproportionate share adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Secretary has been applying updates to the average standardized amounts for each fiscal year as provided under
section 1886(b)(3)(B) of the Act. For FY 1995, section 1886(b)(3)(B)(i)(X) of the Act directs the Secretary to update
the rural national average standardized amount by the amount necessary to make it equal to the other urban national
average standardized amount. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Current &sect;412.63(m) already reflects the requirement that, for discharges occurring after October 1, 1994,
we will pay for inpatient hospital services based on national average standardized amounts for large urban and other

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. Thus, there will no longer be separate national average standardized amounts for other urban and rural areas.
However, we are proposing to make several technical changes to &sect;412.63(m) and (r), concerning computation
of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal rates, to eliminate now obsolete references to the rural and other urban standardized amounts and replace
them with references to the standardized amounts for other areas. In the addendum to this proposed rule, we provide
a description of the updates being proposed for FY 1995 under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the requirements of sections 1886(b)(3)(B)(i)(X) and 1886(d)(3)(A)(iv) of the Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
D. Outliers (&sect;&sect;412.80, 412.82 and 412.84) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 1886(d)(5)(A) of the Act provides that, in addition to the basic prospective payment rates, payments must
be made for discharges involving day outliers and may be made for cost outliers. Under section 1886(d)(3)(B) of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Act, the Secretary has been required to separately reduce the urban and rural national standardized amount by
the proportion of estimated total DRG payments attributable to outliers in each respective area. Beginning with

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995, section 1886(d)(3)(B) of the Act, as amended by section 4002(c)(2)(B)(iii) of Public Law 101&hyph;508,
requires the Secretary to reduce the large urban and other national standardized amounts by the same factor to account
for the proportion of total DRG payments made to outlier cases. (As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
explained in section IV.C. of this preamble, section 1886(d)(3)(A)(iii) of the Act specifies that beginning in FY
1995, the standardized amount for other urban and rural areas will be equal, with the result that there will be only

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
two standardized amounts, one for large urban areas and one for all other areas.) Section 1886(d)(9)(B)(iv) of the
Act requires that the urban and other standardized amounts applicable to hospitals in Puerto Rico be reduced by the

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proportion of estimated total DRG payments attributable to estimated outlier payments. Furthermore, section 1886(d)(5)(A)(iv)
of the Act directs that outlier payments in any year may not be less than 5 percent nor more than 6 percent of total payments
projected or estimated to be made based on the prospective payment rates. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 13501(c) of the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1993 (Public Law 103&hyph;66) amended section 1886(d)(5)(A)
of the Act with respect to outliers beginning in FY 1995. With regard to cost outliers, section 13501(c) of Public Law
103&hyph;66 requires that the Secretary modify the methodology for determining the cost outlier threshold. Formerly,
section 1886(d)(5)(A)(ii) of the Act specified that a hospital can receive payment for a cost outlier if the adjusted
costs for a discharge exceed the greater of a fixed dollar amount or a fixed multiple the DRG payment for the case. As
amended by section 13501(c)(2) of Public Law 103&hyph;66, section 1886(d)(5)(A)(ii) of the Act now specifies that,
for discharges on or after October 1, 1994, a hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may receive payment for a cost outlier if adjusted costs exceed the DRG prospective payment rate plus a fixed dollar
amount determined by the Secretary. We will refer to this revised cost outlier threshold as ``fixed loss.'' A further
discussion of the methodology for determining the fixed loss 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is provided below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 13501(c) of Public Law 103&hyph;66 also amended section 1886(d)(5)(A) of the Act with respect to day outliers.
Beginning with FY 1995, section 1886(d)(5)(A) of the Act requires the Secretary to reduce the proportion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total outlier payments paid under the day outlier methodology. Under the requirements of section 1886(d)(5)(A)(v)
of the Act, the proportion of outlier 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments paid under the day outlier methodology, relative to the proportion of outlier payments in FY 1994, shall
be 75 percent in FY 1995, 50 percent in FY 1996 and 25 percent in FY 1997. Under the provisions of section 1886(d)(5)(A)(i)
of the Act, the Secretary will no longer pay for day outliers after September 30, 1997. As indicated in the table published
in our September 1, 1993 final rule (58 FR 46348), we estimated that 18 percent of FY 1994 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlier payments would be for outliers meeting the day outlier threshold only, while 13 percent would meet the day
and cost outlier thresholds and be paid under the day outlier methodology. Thus, a total of 31.3 percent of total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlier payments in FY 1994 would be paid as day outlier cases. Pursuant to section 1886(d)(5)(A) of the Act, we propose
setting the day outlier thresholds for FY 1995 through FY 1998 so that day outlier payments approximate the following
proportion of total outlier payments: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;FY 1995_24 percent (75 percent of 31.3 percent). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;FY 1996_16 percent (50 percent of 31.3 percent). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;FY 1997_8 percent (25 percent of 31.3 percent). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As payments to day outliers are reduced, there will be a corresponding increase in payments to cost outliers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00075 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. FY 1995 Outlier Thresholds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For FY 1994, the day outlier threshold is the geometric mean length of stay for each DRG plus the lesser of 23 days or 3.0
standard deviations. The marginal cost factor (or the percent of Medicare's average per diem payment paid for each
outlier day) for day outliers is equal to 55 percent in FY 1994. The cost outlier threshold is the greater of 2.0 times
the prospective payment rate for the DRG or $36,000 ($33,000 for hospitals that have not yet entered the prospective
payment system for inpatient capital-related costs). The marginal cost factor (or the percent of costs paid after
costs for the case exceed the threshold) for cost outliers is 75 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For FY 1995, we propose to set the day outlier threshold at the geometric mean length of stay for each DRG plus the lesser
of 22 days or 3.0 standard deviations. Section 13501(c)(3) of Public Law 103&hyph;66 provides that the additional
payments for outlier cases may be different than the hospital's marginal cost of care during the transition period
phasing out payments to day outliers. We are proposing to revise &sect;412.80 to reflect this provision. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also propose to revise &sect;412.82(c), so that it does not specify a marginal cost factor of 55 percent. HCFA will
specify the level of the marginal cost factor during the day outlier phase-out in the annual prospective payment system
rulemaking process. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our proposed policy would reduce the proportion of outlier payments paid to day outliers as required by section 1886(d)(5)(A)
of the Act. We are proposing to accomplish the required reduction in payments to day outliers for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995 solely through a reduction in the marginal cost factor rather than through an increase in the threshold. To
determine the marginal cost factor necessary to achieve the mandated reduction in outlier payments, we simulated
both the current and proposed outlier policies. Based on these simulations, we determined that a reduction in the
marginal cost factor to 49 percent is necessary to achieve the required 25 percent reduction in the proportion of payments
paid under the day outlier methodology. Therefore, we are proposing to reduce the marginal cost factor from 55 percent
in FY 1994 to 49 percent in FY 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are also proposing a fixed loss cost outlier threshold in FY 1995 equal to the prospective payment rate for the DRG
plus $23,300 ($21,400 for hospitals that have not yet entered the prospective payment system for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital-related costs). The fixed loss threshold would replace the current threshold, which is equal to the greater
of 2.0 multiplied by the DRG payment for the case or $36,000. We are proposing the fixed loss threshold under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the requirements of section 1886(d)(5)(A) of the Act. We believe the fixed loss threshold is an improvement over current
policy because it focuses Medicare's outlier payments on the most costly cases. Under current policy, it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is possible that cases in certain high-weight DRGs can incur losses significantly greater than $36,000 before Medicare
will make an outlier payment. (We note that the fixed loss threshold is adjusted by the wage index, similar to the current
cost outlier threshold. Thus, hospitals that are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paid based on a wage index value of less than 1.0 will have this threshold reduced while other hospitals will be paid
based on a higher threshold.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To further focus Medicare's cost outlier payments on the costliest cases, we are proposing to increase the marginal
cost factor from 75 percent to 80 percent. We note that raising the marginal cost factor to 80 percent is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent with a recommendation made by ProPAC in its March 1, 1994 annual report. While we are raising the marginal
cost factor for cost outlier cases to 80 percent, we are continuing to set the cost and day outlier thresholds in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to maintain the estimated 5.1 percent proportion of total DRG payments paid as outliers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The thresholds we would propose if we were not implementing the change in the day and cost outlier payment methodology
required by section 1886(d)(5)(A)(v) of the Act and discussed above would be: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Day outliers_The geometric mean length of stay for each DRG plus the lesser of 21 days or 3 standard deviations.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Cost outliers_The greater of 2.0 times the prospective payment rate for the DRG or $31,000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We estimate that the proposed FY 1995 outlier thresholds would result in 76 percent of outlier cases paid using the
cost outlier methodology and 24 percent paid using the day outlier methodology. Cases that meet the day outlier threshold
but that would be paid using the cost outlier methodology, because it yields the higher payment, would represent 20
percent of all outlier cases. Our simulation of FY 1995 outlier payments based on FY 1993 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MedPAR data indicates that the percentage of outlier cases that would qualify as day outliers is approximately 67
percent. The cases qualifying as day outliers would receive 69 percent of operating outlier payments in FY 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An estimated 33 percent of outlier cases would be cost-only outlier cases, which would receive about 31 percent of
operating outlier payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



