<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
When we modeled the combined operating and capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlier payments, we found that using a common set of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thresholds resulted in a slightly higher percentage of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlier payments for capital-related costs than for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating costs. We estimate the proposed thresholds for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995 would result in outlier payments equal to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5.1 percent of operating DRG payments and 6.3 percent of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital payments based on the Federal rate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Outlier Changes Planned for FY 1996 Through FY 1998 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As stated above, section 1886(d)(5)(A) of the Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requires us to phase out payments for day outliers over the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995 through FY 1997 period. We currently expect to meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the statutory reductions in day outlier payments for FY 1996 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and FY 1997 solely through a reduction in the marginal cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factor without having to raise the day outlier threshold. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If necessary, we will propose achieving the reduction in day 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlier payments between FYs 1996 and 1997 through a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combination of reducing the marginal cost factor and raising 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the threshold. There will be corresponding increases in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments to cost outliers as we phase-out payment to day 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outliers. We welcome comments on this issue. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
E. Rural Referral Centers (&sect;412.96) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under the authority of section 1886(d)(5)(C)(i) of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act, &sect;412.96 sets forth the criteria a hospital must meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in order to receive special treatment under the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system as a rural referral center. For discharges 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occurring before October 1, 1994, rural referral centers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receive the benefit of payment based on the other urban 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment rate rather than the rural payment rate. As of that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
date, the other urban and rural payment rates will be the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
same. However, rural referral centers will continue to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receive special treatment under both the disproportionate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
share hospital payment adjustment and the criteria for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geographic reclassification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One of the criteria under which a rural hospital may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualify as a referral center is to have 275 or more beds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available for use. A rural hospital that does not meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
bed size criterion can qualify as a rural referral center if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the hospital meets two mandatory criteria (number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discharges and case-mix index) and at least one of three 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
optional criteria (medical staff, source of inpatients, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
volume of referrals). With respect to the two mandatory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria, a hospital is classified as a rural referral 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
center if its_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Case-mix index is at least equal to the lower of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
median case-mix index for urban hospitals in its census 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
region, excluding hospitals with approved teaching programs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or the median case-mix index for all urban hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nationally; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Number of discharges is at least 5,000 discharges 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per year or, if fewer, the median number of discharges for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
urban hospitals in the census region in which the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is located. (The number of discharges criterion for an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
osteopathic hospital is at least 3,000 discharges per year.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Case-Mix Index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 412.96(c)(1) provides that HCFA will establish 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
updated national and regional case-mix index values in each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year's annual notice of prospective payment rates for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purposes of determining rural referral center status. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining the proposed national and regional case-mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index values, we would follow the same methodology we used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the November 24, 1986, final rule, as set forth in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations at &sect;412.96(c)(1)(ii). Therefore, the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national case-mix index value includes all urban hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nationwide, and the proposed regional values are the median 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
values of urban hospitals within each census region, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excluding those with approved teaching programs (that is, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those hospitals receiving indirect medical education 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments as provided in &sect;412.105). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These values are based on discharges occurring during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1993 (October 1, 1992, through September 30, 1993), and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include bills posted to HCFA's records through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
December 1993. Therefore, in addition to meeting other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria, we are proposing that to qualify for initial rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
referral center status for cost reporting periods beginning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on or after October 1, 1994, a hospital's case-mix index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
value for FY 1993 would have to be at least_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;1.3028; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Equal to the median case-mix index value for urban 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals (excluding hospitals with approved teaching 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs as identified in &sect;412.105) calculated by HCFA for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the census region in which the hospital is located. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The median case-mix values by region are set forth in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the table below: 
