<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We reiterate that, to qualify for rural referral center 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
status for cost reporting periods beginning on or after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1994, an osteopathic hospital's number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discharges for its cost reporting period that began during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1993 would have to be at least 3,000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Retention of Referral Center Status 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 412.96(f) states that each hospital receiving 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the referral center adjustment is reviewed every 3 years to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determine if the hospital continues to meet the criteria for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
referral center status. To retain status as a referral 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
center, a hospital must meet the criteria for classification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as a referral center specified in &sect;412.96 (b)(1) or (b)(2) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or (c) for 2 of the last 3 years, or for the current year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A hospital may meet any one of the three sets of criteria 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for individual years during the 3-year period or the current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year. For example, a hospital may meet the two mandatory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements in &sect;412.96(c)(1) (case-mix index) and (c)(2) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(number of discharges) and the optional criterion in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paragraph (c)(3) (medical staff) during the first year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
During the second or third year, the hospital may meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria under &sect;412.96(b)(1) (rural location and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate bed size). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A hospital must meet all of the criteria within any one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of these three sections of the regulations in order to meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the retention requirement for a given year. That is, it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will have to meet all of the criteria of &sect;412.96(b)(1) or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;412.96(b)(2) or &sect;412.96(c). For example, if a hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meets the case-mix index standards in &sect;412.96(c)(1) in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years 1 and 3 and the number of discharge standards in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;412.96(c)(2) in years 2 and 3, it will not meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
retention criteria. All of the standards would have to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
met in the same year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The requirement for triennial review was originally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
added to the regulations in 1984, to be effective for cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting periods beginning on or after October 1, 1987 (the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
end of the first 3 years of the referral center adjustment). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, three statutory moratoriums on the performance of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the triennial reviews were enacted by Congress. The third 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of these moratoriums expires at the end of cost reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
periods that began during FY 1994. (See the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
September 1, 1993 final rule (58 FR 46310) for a detailed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
explanation of the moratorium and the implementation of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
triennial reviews.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With the expiration of the current moratorium, we will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
begin reviewing each rural referral center's compliance with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the triennial review criteria effective with cost reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
periods beginning on and after October 1, 1994. As noted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above, although rural referral centers will no longer be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paid a higher standardized amount than other rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals, hospitals approved as rural referral centers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to be entitled to higher disproportionate share 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments and continue to receive preferential consideration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before the Medicare Geographic Classification Review Board. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, we believe it is important that only those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals that meet the rural referral center triennial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review criteria continue to be so classified. Thus, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effective with cost reporting periods beginning on and after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1994, a hospital that has been classified as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
referral center for at least 3 years will be subject to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
triennial review criteria as discussed below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In accordance with &sect;412.96(f)(2), the review process 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is limited to the hospital's compliance during the last 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3 years. Thus, if a hospital meets the criteria in effect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for at least 2 of the last 3 years or if it meets the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria in effect for the current year (that is, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria for FY 1995 outlined above in this section of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preamble), it will retain its status for another 3 years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
No hospital is subject to a review until the end of its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
third full cost reporting period as a referral center. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have constructed the following chart and example to aid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals that qualify as referral centers under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria in &sect;412.96(c) in projecting whether they will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
retain their status as a referral center. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under &sect;412.96(f), to qualify for a 3-year extension 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effective with cost reporting periods beginning in FY 1995, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital must meet the criteria in &sect;412.96(c) for FY 1995 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or it must meet the criteria for 2 of the last 3 years as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
follows: 
