<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Example: A hospital with a cost reporting period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning July 1 qualified as a referral center effective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
July 1, 1992. The hospital has fewer than 275 beds. Its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3-year status as a referral center is protected through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
June 30, 1995 (the end of its cost reporting period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning July 1, 1994). To determine if the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should retain its status as a referral center for an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional 3-year period, we will review its compliance with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the applicable criteria for its cost reporting periods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning July 1, 1992, July 1, 1993, and July 1, 1994. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital must meet the criteria in effect either for its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost reporting period beginning July 1, 1995, or for two out 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the three past periods. For example, to be found to have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
met the criteria at &sect;412.96(c) for its cost reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period beginning July 1, 1993, the hospital's case-mix index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
value during FY 1991 must have equaled or exceeded the lower 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the national or the appropriate regional standard as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
published in the September 1, 1992 final rule. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's total number of discharges during its cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting year beginning July 1, 1991, must have equaled or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeded 5,000 or the regional standard as published in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
September 1, 1992 final rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For those hospitals that seek to retain referral center 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
status by meeting the criteria of &sect;412.96(b)(1)(i) and (ii) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(that is, rural location and at least 275 beds), we will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
look at the number of beds shown for indirect medical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
education purposes (as defined at &sect;412.105(b)) on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's cost report for the appropriate year. We will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consider only full cost reporting periods when determining a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's status under &sect;412.96(b)(1)(ii). This definition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
varies from the number of beds criterion used to determine a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's initial status as a referral center because we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believe it is important for a hospital to demonstrate that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it has maintained at least 275 beds throughout its entire 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost reporting period, not just for a particular portion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
F. Determination of Number of Beds in Determining the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Indirect Medical Education Adjustment (&sect;412.105) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 1886(d)(5)(B) of the Act provides that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment hospitals that have residents in an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approved graduate medical education program receive an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional payment to reflect the higher indirect operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs associated with graduate medical education. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations regarding the calculation of this additional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment, known as the indirect medical education (IME) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment, are at &sect;412.105. The additional payment is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculated by multiplying a hospital's DRG revenue 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(including outlier payments) by the applicable IME 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment factor. The adjustment factor is calculated by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
using a hospital's ratio of residents-to-beds in the formula 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
set forth at section 1886(d)(5)(B)(ii) of the Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 412.105(b) states that the number of beds used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the resident-to-bed ratio is calculated by dividing the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of days during the cost reporting period into ``the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of available bed days during the cost reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period, not including beds assigned to newborns, custodial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
care, and excluded distinct part hospital units *&blank;*&blank;*.'' Since 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we added this language to the regulations in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
September 3, 1985 final rule (50 FR 35679 and 35690), there 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
has been some confusion over whether this definition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes or excludes bed days attributable to neonatal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensive care units. This confusion has resulted in some 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals contesting their fiscal intermediaries' inclusion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of intensive care neonatal intensive care beds in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals' count of available beds. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Prior to the adoption of &sect;412.105(b), the definition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of available beds was at section 2510.5A of the Provider 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reimbursement Manual_Part 1, which was originally used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish bed-size categories for purposes of applying the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost limits under section 1861(v)(1)(A) of the Act. That 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition excluded newborn beds but specifically included 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beds in intensive care units, coronary care units, and other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
special care inpatient hospital units. This exclusion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
newborn beds was consistent with the exclusion of newborn 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
days and costs from the determination of Medicare's share of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowable routine service costs (see section 2202.11B of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual). If a neonatal unit qualifies as an intensive care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unit, however, the costs of that unit are included in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining Medicare's costs (see section 2202.7.II.B of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual). Correspondingly, the days in a neonatal unit that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifies as an intensive care unit are counted as intensive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
care type days rather than nursery days (see section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2202.7.II.A of the Manual). Finally, section 2510.5A of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual clearly indicates that intensive care unit beds and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
special care unit beds are included in a hospital's bed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
complement. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the September 3, 1985 final rule, we added the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition of available beds to the regulations governing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the IME adjustment (then &sect;412.118(b)). The expressed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purpose for the change was to stop counting beds ``based upon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the total number of beds available on the first day of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pertinent cost reporting period'' and to begin counting based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on ``the number of available bed days (excluding beds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assigned to newborns, custodial beds, and beds in excluded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
units) during the current cost reporting period divided by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the number of days in the cost reporting period'' (50 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
35679). We did not change the definition of available beds. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our current position regarding the treatment of these beds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is unchanged from the time when the cost limits established 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under section 1861(v)(1)(A) of the Act were in effect and is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent with the way we treat beds in other hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. That is, if the bed days and costs are allowable in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the calculation of Medicare's share of inpatient costs, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beds within that unit are included as well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Some hospitals have asserted that when we excluded beds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assigned to newborns, we changed our treatment of neonatal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensive care unit beds. However, we note that the term 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``newborn'' has historically been used synonymously with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nursery. Perhaps the clearest illustration of this is the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition of a ``newborn inpatient day'' as a day that ``an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
infant occupies a newborn bed in the nursery'' (see section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2815 of the Provider Reimbursement Manual_Part 2.) This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition dates back at least to 1975. If &sect;412.105(b) was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indeed meant to change our established policy, we would have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
referenced that intent at the time. This would have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary since neonatal intensive care costs and days were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
still included in the calculation of Medicare's costs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, in August 1988, we issued Provider Reimbursement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual Transmittal No. 345, revising section 2405.3G of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual, which provides implementing instructions for the IME 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment. As part of this revision, the definition of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of beds was clarified to specifically exclude beds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``*&blank;*&blank;* assigned to newborns which are not in intensive care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas *&blank;*&blank;*.'' Those arguing that &sect;412.105(b) excludes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neonatal intensive care unit beds from the hospital bed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
count also argue that this Manual issuance is contradictory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to clarify our policy, we are proposing to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revise the regulations at &sect;412.105(b) to exclude 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specifically only beds assigned to newborns in the nursery. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, neonatal intensive care beds would not be excluded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the bed count. We would like to stress that this does 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not represent a policy change in our bed counting rules, but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rather a reaffirmation of our longstanding position. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Revising the wording of the regulations should alleviate any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
future misunderstandings, as well as clarify our position 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regarding previous application of this policy, both before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and since the original publication of our bed counting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations now specified at &sect;412.105(b). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are also proposing to make a technical change to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;412.105(d)(1) to correct a reference. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00081 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
G. Disproportionate Share Adjustment (&sect;412.106) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 1886(d)(5)(F) of the Act provides for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional Medicare payments for hospitals that serve a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disproportionate share of low income patients. Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(d)(5)(F)(vii) of the Act, as added by section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
6003(c)(1)(B) of Public Law 101&hyph;239 and amended by section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4002(b)(1) of Public Law 101&hyph;508, specifies the formula for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining the disproportionate share adjustment percentage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for hospitals that are located in an urban area and have 100 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or more beds, or are located in a rural area and have 500 or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more beds. The statute establishes different payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formulas for different years, including a change in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formula for discharges occurring on or after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Regulations concerning the Medicare disproportionate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
share adjustment are set forth at &sect;412.106. Under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;412.106(c)(1)(i), a hospital that is located in an urban 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area and has 100 or more beds, or is located in a rural area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and has 500 or more beds, must have a ``disproportionate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient percentage'' of at least 15 percent to qualify for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disproportionate share payment adjustment. Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
412.106(d)(2) sets forth the formulas for determining the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disproportionate payment adjustment factors applicable to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these two groups of hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are not proposing changes to any part of &sect;412.106 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in this proposed rule. However, we wish to ensure that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals are aware of the revised payment formulas that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will take effect in FY 1995 pursuant to the statute. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Specifically, for discharges occurring on or after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
October 1, 1994, any such hospital with a disproportionate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
share patient percentage greater than 20.2 percent will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receive a disproportionate share adjustment equal to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5.88 percent plus 82.5 percent of the difference between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
20.2 percent and the hospital's disproportionate share 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient percentage. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
H. Changes Affecting Essential Access Community Hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(EACHs) and Rural Primary Care Hospitals (RPCHs) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(&sect;&sect;412.109, 485.602, 485.635, and 489.102) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
On May 26, 1993, we published a final rule to implement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the EACH program (58 FR 30630). The rule set forth the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements for designating certain hospitals as EACHs or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RPCHs, the conditions that an RPCH must meet to participate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Medicare, and the rules for Medicare payment for services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
furnished by EACHs and RPCHs. The final rule implemented 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 1820 of the Act, as added by sections 6003(g) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
6116(b)(2) of Public Law 101&hyph;239 and revised by section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4008(d) of Public Law 101&hyph;508. The amendments were intended 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to promote regionalization of rural health services in grant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
States, improve access to hospital and other health services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for rural residents, and enhance the provision of emergency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and other transportation services related to health care. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since the publication of that rule, we have identified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
several necessary additions to the EACH/RPCH provisions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
First, to provide for orderly review and adjudication of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disagreements about whether a facility's designation has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been terminated properly, we propose to specify under new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;412.109(e) that a determination by HCFA that a hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
does not meet the criteria for EACH designation, or that a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's EACH designation should be terminated, is subject 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to review under 42 CFR part 405, subpart R. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We also propose several clarifying changes to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition of ``direct services'' at &sect;485.602, as well as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
clarifying references in &sect;485.635, the condition of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participation on provision of services. These revisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
merely would restate more clearly our existing policies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under which the services listed in &sect;485.635(b) (``direct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services'') must be furnished by employed staff, not under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agreements or arrangements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, we are proposing to revise the range of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory services that an RPCH must furnish as direct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services. In a final rule with comment period published on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
December 2, 1993 (58 FR 63533), we revised the RHC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations at &sect;491.9 to eliminate, as required direct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services, tests not classified as waived under the Clinical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Laboratories Improvement Act of 1988 (CLIA). (See 42 CFR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
493.15.) We made these changes in the RHC regulations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because the CLIA program introduced participation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements that may cause some RHCs to withdraw from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program, creating a shortage of available medical care in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because RPCHs typically are located in rural areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
similar to those served by RHCs and serve similar patient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
populations, and because the RPCH designation criteria in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 1820(f)(1)(H) of the Act state that RPCHs must meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the requirement for direct provision of routine diagnostic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services in the statutory definition of ``rural health 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
clinic,'' we believe it is appropriate to impose the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements in both settings. Therefore, we propose to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revise the RPCH regulations at &sect;485.635 to require the RHCs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to provide directly the following laboratory services: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Chemical examination of urine by stick or tablet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method or both (including urine ketones); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Hemoglobin or hematocrit; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Blood glucose; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Examination of stool specimens for occult blood; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(5) Pregnancy tests; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00082 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(6) Primary culturing for transmittal to a certified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
laboratory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We note that this change would not prevent any RPCH 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from providing tests that are not listed in &sect;485.635(b)(2). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An RPCH is free to choose a higher level CLIA certification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than the certificate of waiver if it wishes to provide tests 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of a higher complexity and to comply with all CLIA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The current regulations do not set out a clear and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent position on the applicability of the advance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
directives requirement. Under the regulations in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;485.645(b)(1), swing-bed RPCHs are required to meet a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of skilled nursing facility (SNF) requirements, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including &sect;483.10(b)(4). That regulation requires that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient be given the right to make an advance directive. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(An advance directive is a written instruction, such as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
living will or a durable power of attorney for health care, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that deals with the provision of health care when the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual is incapacitated. See Subpart I of 42 CFR Part 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
489.) Thus, the regulations apply the advance directives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement to RPCH inpatients receiving a SNF level of care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in swing beds, but not to inpatients receiving RPCH care. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe the RPCH benefit could be administered more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
simply and equitably if both hospitals and RPCHs were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to comply with the same advance directives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement. As explained in the preamble to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
May 26, 1993 final rule on EACHs (58 FR 30635&hyph;30636), as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorized by section 1861(e) of the Act, we have read the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
word ``hospital'' to include RPCHs where the context appears 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to support such a reading. We believe the context of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 1866(a)(1)(Q) of the Act would support this reading, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
since many patients are likely to seek RPCH care as an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative to treatment in a hospital or SNF, and many 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RPCHs will accept patients who are at risk of the kind of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incapacitating condition addressed by an advance directive. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, we propose to revise the regulations at &sect;489.102 to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide for more consistent application of the advance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
directive requirement, by specifying that it will apply to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RPCHs on the same basis as to hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe this approach is simpler to administer and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expands beneficiary rights. Moreover, since all prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RPCHs must be (or have been) hospitals and have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to meet the advance directive requirement, there 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should not be any added burden. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
I. Clarification of Payment to RRC/EACH Hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We wish to clarify a response in the EACH final rule of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
May 26, 1993 (58 FR 30639&hyph;30640), regarding the effect on a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rural referral center's (RRC) payment rate should it elect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to become an EACH. An RRC that elects to become an EACH is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered to have dual classification; that is, it is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
classified as an RRC/EACH. Since section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(d)(5)(D)(iii)(III) of the Act specifies that an EACH is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be treated as an sole community hospital (SCH), in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining which of the three payment rates will yield the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
highest aggregate payment, we will use the ``other'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standardized amount in determining the Federal payment rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for discharges occurring on or after October 1, 1994. (As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed above, as of that date, the other urban and rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment rates will be the same.) For discharges occurring 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before October 1, 1994, the Federal payment rate is based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ``other urban'' payment rate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, an RRC that elects to become an EACH is paid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based on whichever of the following rates yields the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
greatest aggregate payment for the cost reporting period: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The national Federal rate applicable to the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(that is, other urban before October 1, 1994 and ``other'' on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or after that date); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The updated hospital-specific rate using FY 1982 cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per discharge; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The updated hospital-specific rate using the FY 1987 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost per discharge. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
J. Direct Graduate Medical Education Payment (&sect;413.86) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Definition of Initial Residency Period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 1886(h)(4) of the Act bases payment for direct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
graduate medical education (GME) costs on the hospital's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of full-time equivalent (FTE) residents multiplied by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital-specific adjusted base year per resident amount. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The number of FTE residents is determined by applying a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
weighting factor to each FTE resident. A resident in an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
initial residency period is weighted as 1.0 FTE. Residents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in approved training programs that are beyond their initial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
residency period are required by law to be counted as .50 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FTE. The initial residency period is defined at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;413.86(g)(1), as the minimum number of years of formal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
training necessary to satisfy the requirements for initial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
board eligibility in the particular specialty plus 1 year, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not to exceed 5 years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 13563(b) of Public Law 103&hyph;66 amended section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(h)(5)(F) of the Act by deleting ``plus one year'' from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the statutory definition of initial residency period, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effective July 1, 1995. To conform the regulations to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statute, we propose to revise &sect;413.86(g)(1) by specifying 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that, effective July 1, 1995, an initial residency period is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
defined as the minimum number of years required for board 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eligibility. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00083 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For example, if a resident completed a 3-year internal medicine program and then started a cardiology program that
requires an additional 2 years of training, the resident would be counted as 1.0 FTE for the 3 years spent in internal
medicine and as a .50 for 2 years in the cardiology program. (Prior to Public Law 103-66, the resident would have been
counted as 1.0 FTE during the first year of the cardiology residency program.) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Part-time Resident 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Currently, the last sentence of &sect;413.86(f)(1)(ii) specifies that, for purposes of GME, ``[a] part-time resident
counts as a partial FTE based on the proportion of time worked as compared to the average time spent by other residents
working in the same specialty program.'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The definition of part-time resident for purposes of GME is consistent with the definition of part-time resident
for purposes of IME. However, the language in the regulations text differs and this difference has led to some confusion.
Therefore, we are proposing to revise the language at &sect;413.86(f)(1)(ii) as follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A part-time resident counts as a partial FTE based on the proportion of time worked in the hospital compared to the total
time necessary to fill a full-time internship or residency slot. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This definition of part-time resident, for the purpose of determining GME full-time equivalent resident counts,
is consistent with the IME definition specified at &sect;412.105(g). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, in determining the FTE count for a part-time resident, for purposes of GME, the proportion of time the resident
works in an approved program in any area of the hospital complex is compared to the total time necessary to fill a full-time
slot. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are also proposing to make a technical change to &sect;412.113(b)(3) to correct a reference to &sect;413.86. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
K. Other Technical Changes (&sect;412.232) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We are republishing provisions in &sect;412.232(c) that were inadvertently deleted from the regulations text published
in the Code of Federal Regulations (CFR) due to an error in amendatory language in our September 1, 1992 final rule.
In order to provide the public with the complete text of the provisions of &sect;412.232(c), pending publication
