<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
V. Proposed Changes and Clarifications to the Prospective Payment System for Capital-Related Costs 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Evaluation of Provisions Relating to Obligated Capital for Hospitals Subject to Lengthy Certificate-of-Need
(CON) Process (&sect;412.302(c)(2)) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 412.302(c)(2) of the regulations specifies the conditions under which capital projects may be treated as
obligated capital for hospitals subject to a lengthy CON process. Under this provision, a capital project that is
subject to a CON process may qualify as obligated capital if several conditions are met. These conditions are: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The hospital is required under State law to obtain prior approval of the capital project by a designated State
or local planning authority in the State in which the hospital is located; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The hospital filed by December 31, 1989, an initial application meeting the requirements of the State that
includes, at a minimum, a detailed description of the project and its estimated cost, and had not received approval
or disapproval by September 30, 1990; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The hospital expended the lesser of $750,000 or 10 percent of the estimated project cost by December 31, 1990;
and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The project is completed and the asset put into use for patient care on or before the earlier of September 30,
1996 or 4 years from the date CON approval is received. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The purpose of allowing hospitals in CON states to qualify projects for recognition as obligated capital under these
special conditions was to recognize that in situations where there is a lengthy CON process, hospitals may have deferred
making the legal commitments required under the general rule for recognizing obligated capital until the CON approval
was received. We believe that such hospitals should not be disadvantaged if they reasonably anticipated the CON approval
process in their capital planning but were unable to meet the December 31, 1990 cut-off date for obligated capital
because timely approval had not been received. Our intent was not to afford more favorable treatment for these hospitals
relative to other hospitals, but rather to make a reasonable and equitable allowance for the impact a lengthy CON process
may have on the ability of hospitals that have already made a substantial financial commitment to meet the criteria
for recognition of obligated capital costs. Thus, the intent was to put these hospitals on an even footing with other
hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since we established this special provision in the August 30, 1991 final rule implementing the prospective payment
system for capital-related costs (56 FR 43393&hyph;43394), we have received comments from individual hospitals
and hospital associations requesting revision to the provision. With one exception, these hospitals come from a
single State and indeed most of them are located in a single large urban area of that State. Citing varied problems and
delays frequently encountered throughout the CON process, such as a requirement for separate CON approval for each
phase of a multi-phase project, the commenters have suggested that the policies be revised as follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Allow later phases of a staged construction project to be deemed part of the originally approved obligation
for purposes of meeting the December 31, 1989 deadline. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Extend the deadline for putting the project into use for patient care by up to 3 years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Allow a 10-year transition for each phase under the hold-harmless methodology. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We stated in our September 1, 1993 final rule that we would conduct an evaluation of the issues raised by these commenters
once we were able to obtain data on the impact of the current provision. We also noted that any reassessment of the current
provisions would be made solely within the policy framework used to establish the initial transition payment policies.
We emphasized that we would not adopt revisions that would give hospitals subject to CON processes more favorable
treatment than other hospitals. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00084 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We now have limited, but significant, data available on the impact of the obligated capital provisions. Since the
beginning of the capital prospective payment system, the Medicare intermediaries have compiled a special data set
to monitor the implementation of the system. These intermediary data have provided us with information concerning
virtually all hospitals that have requested recognition of obligated capital and the amount, if any, of the obligated
capital approved by the intermediary. Using this data, we have been able to develop some relevant comparisons. The
data show, for example, that nationally 42.9 percent (2,281 out of 5,316) of all hospitals requested recognition
of obligated capital. A total of $21.2 billion in obligated capital has been recognized by the intermediaries. The
average amount of obligated capital recognized is $9.3 million per requesting hospital. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since most of the comments concerning the effects of the CON provision have come from a small group of hospitals in one
particular State with a lengthy CON process, we compared the data for that group of hospitals, and the hospitals in
that State as a whole, with the national average and the averages for similar States. We found that the group of hospitals
that has recommended revisions to the current rule had an average approved amount of obligated capital far above the
national average approved amount per requesting hospital. All of the 21 hospitals in the group in question requested
recognition of obligated capital. Furthermore, 17 of those hospitals (81 percent) received at least partial approval
of that request. The average amount of obligated capital approved was $139.5 million. Since 1 of these 21 hospitals
received approval for an extraordinarily large obligated capital project, we also computed the average amount of
obligated capital approved for the other 20 hospitals. Even excluding the hospital with the extraordinarily large
project, the average amount of obligated capital approved in the group in question is $67.5 million. This group of
21 hospitals, in fact, accounted for 13.8 percent of all the obligated capital recognized in the country (6.4 percent
for 20 hospitals, excluding the hospital with the extraordinarily large project). This group of hospitals also has
averages of obligated capital approved that are significantly above the average for the State in which they are located.
In turn, the averages for the State in which they are located are significantly higher than the averages for similar
States (that is, States with large populations, CON processes, and/or large urban areas). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although the fiscal intermediary data base contains data on the amount of obligated capital approved for each hospital,
it does not include data on the amount of obligated capital requested by each hospital. We are therefore unable to develop
ratios of obligated capital approved to obligated capital requested for specific hospitals and groups of hospitals
in order to determine whether that ratio is lower for this group of hospitals than the average for any regional or other
groups of hospitals. However, we believe that the large amounts of obligated capital approved for the hospitals that
have requested revision of the current rules indicate that the current rules do not disadvantage hospitals subject
to lengthy CON processes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, our current data also cannot show how much of the obligated capital tentatively approved by the intermediaries
will eventually meet the deadlines imposed under the general rule for obligated capital and each of the special rules
for putting assets into use for patient care. Under the general rule, the asset must be put in use for patient care no
later than October 1, 1994 (which is 45 months after the latest date by which the asset must have been obligated under
the general rule). In cases of extraordinary circumstances beyond the hospital's control, that deadline can be extended
to no later than September 30, 1996. For hospitals in CON states, the deadline for putting the asset into use for patient
care is the earlier of September 30, 1996 or 4 years from the date of CON approval (&sect;412.302(c)(2)(D)). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, under current regulations, hospitals that receive CON approval after September 30, 1992 may have less than
4 years to complete the project in order to meet the deadline. In the interests of maintaining an equal playing field
for those hospitals, we are proposing to change the deadline for putting assets into use for patient care to the later
of September 30, 1996 or 4 years from the date of CON approval. We believe that establishing any later deadline for hospitals
in CON states would violate the principle of equal treatment by giving those hospitals an advantage over hospitals
that must satisfy the upcoming deadline established in the general rule for obligated capital. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On the basis of the available data, it appears that the existing CON provision has otherwise given this group of hospitals
fair access to the special protection for obligated capital. The amounts of obligated capital approved for the affected
hospitals simply do not support the need for more extensive relief to these hospitals in the context of the obligated
capital provisions. Revision of practically every element of the obligated capital provision would be required
to accommodate all the requests of the affected hospitals. Since it appears the CON rules have provided fair access
to obligated capital protections, we do not believe that it would be appropriate to propose significant changes.
We are therefore proposing no other revisions to the obligated capital rules at this time. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, this does not mean that revisions to other provisions of the capital prospective payment system, such as
the exceptions process, are also unwarranted. The same hospitals that have recommended changes in the obligated
capital rules have also recommended policy measures in connection with exceptions payments under the prospective
payment system for capital-related costs. Specifically, these hospitals have asked that the present 80 percent
minimum payment level for urban hospitals with at least 100 beds and a disproportionate share percentage of at least
20.2 percent be guaranteed through the rest of the transition and extended for at least 10 years after the transition.
While we do not propose to adopt those precise recommendations, we have tried to address the legitimate concerns of
these hospitals in developing the revisions which we are proposing to exceptions payment policy below in section
V.C. of this preamble. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00085 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Specific Adjustment for Taxes to the Capital Prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Payment System Federal Rate (&sect;412.312) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In the August 30, 1991 final rule implementing the capital prospective payment system, we acknowledged the need to
study whether special treatment of the property tax component of capital-related costs is appropriate (56 FR 43364&hyph;43365).
Subsequently, comments received on the May 26, 1993 proposed rule asked that we revisit this issue in a timely manner
to eliminate any inequitable payment distributions. One of the commenters also suggested a specific methodology
to adjust the capital Federal rate using a capital prospective payment system base-year survey to develop a hospital-specific
adjustment to the capital Federal rate payment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Medicare cost report has recently been revised to obtain tax data directly from each hospital for cost reporting
periods beginning on or after October 1, 1991 (FY 1992). These data are just now becoming available to us. Our preliminary
review indicates a need for additional information and analysis. The FY 1992 cost report data currently available
show that with slightly less than 90 percent of all prospective payment system hospitals in the data base (only 60 percent
of which reflect audited data), only 77 percent of the proprietary hospitals reported property tax payments. This
leaves a significant number of proprietary hospitals for which no tax data have been reported. In addition, approximately
13 percent of the non-proprietary hospitals are reporting some form of payments ``in lieu of taxes.'' The nature of
the payments ``in lieu of taxes'' is not yet entirely clear. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given the limitations of the available data, we believe that it would be premature to develop a proposal for a property
tax adjustment. We do not believe that we should proceed with development and implementation of a property tax adjustment
until we have determined the status of the reported payments in lieu of taxes and whether an adjustment would be appropriate
for these expenses. We also need to determine why a significant number of proprietary hospitals have reported no tax
payments and what effect this might have on a budget neutrality adjustment to the Federal rate if a property tax adjustment
were adopted. We have therefore asked our regional offices to develop information on a sample of hospitals from each
group in order to refine and complete our analysis. We invite submission of relevant information and data by interested
parties and the public during the comment period to this proposed rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Furthermore, although we appreciate the concern that this issue addresses, we believe that at this point the possible
adverse effects of our current policy are minimal. Capital costs represent less than 10 percent of total costs and
a proprietary hospital's average tax cost burden for assessed property represents only 7 to 9 percent of its total
capital-related costs. The tax burden for proprietary hospitals thus amounts to less than 1 percent of total costs.
In addition, for the first 5 years of the capital prospective payment system transition period, the majority of payments
to most hospitals are based on each hospital's own cost experience. Therefore, we do not believe delaying a specific
adjustment to proprietary hospitals until more data are available would cause undue hardship to the affected hospitals.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the interim, we hope to advance the discussion of this issue by raising the issue of whether a property tax adjustment
would be appropriate and by presenting one approach to such an adjustment. We welcome comments and recommendations
on every aspect of this presentation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One approach to a methodology for a property tax adjustment for proprietary hospitals is to develop a property tax
factor (PTF) from data on hospital-specific tax payments in the most recently audited cost reporting year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00086 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
The PTF would take the form of a percentage add-on to the Federal rate and would remain fixed for future periods. That
is, unless a hospital had property tax costs in its most recently audited cost reporting year, it would not qualify
for an adjustment. The PTF would be based on capital cost and property tax information provided on the most current
audited cost report, as follows: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) Audited cost report property tax data;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Total capital cost for that year;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Property tax unit cost multiplier (Line 1 divided by Line 2);
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Medicare inpatient capital cost;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(5) Medicare inpatient property taxes (Line 3 times Line 4);
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(6) Medicare discharges;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(7) Medicare inpatient property taxes per discharge (Line 5 divided by Line 6);
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(8) Federal capital rate (for fiscal year with most recent audited data);
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(9) Property tax factor (Line 7 divided by Line 8).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This approach would be consistent with the philosophy of a prospective payment system. Although the initial determination
of the PTF reflects a hospital's actual tax burden in a specific year, the PTF divorces payment from subsequent changes
in the tax costs a hospital may experience. Because the PTF would be determined on the basis of tax costs from an audited
cost reporting year and computed as a constant ratio of the Federal rate, States could not use property tax changes
to capriciously increase Medicare reimbursement. Under this plan, Medicare's payment for taxes would increase
with the underlying rate of increase in the Federal rate. One objection to the PTF is that over time the ratio of actual
Medicare allowable property taxes to Medicare inpatient capital costs in a rate year could diverge from the ratio
in the year used to set the PTF. Such a divergence would create windfall gains or losses for particular hospitals, but
we know of no way to determine the magnitude of possible gains and losses to particular hospitals. The greater the magnitude
of such gains and losses, the less appropriate the PTF methodology would be. We welcome information on this issue and
recommendations about how to determine the magnitude of such gains and losses, while safeguarding the program from
arbitrary increases in hospital taxes. A related problem is that applying a constant ratio to the Federal rate has
the effect of increasing (or decreasing) the amount of the adjustment for property taxes across all hospitals by the
same factor as the increase (or decrease) to the national rate. We currently have no data that show conclusively whether
property taxes generally increase at the same rate as capital costs as a whole. This is difficult to determine since
property tax assessment systems vary enormously from one locality to another, and changes in property taxes can reflect
complicated changes in tax rates, methods of property assessment, and the tax base (that is, the percent of property
value subject to tax assessment) in different localities, as well as appreciation of existing property values. We
know of no definitive sources of information on this matter. We encourage submission of relevant information, observations,
and suggestions. An alternative methodology could employ a hospital-specific add-on amount, as opposed to the hospital-specific
ratio discussed above. Such an add-on could be determined on the basis of the base year tax costs per discharge attributable
to Medicare. This alternative may be preferable if strong evidence emerges that property taxes do not tend to increase
at the same rate as other capital costs. However, in that case, a tax increase index would need to be developed. We welcome
comments on the merits of this alternative. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00087 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We are especially interested in comments regarding an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment for non-proprietary hospitals that make payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in lieu of taxes and on a methodology for determining such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an adjustment. As we previously noted, 13 percent of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
non-proprietary hospitals report payments in lieu of taxes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The uncertainty about the exact nature of those payments is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one reason why we have decided to delay implementation of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific property tax adjustment for those hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, our initial limited investigation of this issue 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggests that those payments do not reflect assessments that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
can be categorized as capital-related costs under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;413.130(a)(3). It appears that some non-proprietary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals may have reflected payments made for assessments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that are not based on the valuation of the hospital's real 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or personal property. Thus, the available evidence does not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
support adoption of an adjustment for non-proprietary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals' payments in lieu of taxes. However, we will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to study this matter. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Any property tax adjustment would have to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implemented in a budget neutral fashion, so that total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated payments under the prospective payment system for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital-related costs with such an adjustment would equal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total estimated payments in the absence of a property tax 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment. A property tax adjustment applied to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal rate thus requires a corresponding reduction to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
base Federal rate to assure budget neutrality. It is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impossible to determine the precise magnitude of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required adjustment to the Federal rate until we determine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
both the status of those proprietary hospitals that are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently reporting taxes and the nature of the payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reported by non-proprietary hospitals in lieu of taxes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, since our preliminary data indicates that tax 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments by proprietary hospitals are at least 0.77 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of total capital costs, we believe that a reduction to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
base capital rate of at least 0.77 percent would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary. The final amount of the necessary adjustment to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Federal rate would depend upon the ultimate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination concerning the proprietary hospitals not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting tax payments and the nature of the payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reported by non-proprietary hospitals. We welcome comments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the equity of removing tax amounts from the base Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate and distributing them only to tax-paying hospitals in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the form of an adjustment to the Federal rate payment. Our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concern is that special payment adjustments recognize only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs that are distinguishable and unique. We also wish to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
avoid granting unwarranted advantages to any particular 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
class of hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We plan to conduct further analysis on this issue prior 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the publication of the final rule. We will also analyze 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comments and recommendations submitted in response to this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussion. We will make a determination concerning whether 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to adopt a property tax adjustment, and the form that any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such adjustment would take, when we have obtained sufficient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relevant data and have resolved all significant problems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inherent to any adjustment methodology. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Revision of Provision Relating to Exceptions Payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(&sect;412.348) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 412.348 provides that, during the transition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period, a hospital may receive additional payment under an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceptions process when its regular payments are less than a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimum percentage, established by class of hospital, of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's reasonable capital-related costs. The amount of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the exceptions payment is the difference between the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's minimum payment level and the payments the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital would receive under the capital prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system in the absence of an exceptions payment. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comparison is made on a cumulative basis for all cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting periods during which the hospital is subject to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the capital prospective payment transition rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under current &sect;412.348(b)(1), the minimum payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percentages by class of hospitals for FY 1995 are: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00088 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;For sole community hospitals, 90 percent; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;For urban hospitals with at least 100 beds that have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a disproportionate share patient percentage of at least 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
20.2 percent or that received more than 30 percent of its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
net inpatient care revenues from State or local governments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for indigent care, 80 percent; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;For all other hospitals, 70 percent of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's reasonable inpatient capital-related costs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The current regulations also provide that total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated payments under the exceptions process in a year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may not exceed 10 percent of total estimated capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payments (exclusive of hold-harmless payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for old capital) for the same fiscal year. As we stated in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the August 30, 1991 final rule, we will revise the minimum 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment percentages during any subsequent transition year, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
if necessary, to ensure that exceptions payments do not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceed the 10 percent limit. It has not yet been necessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to reduce the original minimum payment percentages to meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 10 percent limit. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the final rule for the prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for capital-related costs, we stated that we would carefully 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
monitor the impact of the capital prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in order to determine whether ``some type of permanent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceptions process is necessary and the circumstances under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which additional payments would be made (56 FR 43409).'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since the publication of the final rule for the capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system, several commenters have urged us 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to provide special protection for hospitals that are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
undertaking major renovation or replacement of aging 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities during the decade of the transition. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commenters have pointed out that, unlike hospitals that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
undertook major renovation or replacement during the 1980s, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
many hospitals that undertake capital replacement or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
renovation projects during the transition do not qualify for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the protection for old capital or obligated capital during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the transition. In addition, unlike hospitals that will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
undertake major capital replacement or renovation in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decade after the transition period, such hospitals have not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
had an opportunity to accrue capital prospective payments to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fund the projects. We are aware of a number of major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projects, scheduled for completion later in the transition, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that could therefore not be included as old or obligated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital, and that involve major replacement or renovation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aging facilities. In the design of the capital prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system, we have made every effort to consider the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
circumstances of hospitals for whom the transition to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment poses special difficulties. We agree 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the commenters that hospitals that need to undertake 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major renovation or replacement projects during the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transition face special difficulties merely because of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
timing of their projects. We do not believe that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare program should guarantee to underwrite every major 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program of replacement or renovation of capital assets. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, under certain circumstances we believe that it is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fair to provide special protection, through the exceptions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process, for hospitals that find themselves in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
circumstances cited. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition to the exceptions categories identified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above, we are therefore proposing at &sect;412.348 to provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
special protection for some hospitals that are undertaking 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major projects to renovate or replace aging plant during the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transition period. This special protection, which will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide a 70 percent minimum payment level for up to ten 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years beyond the transition period, will be available only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the following classes of hospitals: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Sole community hospitals that have a current ratio 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
less than or equal to of 1.75 in the exception year; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Urban hospitals with at least 100 beds that either 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have disproportionate share percentages of at least 20.2 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent or receive at least 30 percent of their revenue from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State or local funds for indigent care, and that have a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current ratio less than or equal to 1.75 in the exception 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Hospitals with a combined inpatient Medicare and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicaid utilization of at least 70 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, the hospital must meet an age of asset test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and, if applicable, receive approval for the project from a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State or local planning authority. If planning approval is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not required, an urban hospital must demonstrate either that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it is in a metropolitan statistical area (MSA) that does not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have an overall average occupancy rate less than 80 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or that its capacity is no more than 80 percent of its prior 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capacity (in terms of bed size). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe that this exception should apply only to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals that have previously been identified for special 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
protection under the Medicare program (that is, sole 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
community hospitals or large urban hospitals that serve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disproportionate shares of indigent patients), or that have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heavy utilization by beneficiaries of the Federal health 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs. The average hospital utilization by Medicaid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beneficiaries is approximately 10 percent. Hospitals with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
60 percent Medicare utilization can reasonably claim high 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare utilization rates. We have therefore determined 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that, aside from certain sole community hospitals and large 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
urban disproportionate share hospitals, this special 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exception should be restricted to hospitals with combined 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare and Medicaid utilization of at least 70 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are also requiring that sole community hospitals and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
large urban disproportionate share hospitals must have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current ratios of less than or equal to 1.75 in order to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eligible for this special exception. We do not believe that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals that do not have high Medicare and Medicaid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
utilization should be eligible for this special exception 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unless they are financially vulnerable. The current ratio 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is perhaps the most widely used measure of hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liquidity. It is defined as the number of dollars held in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current assets per dollar of current liabilities. Thus, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
higher values reflect a stronger short-term financial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
condition. A current ratio of 2.0 or better is a common 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital target for financial performance. We therefore 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believe that a current ratio of less than or equal to 1.75 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is a sufficient indication of financial stress to qualify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for special protection under the exceptions policy. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current ratio can be determined from information available 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the Medicare cost report (HCFA 2552&hyph;92, Worksheet G, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Lines 11 and 43). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00089 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
One concern that we have about the use of the current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ratio as a measure is that it may be possible for hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to manipulate cost report statistics to meet a current ratio 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
threshold. Some analysis suggests that the current ratio 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be supplemented by measures such as days in accounts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receivable and/or days in accounts payable in order to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduce the prospects for manipulation. We will continue to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
study this issue before publication of a final rule on this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
special exceptions provision. We welcome comments on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use of the current ratio as a measure of hospital financial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vulnerability, on the appropriate threshold under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measure, and on whether the current ratio should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supplemented by other measures. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An eligible hospital must pass an age of asset test in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to qualify for an exception. Hospitals almost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
universally have plant and equipment of varying ages. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average age of a hospital's assets is commonly determined as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ratio of accumulated depreciation to current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation expense. The current version of the Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost report (HCFA 2552&hyph;92) allows for determination of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average age of assets for all hospital assets, for building 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and fixed equipment alone, or for moveable equipment alone. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The average age of buildings and fixed equipment can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determined on the basis of information on the current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare cost report (HCFA 2552&hyph;92, Worksheet G, Lines 14, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
14.01, 16, 16.01, 18, 18.01, 20, and 20.01, and Worksheet A&hyph;7, Part III, Column 9, Lines 1 and 3). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We propose a two-part age of asset test to identify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aging hospitals that undertake needed major capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replacement or renovation projects during the transition. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The test would only consider changes in the average age of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
building and fixed equipment (determined by the ratio of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accumulated depreciation for building and fixed equipment to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current depreciation for building and fixed equipment) in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to focus on major capital replacement and renovation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projects. We believe that it is appropriate to grant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceptions only to aging hospitals that undertake and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
complete major capital projects during the capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment transition period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The first part of the age-of-asset test is designed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determine whether a hospital had relatively aged fixed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assets at the beginning of the capital transition period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(that is, the hospital's first cost reporting period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning on or after October 1, 1991). We would determine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital's average age of building and fixed equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
using cost report data for the first year of the transition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in comparison to the national average age of buildings and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fixed equipment for that year. The threshold for qualifying 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under this part of the test would be the 75th percentile. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We do not believe that the special protection we are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing under this provision should be extended to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals who cannot demonstrate an urgent need for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replacement or renovation of facilities. We believe that an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average age of assets at or above the 75th percentile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nationally is a sufficient indicator that a hospital had a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pressing need for renovation or replacement at the beginning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the capital transition period to serve as a threshold 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifier for a special exception. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We would determine whether a hospital is at or above 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 75th percentile nationally on the basis of Medicare cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report data for FY 1992. That data is becoming available 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this year. Preliminary but incomplete data suggest that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average age of buildings and fixed equipment at the 75th 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percentile is approximately 16.4 years. We intend to make a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final determination on the basis of more complete data that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will become available at a later date. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The second part of the test would compare the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
age of buildings and fixed equipment in the cost reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period beginning in FY 1992 with the average age of fixed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assets in the year the hospital reports the completion of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major capital replacement or renovation. A major renovation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or replacement of hospital capital during the transition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period should result in a dramatically reduced average age 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of buildings and fixed equipment compared to the first year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the transition. We are therefore proposing that the test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be whether a hospital's average age of buildings and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fixed equipment has declined by at least 50 percent since 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the first year of payment under the capital prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system. Thus, a hospital whose average age of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
buildings and fixed equipment is at or above the 75th 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percentile nationally in the first year of capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment, and whose average age of buildings and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fixed equipment has declined by at least 50 percent in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designated year later in the transition, meets the age-of-assets test. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We welcome comment on the use of an average age of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
asset measure, on the age-of-assets test as we have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conceived it, and on the appropriate threshold levels to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
apply under such a test. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00090 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We do not believe that this special exception for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replacement and renovation of old facilities should apply to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals whose capital projects preserve the overcapacity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that is currently evident in much of the hospital industry. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, a hospital cannot qualify for an exception unless 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it has received any required approval from a State or local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
planning authority. If such approval is not required, an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
urban hospital must demonstrate either that (1) overall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average occupancy in its MSA is at least 80 percent, or (2) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its capacity is no more than 80 percent of its prior 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capacity (in terms of bed size). We are exempting rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals from this requirement because of concern about 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintaining access to needed services in rural areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Hospitals that qualify for exceptions under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
special provision may continue to receive exceptions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments for up to 10 years beyond the transition. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Exceptions payments will be available to hospitals that meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the special criteria above for 10 years after the date when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the hospital meets the age of asset test, provided that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital meets the age of asset test no later than the end 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the capital prospective payment system transition period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the hospital (that is, the last day of the hospital's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
last cost reporting period beginning before October 1, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2001). We believe that extending exceptions payments for up 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to 10 years after the transition should be sufficient for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals who must undertake major renovation or replacement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of facilities during the transition period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We would continue to determine the amount of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceptions payments under this special provision on a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cumulative basis. That is, we would determine the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's exception payment by comparing the cumulative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments made to the hospital under the capital prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments system to the cumulative minimum payment levels 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable to the hospital for each cost reporting period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subject to the prospective payment system. Any amount by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which the hospital's cumulative payments exceed its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cumulative minimum payment levels would continue to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deducted from the additional payment that would otherwise be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payable for the cost reporting period. In addition, we will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
apply another test designed to limit the amount of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceptions payments under this special provision. We would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduce the amount of an exception payment that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise be made for any cost reporting period under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
special provision by the amount of any Medicare hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inpatient prospective payment system payments for the cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting period (less 75 percent of operating prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system disproportionate share payments if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable) that are in excess of Medicare inpatient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating and capital costs. This calculation includes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments under both the capital and operating prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment systems. It does not include graduate medical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
education payments, outpatient service payments, or payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for any other services covered under Medicare Part B. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
propose to exclude 75 percent of operating prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system disproportionate share payments from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination of the Medicare operating prospective payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system payments in order to account for the financial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pressure that disproportionate share hospitals experience in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other parts of their operations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, we emphasize that these proposed policies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be subject to reevaluation in the light of passage of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comprehensive health care reform by Congress. The hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for whom we currently propose to provide special protections 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may no longer require such protections after the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implementation of comprehensive health care financing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reform. Therefore, we will carefully monitor the impact of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final health care reform legislation on the hospitals that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would receive special treatment under the policies proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above to determine whether such legislation justifies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revision of those policies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are proposing the following revisions to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations text to implement the changes discussed above: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Under new &sect;412.348(a), we would define current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ratio, fixed assets, and average age of fixed assets. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The new special exceptions requirements are set 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forth at proposed &sect;412.348(g). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Current paragraphs (a) through (e) have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
redesignated as paragraphs (b) through (f), and current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paragraph (f) has been redesignated as paragraph (h). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;We are also proposing to make conforming technical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes to &sect;412.348 to correct references. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00091 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
D. Extraordinary Circumstances Exceptions Payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(&sect;412.348(e)) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Currently, &sect;412.348(e) provides that a hospital may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
request an additional payment during the capital transition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period if the hospital incurs an unanticipated capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expenditure in excess of $5 million (net of insurance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proceeds) due to extraordinary circumstances beyond the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's control. Extraordinary circumstances include but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are not limited to a flood, fire, or earthquake. A hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must apply to the HCFA regional office, for a determination 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the HCFA Administrator, within 180 days of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extraordinary circumstance that caused the unexpected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expenditures in order to qualify for exceptions payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We propose to revise &sect;412.348 to clarify our policy and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reconcile conflicts between the regulation text and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
language and intent of the preamble to the August 30, 1991 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final rule that implemented prospective payment system for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital-related costs (56 FR 43411). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to current &sect;412.348(e)(1), we intend to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
clarify that the minimum expenditure criteria that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital must meet in order to qualify for payments is the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
net amount of proceeds expected to be recovered by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital in connection with the event from any other source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including, but not limited to, insurance, litigation, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other government relief funding. In the August 30, 1991 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final rule, in response to a comment concerning the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceptions process, we indicated that we intended to provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for an additional payment when a hospital has extraordinary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
circumstances that result in unanticipated capital spending 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of at least $5 million. We then stated that in determining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether this threshold was met, we would take into account 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any insurance proceeds received by the hospital in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
connection with the extraordinary circumstances. However, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we inadvertently failed to list other sources that may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduce the unanticipated capital-related expense to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital. Later in that response we noted that HCFA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decision will be contingent on a final determination of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated costs for the unanticipated circumstance and we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provided an example of how the extraordinary circumstance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exception policy would be applied. In that example we noted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the hospital must provide evidence of the extent to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which the loss will be covered by insurance proceeds or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other sources. In the regulations text, we reflected our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intention to establish the threshold net of insurance but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
did not include the other comparable sources, such as court 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
awards for damages, or other governmental relief funds. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The purpose of establishing this exception was to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
protect hospitals during the transition period from out-of-pocket expenditures at a level that could put severe

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
financial strain on them. Thus, we clearly did not intend 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to replace or supplement other payment sources that have an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation to pay or provide special authorization for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funding in these same circumstances. As a result, we intend 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to revise current &sect;412.348(e)(1) to conform with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
example provided in the August 30, 1991 final rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concerning application of the extraordinary circumstances 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exception policy and, specifically, documentation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evidence of the extent of net loss. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We also propose to correct the provision in current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;412.348(e)(2) regarding the level at which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination of a hospital's eligibility for an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extraordinary circumstance exception payment is made. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although the determination authority was reserved to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
HCFA Administrator, we believe that the appropriate official 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to make this decision is the Director of the Office of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Payment Policy, Bureau of Policy Development, Health Care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Financing Administration. This change is warranted because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it is inappropriate that the HCFA Administrator make a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decision that he or she may be called on to review if a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital requests an administrative appeal of an adverse 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decision on its exceptions application. As the director of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the HCFA component that determines policy on hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment in general and exceptions payment policy in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particular, the Director of the Office of Payment Policy is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ideally suited to make the determination on an exceptions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
request. The determination is subject to the appeal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures of subpart R of 42 CFR part 405 when the provider 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
receives the NPR for the cost reporting period for which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provider is seeking additional payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
E. Funding of Depreciation (&sect;&sect;413.134 and 413.153) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 1861(v)(1)(A) of the Act gives the Secretary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
broad latitude to prescribe regulations concerning Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments to providers on a reasonable cost basis. Under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this authority, &sect;413.134(e) provides that, although we do 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not require the funding of depreciation, we strongly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommend it as a means of conserving funds for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replacement of depreciable assets. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Offset of Investment Income 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
To encourage the funding of depreciation, we have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified at &sect;&sect;413.134(e)(1) and 413.153(b)(2)(iii) that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
investment income earned on funded depreciation will not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used to reduce allowable interest expense. Further, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;413.134(e) stipulates that additions to the funded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation account must remain in the account for at least 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
6 months to be considered valid funding transactions and be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eligible for the benefits of a funded depreciation account. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00092 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In response to a comment in the August 30, 1991 final rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the prospective payment system for inpatient hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital-related costs (56 FR 43424), we stated that interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on any funds that do not qualify as funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
account funds, which would include funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deposits of less than 6 months duration, must be used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offset interest expense. Section 226.3 of the Provider 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reimbursement Manual (HCFA Pub. 15&hyph;1) provides that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
investment income earned prior to the elapse of the 6-month 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period will not be offset unless the deposits are actually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
withdrawn during this period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are proposing to revise &sect;413.134(e)(1) to state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specifically that investment income earned during the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
6-month period will not be used to offset interest expense 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unless the funds are withdrawn for an improper purpose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during this period. Under this change, if a provider 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
withdraws funds from the funded depreciation account before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the expiration of the 6-month time limit, and the withdrawal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is made for an allowable purpose as described in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;413.134(e)(3)(i)(A), the investment income earned on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funds is not offset against allowable interest expense. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Loans to the General Fund 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We propose to revise and clarify our policy regarding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
loans from the provider's funded depreciation fund to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provider's general fund. Sections 413.153(b)(3)(ii) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c)(2) permit a provider to lend its funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funds to its general fund. Moreover, these sections provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that interest expense incurred on such loans are considered 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowable costs. Section 226.1 of the Manual amplifies this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
policy by explaining that, in order for the interest expense 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incurred on such loans to be allowable, the interest expense 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must meet the ``necessary and proper'' tests (set forth in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations at &sect;413.153(b) (2) and (3), and in the Manual at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sections 202.2 and 202.3). This section of the Manual also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
explains that the funds loaned to the general fund must not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be used to acquire depreciable assets used to furnish 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient care because the provider is expected to use its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funded depreciation, rather than to lend it, for that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purpose. Finally, this section of the Manual explains that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deposits to the funded depreciation fund will not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recognized to the extent that loans of funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funds to the general fund are outstanding. Rather, such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deposits will be considered repayments of the loan to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general fund. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Three other sections of the Manual address loans from a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provider's funded depreciation fund to the provider's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general fund. Section 226.C of the Manual, in discussing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the availability of funded depreciation funds, provides that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
loans made from funded depreciation do not alter the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement that funded depreciation must be available for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the acquisition of the provider's depreciable assets used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
furnish patient care, or for other capital purposes related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to patient care. Section 226.3 of the Manual, in discussing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the application of the 6-month rule for a deposit to become 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a valid funding transaction, provides that a loan of funded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation funds to the general fund is considered a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
withdrawal for purposes of applying the 6-month rule. That 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is, if the provider borrows from funded depreciation for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
working capital purpose, and some or all of the funds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
borrowed were not on deposit in the funded depreciation fund 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for at least 6 months, the borrowing would be considered a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
withdrawal from the funded depreciation account. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addition, if a deposit did not satisfy the 6-month rule any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
investment income earned on the deposit would have to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used to offset otherwise allowable interest expense. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 226.4B of the Manual, in an example of applying of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the last-in, first-out basis to withdrawals from funded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation for an improper purpose, also characterizes a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
loan to the general fund from the funded depreciation fund 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as a withdrawal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In recent years, we have received a number of inquiries 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggesting that loans from the provider's funded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation fund to the provider's general fund are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
characterized more appropriately as investments of funded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation, rather than withdrawals. We agree with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inquirers. The term ``withdrawal'' implies that the funds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have been removed from the funded depreciation account and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used, either for a proper purpose (for example, acquisition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of a depreciable asset) or an improper purpose (for example, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to purchase office supplies). In contrast, the term 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``investment'' implies that the funds have not actually been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
removed from the funded depreciation account and used; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rather, they have been temporarily converted to one or more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
income producing vehicles (for example, the purchase of U.S. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Treasury bills). Because loans from the funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funds to the general fund generate investment income for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funded depreciation fund, we believe that such loans more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
properly should be considered as investments, rather than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
withdrawals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In conjunction with this change, we had to decide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether such loans would have to meet the ``readily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
marketable'' test for allowable funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
investments expressed in &sect;413.134(e) and section 226 of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual. We believe that, because we require that loans made 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from funded depreciation do not alter the requirement that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the funded depreciation be available for the acquisition of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciable assets used to furnish patient care, or for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other capital purposes related to patient care, application 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the ``readily marketable'' test would be redundant. That 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is, if a provider loans funded depreciation to the general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fund, and while that loan is outstanding the provider needs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funds to acquire a depreciable asset related to patient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
care, the provider may not borrow funds to acquire the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciable asset. Rather, the provider must recall the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funds from the general fund loan to use to acquire the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
asset. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accordingly, we propose to add paragraph (e)(4) to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;413.134 to address loans from the funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
account. New &sect;413.134(e)(4) would both consolidate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
policies now set forth in the manual sections cited above 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and revise our policy regarding the characterization of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
loans from the funded depreciation account. Such loans 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be considered investments, rather than withdrawals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00093 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Spenddown of ``Tainted'' Funded Depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The preamble to the August 30, 1991 final rule also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addressed the issue of spenddown (56 FR 43421). ``Spenddown'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is a process whereby we permit providers to ``cure'' borrowing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that was found to be unnecessary because of available funded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation by using those funded depreciation funds for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proper purpose. Under spenddown, if additional deposits are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
made to funded depreciation after the unnecessary borrowing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination, withdrawals from the funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subsequent to a determination of unnecessary borrowing are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
made on a last-in, first-out basis (typically, withdrawals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for a proper purpose are made on a first-in, first-out 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basis). The result of this policy is that all additional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deposits to funded depreciation must be used before spending 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
can be allotted to the ``tainted'' funds, that is, the portion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the funded depreciation that resulted in the unnecessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
borrowing. In addition, Medicare's policy has been that any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other funded depreciation funds in the account at the time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the unnecessary borrowing must be used before the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``tainted'' funds can be spent down or cured. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since the publication of the August 30, 1991 final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule, we have received several inquiries asking why our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
policy on spenddown was not incorporated into the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. These inquiries also have pointed out that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
last-in, first-out method set forth at &sect;413.134(e)(3)(i)(C) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will not work properly when the unnecessary borrowing is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repaid from the funded depreciation account and that account 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contained untainted funded depreciation at the time of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unnecessary borrowing or received additional deposits after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the borrowing. The inquirers suggested that this situation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may be rectified by establishing an exception to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
last-in, first-out rule when the unnecessary borrowing that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
caused the ``tainting'' is repaid out of funded depreciation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In this case, any use of funded depreciation to repay the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unnecessary borrowing will be deemed to be from ``tainted'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funded depreciation. We agree and propose to provide for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such an exception in &sect;413.134(e)(2)(iv). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Similarly, where the provider repays part or all of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
principal of the unnecessary borrowing that caused the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``tainted'' funded depreciation by using general funds, a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
corresponding amount of the ``tainted'' funded depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must be cured. This is necessary so that the ``tainted'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portion of the funded depreciation does not exceed the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amount of the balance of the unnecessary borrowing that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
caused the ``tainting'' in the first place. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are proposing to revise &sect;413.134(e) to incorporate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the curing process of ``tainted'' funded depreciation and also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to provide that the provider's subsequent repayment of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
principal portion of the unnecessary borrowing will be from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ``tainted'' funds in the funded depreciation. Thus, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neither the first-in, first-out nor the last-in, first-out 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basis would be applicable for such repayments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Necessary and Unnecessary Borrowing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In February 1977, HCFA published &sect;203 of the Provider 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reimbursement Manual to address interest on loans in excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of asset value acquired after 1970. Section 203.D of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual states that ``[r]epayments of the funds borrowed are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applied first to reducing the portion of the loan applied to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the allowable cost of the patient care assets, then to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tangible and intangible assets not considered reasonably 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
related to patient care, and lastly to goodwill.'' We have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applied this long-standing principle to cases where a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provider repays borrowed funds where some of these funds are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deemed to be unnecessary. In these circumstances, we have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
held that all of necessary borrowing must be repaid before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any of the repayments may be applied to the unnecessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
borrowing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We also apply this principle to unnecessary borrowing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that causes ``tainted'' funded depreciation. When a portion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of a provider's borrowing is considered unnecessary because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the availability of funded depreciation, subsequent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repayments, whether from funded depreciation or general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
funds, are first applied to the allowable portion of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
borrowing and, when all of the allowable borrowing is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repaid, to the unallowable portion of the loan. This places 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the provider in the same position as if the available funded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation had been used without any unnecessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
borrowing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00094 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
These applications have not been clearly set forth in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the regulations. Therefore, we propose to revise 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;&sect;413.134(e) and 413.153(d) to clearly address both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
situations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We are also proposing to make a technical change to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&sect;413.134(d)(1) to correct a reference. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
VI. Proposed Changes for Hospitals Excluded From the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Prospective Payment Systems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. New Requirements for Certain Long-Term Care Hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Excluded From the Prospective Payment Systems (&sect;412.23) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 1861(e) of the Act and the regulations at 42 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CFR part 482 define a hospital as an institution that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifies to participate in Medicare because it meets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certain health and safety standards, known as ``conditions of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participation'' (COPs). A hospital must be surveyed for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance with the COPs by State agencies that work under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agreement with HCFA unless, under section 1865 of the Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and regulations at 42 CFR part 488, the hospital is presumed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to meet the COPs on the basis of accreditation by the Joint 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Commission on Accreditation of Healthcare Organizations or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the American Osteopathic Association. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Recently, some entities have discovered that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process for becoming a provider under the COPs can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manipulated to permit them to receive exclusion from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment systems. Specifically, hospitals have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
begun to organize themselves under what they themselves 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refer to as the ``hospital within a hospital'' model. Under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this model, an entity may operate in space leased from a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital, and have most or all services furnished under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
arrangements by employees of the lessor hospital. The newly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organized entity may be operated by a corporation formed and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controlled by the lessor hospital, or by a third entity that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controls both. In either case, the new entity seeks State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
licensure and Medicare participation as a hospital, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrates that it has an average length of stay of over 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
25 days, and obtains an exclusion from the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment systems. The effect of this process is to extend 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the long-term care hospital exclusion to what is for all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practical purposes a long-term care hospital unit. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe it may not be appropriate for a long-term 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
care ``hospital within a hospital'' to receive an exclusion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the prospective payment systems. The considerations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
underlying exclusions from the prospective payment systems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may not apply to these entities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under the prospective payment systems, hospitals are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paid for inpatient services on the basis of prospectively 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determined rates. Under section 1886(d)(1)(B) of the Act, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certain hospitals and units are excluded from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment systems including, psychiatric, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rehabilitation, children's, and long-term care hospitals, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and psychiatric and rehabilitation units. These hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and units are excluded because they could not be paid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriately under the prospective payment systems. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statute does not exclude ``long-term care units'' of general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The prospective payment systems are based on an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
averaging concept which recognizes that some patients will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stay longer and consume more resources than expected, while 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
others will have shorter, less costly stays. We expect that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an efficiently operated hospital will be able to deliver 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality care to Medicare patients at an aggregate cost no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
greater than its aggregate Medicare payments under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment systems for each cost reporting period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The DRG system does not, however, apply well to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
long-stay hospitals. Those hospitals have few short-stay or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
low-cost cases, and might be systematically underpaid if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment method applied. Thus, exclusion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
entire long-term care hospitals from the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
systems is appropriate. However, if an entity that provides 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
long-term care is part of a larger hospital, the reasons for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exclusion may not apply. A long-term care ``hospital within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital'' is essentially a long-term care hospital unit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which accounts for only a part of the larger hospital's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patient load. The principles underlying the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment systems do apply to the larger hospital, unlike 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
entire long-term care hospitals. Exclusion of long-term 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
care units could inadvertently encourage hospitals to try to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
abuse the prospective payment systems, by diverting all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
long-stay cases to the excluded unit, leaving only the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shorter, less costly cases to be paid for under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment systems. In such cases, hospitals would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
profit inappropriately from prospective payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For these reasons, we believe it may be inappropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to grant an exclusion to a long-term care ``hospital within a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital.'' Moreover, exclusion of long-term care ``units'' is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inconsistent with the statutory scheme. Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(d)(1)(B) of the Act clearly provides for exclusions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the prospective payment systems for long-term care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals, and also for psychiatric units and rehabilitation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
units, but the statute does not provide for exclusion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
long-term care units. Also, we note that section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(d)(1)(B) of the Act begins with the phrase ``as used in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this section'' in referring to hospitals; we believe this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
language suggests that the definition of ``hospital'' for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment purposes may be different from other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definitions. Furthermore, section 1886(d)(1)(B) of the Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provides that excluded from the definition of prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment systems hospital is a psychiatric or rehabilitation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unit of a hospital that is a distinct part of the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``as defined by the Secretary.'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe these distinctions were intended to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meaningful, and that it would undermine the distinctions if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we allowed exclusion of long-term care units. Because we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believe such exclusions are contrary to the purpose and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scheme of section 1886(d)(1)(B) of the Act, we are proposing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to revise the regulations to prevent inappropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exclusions. Section 1871 of the Act provides that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Secretary ``shall prescribe such regulations as may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary to carry out the administration of the insurance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs under this title,'' and section 1102 of the Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorizes the Secretary to ``make and publish such rules and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations *&blank;*&blank;* as may be necessary to the efficient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
administration of the functions with which [the Secretary] 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is charged under this Act.'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00095 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
To avoid recognizing nominal hospitals, while allowing adequate flexibility for legitimate networking and sharing
of services, we are proposing additional criteria in 42 CFR part 412, subpart B for determining whether an entity qualifies
for exclusion from the prospective payment systems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as a separate long-term care hospital. These criteria would be applied to entities seeking exclusion from the prospective
payment systems as a long-term care hospital. The revised criteria would apply to entities seeking exclusion as a
long-term care hospital for cost reporting periods beginning on or after October 1, 1994, including reapprovals
of long-term care exclusions for entities that are currently excluded. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We propose that in addition to meeting any of the classification requirements set forth in &sect;412.23, for cost
reporting periods beginning on or after October 1, 1994, to be excluded from the prospective payment systems, a hospital
located in the same building or in one or more entire buildings located on the same campus as another hospital and seeking
long-term care exclusion must meet the following requirements: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Separate governing body.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The hospital has a governing body that is separate from the governing body of the hospital from which it obtains space.
The hospital's governing body is not under the control of the hospital that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provides space, or of any third entity that controls both hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Separate chief medical officer.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The hospital has a single chief medical officer who reports directly to the governing body and who is responsible for
all medical staff activities of the hospital. The chief medical officer of the hospital is not employed by or under
contract with either the hospital that provides space or any third entity that controls both hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Separate medical staff.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The hospital has a medical staff that is separate from the medical staff of the hospital from which it obtains space.
The hospital's medical staff is directly accountable to the governing body 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the quality of medical care provided in the hospital, and adopts and enforces bylaws governing medical staff activities,
including criteria and procedures for recommending to the governing body the privileges to be granted to individual
practitioners. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Chief executive officer.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The hospital has a single chief executive officer through whom all administrative authority flows, and who exercises
control and surveillance over all administrative activities of the hospital. The chief executive officer is not
employed by, or under contract with, either the hospital that provides space or any third entity that controls both
hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Performance of basic hospital functions.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The hospital performs the basic hospital functions specified in &sect;&sect;482.21 through 482.27, 482.30, and
482.42 through the use of employees or under contracts or other agreements with entities other than the hospital from
which it obtains space, or a third entity that controls both hospitals. Food and dietetic services and housekeeping,
maintenance, and other services necessary to maintain a clean and safe physical environment could be obtained under
contracts or other agreements with the hospital that provides space, or with a third entity that controls both hospitals.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For purposes of these proposals, we would consider ``control'' to exist if an individual or organization has the power,
directly or indirectly, significantly to influence or direct the actions or policies of an organization or institution.
This definition of ``control'' is now codified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the regulations at &sect;413.17(b)(3), concerning allowable Medicare costs, and we would apply the same criteria
to determine whether hospitals and hospital units should be excluded from the prospective payment systems. Thus,
most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital managers and administrators are familiar with this definition. We welcome comments on this and other issues
concerning the proposed exclusion criteria. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, to ensure that hospitals that are excluded from the prospective payment systems are not confused with the
types of facilities that can participate separately in Medicare as distinct-part psychiatric hospitals or skilled
nursing facilities, we propose to delete all references in the current prospective payment systems regulations
to excluded units as ``distinct part'' facilities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00096 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Removal of the 1986 Malpractice Rule (&sect;413.56) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We propose removing from the regulations ``the 1986 malpractice rule'', which is set forth at &sect;413.56. We also
would remove all cross-references to &sect;413.56. This proposal is a technical change designed to conform the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations to the various authorities that have previously established that the 1986 malpractice rule is invalid.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The history of Medicare reimbursement policy under the cost-based reimbursement system for provider malpractice
insurance costs is described in detail in Sections I. and II.A. of the March 27, 1987 final rule (52 FR 9833). To summarize
briefly: for cost reporting periods beginning before July 1, 1979, provider malpractice insurance costs were included
in the general and administrative cost center (``G &amp; A pool'') with other overhead costs, and apportioned to the
Medicare program in accordance with the provider's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare utilization rate (52 FR 9834). (See also &sect;405.452(b)(1) (``the pre-1979 utilization method) redesignated
at &sect;413.53(a)(1)(i).) Effective for cost reporting periods beginning on or after July 1, 1979, ``the 1979 malpractice
rule'' removed provider malpractice insurance costs from the G &amp; A pool, and directly apportioned those insurance
costs based on the ratio of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provider malpractice losses paid for Medicare patients compared to losses paid for all patients (52 FR 9834). On April
1, 1986, we published an interim final rule with comment period that replaced the 1979 malpractice rule, effective
May 1, 1986, with the 1986 malpractice rule (51 FR 11142). The 1986 malpractice rule was set forth in regulations at
&sect;405.457, subsequently redesignated as &sect;413.456, and then confirmed in our above-cited March 27, 1987
final rule. The 1986 malpractice rule reestablished reimbursement of skilled nursing facility malpractice insurance
costs under the pre-1979 utilization method, &sect;413.56(c), whereas hospital malpractice insurance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs were reimbursed based on both a hospital's utilization rate and aspects of the claims-paid approach of the 1979
regulation. (See &sect;413.56(b).) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Secretary's authority to apply the hospital payment provisions of the 1986 malpractice rule has been eliminated
through a series of events. As promulgated initially, the 1986 rule applied retroactively, subject to the Medicare
program's general rules of administrative finality, to cost reporting periods beginning on or after July 1, 1979.
(See &sect;413.56(a).) Subsequently, however, the Supreme Court held that the Medicare statute does not generally
authorize retroactive rulemaking, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Bowen
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Georgetown University 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Hosp.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, 488 U.S. 204 (1988) (``
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Georgetown
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
''). Therefore, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Administrator of HCFA issued HCFA Ruling 89&hyph;1 (Jan. 26, 1989) which, first, discontinued retroactive application
of the 1986 rule and, second, required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application of the pre-1979 utilization method to all open hospital malpractice insurance cost claims, and all properly
pending appeals of the malpractice issue, for cost reporting periods beginning before the May 1, 1986 effective date
of the 1986 rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The prospective applicability of the 1986 malpractice rule was circumscribed from its outset by the prospective
payment system. For hospitals subject to the prospective payment system, malpractice insurance costs attributable
to inpatient services paid under the prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are subsumed under the prospectively determined payment rates that are applied to the various diagnosis related
groups. Thus, the 1986 malpractice rule applied prospectively to only malpractice insurance costs attributable
to: (1) outpatient services furnished by prospective payment hospitals; and (2) all services furnished by specialty
hospitals and distinct-part hospital units excluded from the prospective payment system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00097 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In the March 27, 1987 final rule, we determined that it was necessary to develop separate ``scaling factor formula''
values under the 1986 malpractice rule (see &sect;413.56(b)(3) through (5)), for malpractice insurance costs attributable
to hospital outpatient services and for costs attributable to inpatient services furnished by facilities excluded
from the prospective payment system (see 52 FR 9836). However, we were unable to derive separate formula values for
inpatient and outpatient services for ``the R factor'' in the formula (that is, the national Medicare malpractice
loss ratio, as adjusted for associated claims adjustment expense), due to the difficulty of securing hospital data
attributing malpractice claims (and associated claims 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment expense) separately to outpatient services and inpatient services. Moreover, separate scaling factor
formula values for hospital facilities excluded from the prospective payment system were not developed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Children's National Medical Center, et al.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Sullivan,
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Civil Action No. 90&hyph;1362&hyph;WBB (D.D.C. July 16, 1991) (``
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Children's National
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
''), the Secretary entered into a Consent Order providing that the 1986 malpractice rule is invalid and shall not be
enforced, due to the agency's inability to develop separate scaling factor formula values for outpatient services
and for inpatient services furnished by hospital facilities excluded from the prospective payment system. The 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Children's National
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Consent Order was approved by the United States District Court for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the District of Columbia on July 16, 1991. The Administrator of HCFA then issued HCFA Ruling 91&hyph;1 (Sept. 30, 1991),
which, first, provided that the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Children's National
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Consent Order governs all open hospital malpractice 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
insurance cost claims, and all properly pending appeals of the malpractice issue, for cost reporting periods beginning
on or after May 1, 1986 and, second, required reimbursement of such claims under the pre-1979 utilization method.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Supreme Court's 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Georgetown
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 decision, the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Children's National
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Consent Order, and the two corresponding HCFA Rulings establish that the 1986 malpractice rule is null and void as
to all open hospital malpractice insurance cost claims, and all properly pending hospital appeals of the malpractice
insurance cost reimbursement issue. See 42 CFR 401.108(c). See also HCFA Transmittal No. 395 (December 1993), which
deleted as ``obsolete,'' due to HCFA Ruling 91&hyph;1, the implementation provisions for the 1986 rule that were
formerly included in &sect;2214 of the Medicare Intermediary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manual, Part 2. We are proposing to conform Medicare regulations to the actual state of the law governing provider
malpractice insurance cost reimbursement by removing &sect;413.56 from the regulations, and by eliminating all
cross-references to &sect;413.56 from the regulations. As a result of this proposal, the amended Medicare rules
would reflect the previously established legal requirement that reasonable cost reimbursement for provider malpractice

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
insurance cost claims that are either open or the subject of properly pending appeals, must be determined under the
pre-1979 utilization method. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Related Technical Changes (&sect;&sect;412.20, 412.22, 412.25, 412.27, 412.29, 412.30, 412.96, 412.105, 412.108,
412.116, 412.130, 412.71, 413.40, 413.53, 413.174 and 482.66) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We are proposing to remove the words ``distinct part'', wherever they appear, in the following places: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Section 412.20(b)(1); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Section 412.22(b); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) The section title and paragraph (a) of &sect;412.25; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) The undesignated introductory texts of &sect;&sect;412.27 and 412.29; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) The section title of &sect;412.30; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Section 412.96(c)(1) and (c)(2); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) Section 412.105(b), (f)(5) and (g)(1)(iii); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(h) Section 412.108(a)(2); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) Section 412.116(a) and (b); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(j) The section title of 412.130; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(k) Section 412.130(a)(2) and (a)(3); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(l) Section 413.40(a)(2)(ii), (b)(1)(i) through (b)(1)(iii) and (f)(2); and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(m) In &sect;482.66(a)(7)(i), the second appearance of the words ``distinct part'' are removed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We also propose to revise the section titles of &sect;&sect;412.27 and 412.29 to remove the words ``distinct part''
and replace them with the word ``excluded''. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, we propose to make technical changes to &sect;&sect;412.71(b), 413.53 and 413.174(b)(4)(iv) to remove
and replace obsolete language and to correct references.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00098 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
VII. ProPAC Recommendations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We have reviewed the March 1, 1994 report submitted by ProPAC to Congress and have given its recommendations careful
consideration in conjunction with the proposals set forth in this document. Recommendations 1 through 6 focus 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on specific features of the President's Health Security Act and other health care reform activities and will be resolved
through the legislative process. Therefore, we are not addressing these recommendations in this document. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Recommendations 9, 12, and 13 concerning the update factors for inpatient operating costs are discussed in Appendix
D of this proposed rule. Recommendations 10 and 11 concerning the update factors for inpatient capital costs are also
discussed in Appendix E. Recommendations 15 and 16 concerning hospital wage data and the hospital wage index are discussed
in section III of this preamble. The remaining recommendations are discussed below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Controlling Medicare Spending Across All Sites of Care (Recommendation 7) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Medicare should continue to develop policies to monitor and control total expenditure growth across all sites of
care. Payment methods should not unduly favor the choice of one site over another. They should also provide consistent
incentives to constrain increases in volume and service intensity across physicians and other providers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 In response to a similar ProPAC recommendation last year, we stated in the proposed rule for FY 1994 that controlling
the overall growth of Medicare spending is of critical importance. We believe the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inpatient prospective payment system has been effective in slowing the growth in Medicare spending compared with
estimates of growth in spending under reasonable cost reimbursement. Also, during the first 2 years of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
physician fee schedule, the growth in spending for physician services under Medicare has been reduced. In addition,
we are developing a prospective payment system for outpatient hospital services that will provide the appropriate
economic incentives to help control the growth of Medicare spending for outpatient services. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While prospective payment has shown promise for controlling Medicare spending growth, we remain concerned about
growth in the volume and intensity of Medicare services. Currently, we are using the Medicare volume performance
standard to provide physicians with incentives to control volume and intensity. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
There are no similar volume control systems for other services. We are continuing to study other mechanisms for controlling
expenditures growth, such as bundling payments for multiple services into a single payment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Strengthening Medicare's Risk Contracting Program (Recommendation 8) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The Commission recommends that the Secretary improve Medicare's risk-based managed care program to give more Medicare
beneficiaries the option of enrolling in managed care plans. To increase plan participation, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deficiencies related to the risk adjustment formula and local differences in payments need to be corrected. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 We support giving more Medicare beneficiaries the option of enrolling in managed care plans. We are aware of the need
to institute new methodologies for payment to risk plans. Currently, we are sponsoring research on risk adjustment
methods, including diagnostic cost groups (DCGs) and ambulatory care groups (ACGs). In addition, our recent grants
announcement (Health Care Financing Research and Demonstration Cooperative Agreements and Grants for Fiscal Years
1994 Through 1996) (59 FR 1951) calls for work on risk adjustors, both for the Medicare and the under 65 populations.
We have also initiated a demonstration that is expected to test alternative payment methods that may incorporate
adjustments for enrollee health status. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With regard to correcting local differences in payment to Medicare risk plans, we note that section 1876 of the Act
requires payment to Medicare risk plans to be based on projected Medicare per capita costs for beneficiaries who 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are not enrolled in managed care plans. Given that there are local differences in Medicare's per capita costs for these
beneficiaries, local differences in Medicare's risk plan payment rates are a logical result. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Update to the Composite Rate for Dialysis Services (Recommendation 14) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The FY 1995 update recommendation for the composite rate for dialysis services accounts for the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The projected increase in the market basket for dialysis services in FY 1995, estimated at 4.3 percent; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;A positive adjustment of 0.7 percentage points to reflect the additional costs associated with scientific
and technological advances; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;A negative adjustment of 1.0 percentage points to encourage productivity improvements; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;A negative discretionary adjustment of 4.0 percentage points to reflect the relationship between payments
and estimated FY 1994 costs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00099 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This results in a net update recommendation of 0.0 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 We agree with ProPAC's recommendation not to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
propose a payment rate increase for dialysis services. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1991 audited data support this decision. As the Commission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
points out, these data show that independent renal facility 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
composite payment rates are higher than their Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowable costs, while hospital renal facilities continue to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report costs in excess of their payment rates. The audits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
did not provide an explanation for the higher reported costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of hospital renal facilities relative to independent renal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities. However, auditors found that hospital renal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities often fail to maintain adequate records for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allocation costs in the renal department. Since hospitals' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments are not affected by renal cost reports, Supplement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Worksheet I of HCFA&hyph;2552, hospitals do not always complete 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their cost reports accurately. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although we realize the importance of regular renal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
audits, the budget for audits continues to decline. Thus, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we do not plan to conduct renal audits in 1994 or 1995. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have requested funds to conduct renal audits in 1996. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
During this 2-year interval, we would review the 1992 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1993 cost report data for those renal facilities in the 1991 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
audit sample. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We believe that this analysis will provide cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information about the ESRD program in lieu of audited data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for these years. Furthermore, it will provide a basis for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyzing cost data over time and allow for better 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comparison of audited and unaudited costs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
D. Nursing Facility Wage Index (Recommendation 17) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The Secretary should collect data on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employee compensation and paid hours of employment for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employees of nursing facilities that care for Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
skilled nursing facility (SNF) patients. Once these data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
become available, the Secretary should develop a nursing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facility wage index and use it to adjust Medicare skilled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nursing facility payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 In recommending an adjustment to SNF 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments, we presume that ProPAC is referring to the SNF 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost limits. We agree that HCFA should collect the data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary to develop a SNF wage index. In the conference 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report on OBRA 1993, the conferees indicated an expectation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that HCFA collect such data (H.R. Rep. No. 2264, 103d Cong., 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1st Sess. 747 (1993)). However, we do not believe it is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prudent to recommend implementing a SNF wage index to adjust 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the SNF cost limits until the accuracy of those data and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impact of the resulting wage index have been thoroughly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyzed. Under current Medicare rules, most SNFs are paid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their reasonable costs for routine services subject to a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost limit. The current methodology for computing the SNF 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
routine service cost limits utilizes the hospital wage index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to account for geographic variations in wages. Only about 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
35 percent of facilities have costs at or above these cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We have maintained that, because hospitals and SNFs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generally compete for employees in the same labor market, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one would expect that the relationship of hospital area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average wages to hospital national average wages would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fairly represent the relationship of SNF area average wages 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to SNF national average wages. Therefore, we believe the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital wage index is an accurate mechanism for adjusting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the SNF routine service cost limits for geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variations in wages. While certain limited studies have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggested that a SNF-specific wage index may be more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accurate, no substantial empirical data have been offered to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refute our policy. The study undertaken by ProPAC utilized 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only limited data to develop a simulated wage index. Due to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the limited scope of this study, we believe it is not a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sufficient basis for refuting the accuracy of the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wage index relative to SNF wages. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We plan to begin collecting SNF wage data for potential 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use in the development of a SNF wage index. These data will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
help us answer two important questions related to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development of a SNF wage index. The first is whether SNFs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which are smaller than hospitals and have less sophisticated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accounting systems, can develop and provide accurate wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index data on a national basis. If such data can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provided, our second question is whether differences between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the resulting wage index and the hospital wage index would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be significant enough to justify the increased Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs involved and the increased reporting and information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
collection burden on SNFs (especially small and rural SNFs), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the fiscal intermediaries, and HCFA. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The collection of SNF wage data also will allow us to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determine the potential impact of a SNF wage index on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments to individual SNFs. A change in the wage index may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
favorably impact SNFs in some areas while it may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disadvantage SNFs in other areas (possibly significantly), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
even within the same State. While this latter point is not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a reason to avoid the use of a SNF wage index, it does point 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
out the absolute necessity for collecting accurate wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index data from SNFs for construction of the wage index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our experience with the development of a home health agency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(HHA) wage index is also relevant. In 1988, HCFA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implemented an HHA-specific wage index based on data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
received from HHAs. Subsequently, HCFA and Congress 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
received numerous complaints from providers concerning the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
burden that the reporting requirements posed and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accuracy of the data. As a result, Congress retroactively 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repealed its mandate for use of the HHA wage index and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required continued use of the hospital wage index. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
caused great confusion among both providers and fiscal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intermediaries. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Until SNF wage data have been collected and examined, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
we believe it is premature to recommend their use for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment of either the SNF cost limits or any future 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system that may require a wage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
E. Improving Outlier Payment Policy (Recommendation 18) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The Commission believes the outlier 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment provisions of the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of 1993 will improve the current policy for identifying 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlier cases and determining outlier payments under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system. However, the following changes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also should be implemented for FY 1995: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The estimated cost of the case and the outlier payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amount should no longer be adjusted to reflect the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital's indirect medical education and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disproportionate share status. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The marginal cost factor for cost outliers should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increased to 80 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These changes would further increase the effectiveness 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of outlier payment in protecting against the risk of large 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
losses on some cases. They would also improve the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
distribution of outlier payments across hospitals, including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals that receive a large number of transfer cases. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The Commission's first recommendation with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regard to improving outlier payment policy concerns the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methodology we use to determine whether the cost of a case 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeds the cost outlier threshold. ProPAC is asking that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare no longer standardize hospital costs to remove the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effects of the indirect costs of medical education (IME) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the costs associated with serving a disproportionate share 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of low income patients (DSH). The effect of standardizing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the hospital's charges to remove the effects of their IME 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and DSH adjustments is to lower the ``costs'' of cases, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purposes of applying the cost outlier threshold, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
teaching and disproportionate share hospitals relative to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We responded to this same recommendation in our May 26, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1993 proposed rule (58 FR 30255). In that response, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
noted that sections 1886(d)(5)(B)(i) and 1866(d)(5)(F)(ii) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the Act require that we pay hospitals for IME and DSH 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs associated with their outlier payments. Given this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statutory requirement, we believe it would be inappropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to implement the Commission's recommendation without a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change to the law. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00100 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In that same response, we also stated our belief that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the adjustment for IME and to the prospective payment rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is currently higher than the additional cost of serving a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare beneficiary at a teaching hospital. The policy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
advocated by ProPAC in this recommendation would have the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect of increasing outlier payments to teaching hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Relative to the costs associated with teaching activity, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are concerned that the teaching adjustment may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
overcompensate teaching hospitals for their Medicare cases. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For this reason, we believe it would be inappropriate to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement ProPAC's recommendation without making a reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the level of the IME adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We concur with ProPAC's second recommendation regarding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
raising the marginal cost factor for cost outliers to 80 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent from its current level of 75 percent. We currently 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pay 75 percent of the difference between the cost of a case 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the cost outlier threshold. Paying 75 percent of costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beyond a threshold protects hospitals against inordinate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
losses resulting from the costliest Medicare cases. As we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
describe in section IV.D. of this proposed rule, section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
13501(c) of Public Law 103&hyph;66 requires that we phase-out 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments for day outliers, while simultaneously increasing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments for cost outliers. We believe the change mandated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the statute improves Medicare's outlier policy by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
focusing outlier payments on the costliest cases. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Consistent with focusing Medicare's outlier payments on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cases with the greatest losses, we are raising the marginal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost factor to 80 percent. We note that raising the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
marginal cost factor to 80 percent necessitates an increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the fixed loss cost outlier threshold in order to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintain outlier payments at 5.1 percent of estimated total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
F. Level of the Indirect Medical Education Adjustment to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
PPS Operating Payments (Recommendation 19) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The Commission recommends that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indirect medical education adjustment to prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments for hospital inpatient operating costs be reduced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from its current level of 7.7 percent to 7.0 percent for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fiscal year 1995. This reduction should be implemented with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the anticipated decrease in indirect medical education 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments returned to all hospitals through a proportionate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase in the standardized payment amounts. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Commission also recommends continuation of the indirect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
medical education adjustment to operating payments until an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative system of compensating appropriately for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
higher costs of patient care in teaching institutions is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fully operational. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The first part of this recommendation is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
identical to ProPAC's recommendation on the level of the IME 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment for FY 1994, to which we responded in the May 26, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1993 proposed rule (58 FR 30255) and September 1, 1993 final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule (58 FR 46326) containing the changes to the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment systems for FY 1994. We agree that the IME 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment should be reduced. Although we have disagreed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with ProPAC in the past over the degree of reduction, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have proposed reductions as part of the President's budgets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the last several years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to ProPAC's methodology for estimating the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indirect cost effect of teaching without accounting for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DSH adjustment, we explained our opposition to this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggestion in the September 1, 1993 final rule (58 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
46328). To reiterate, our analysis indicates that there is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evidence of significantly higher costs related to DSH among 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
urban hospitals with 100 or more beds (see O'Dougherty et 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
al., 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Health Care Financing Review
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, Winter 1992, p. 31). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since these hospitals receive over 96 percent of all DSH 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments, we believe ProPAC's claim that ``DSH payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generally do not reflect differences in costs'' is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
overstated. We do agree, however, that the current level of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DSH payments exceeds the measured cost effect of DSH even 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
among urban hospitals with 100 or more beds. In that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regard, we point out that in the regression model used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimate the teaching effect, we did not specify the DSH 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variable as the level of DSH payments, but instead set it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equal to the DSH percentage for urban hospitals with 100 or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more beds and zero for all other hospitals. (For a complete 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
description of our estimating model, see the August 30, 1991 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final rule implementing the capital prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system, 56 FR 43370.) As ProPAC alludes to in its report, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not controlling for DSH leads to its higher IME cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimate relative to our IME cost estimate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The remainder of ProPAC's recommendation raises policy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issues related to the appropriate level of IME funding as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the transition is made from the current system to the new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
health care system. Since these transition issues are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently being addressed as part of the health care reform 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
debate, we are not responding in this document to this part 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of ProPAC's recommendation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
G. Prospective Payment Method for Outpatient Services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Recommendation 20) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The Commission believes a prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system for hospital outpatient services should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implemented. Outpatient payment reform should result in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent policies across all sites and providers. Payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should be based on a prospective price per unit of service 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
until other methods can be developed that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriately control the volume of services. The payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate should be adjusted to reflect justifiable cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
differences across settings. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 We appreciate ProPAC's support for efforts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to develop a prospective payment system for hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outpatient services. We share the Commission's view that a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital outpatient prospective payment system can provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meaningful incentives for controlling costs. We also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believe that such a system could have the potential to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adapted for other outpatient settings, resulting in more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent policies across varying sites of service. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
look forward to the Commission's comments when our report on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a hospital outpatient prospective payment system is issued. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
H. Revision of Payment Formula for Outpatient Services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Recommendation 21) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Until prospective payment systems can 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be implemented for hospital outpatient services, the blend 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formula used to calculate hospital payments for ambulatory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
surgery center-approved procedures, radiology services, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diagnostic services should be revised. The formula should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subtract beneficiary copayment after the total payment has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been calculated. The resulting reduction in Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program payments to hospitals should be used to partially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offset increases in program expenditures due to reducing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beneficiary liability. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00101 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 We agree that the blend formula used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculate Medicare payments to hospitals for ambulatory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
surgical center-approved procedures, radiology services, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certain other diagnostic procedures should be revised, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
suggested by ProPAC. It should be noted that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
President's proposed Health Security Act contains a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provision that would make the correction suggested by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ProPAC. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
I. Beneficiary Liability for Hospital Outpatient Services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Recommendation 22) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Beneficiary coinsurance for hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outpatient services should be limited to 20 percent of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare-allowed payment, as it is in other settings. For 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services not paid on a prospective basis, beneficiary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
copayment would need to be estimated because it is not known 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
at the time of service delivery. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 We share ProPAC's concern that beneficiaries 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may be paying a disproportionate share of the total payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for hospital outpatient services under the current cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based system. However, we are in the process of developing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a prospective payment system for hospital outpatient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services that will be moving away from costs and charges as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a basis for payment. We believe that it would be most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate to make any changes to beneficiary coinsurance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in conjunction with the implementation of this hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outpatient prospective payment system. Therefore, as we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
develop this system, we are reviewing the issue of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beneficiary liability and are working to come up with an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approach that will be fair to beneficiaries, while 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimizing the negative financial impact on the Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
J. Limit the Length of Outpatient Observation Stays 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Recommendation 23) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recommendation:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The Commission recommends limiting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
length of hospital outpatient observation stays. Further, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these changes should be coordinated with Peer Review 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Organizations (PROs) so that appropriate short stay 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
admissions are not denied. ProPAC supports HCFA's efforts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in this regard. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 We appreciate ProPAC's support for our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efforts to limit the length of hospital outpatient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
observation stays. Later this year, we plan to release 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Part A Intermediary Manual and Hospital Manual instructions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to help curb inappropriate use of outpatient observation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services, including lengthy stays of days and weeks. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
instructions will require hospitals to admit patients who 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have been under observation in outpatient departments for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
two consecutive midnights. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Release of this new national policy will provide needed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistency throughout the country in applying outpatient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
observation service guidelines, reduce inappropriate use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such services, and reduce the beneficiary's liability for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Part B copayments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On August 13, 1993, we released a letter to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Executive Directors of all PROs to clarify PRO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibilities when conducting reviews of cases involving 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
short hospital stays. PROs were asked to assure that their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review staffs base their determinations on the beneficiary's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
need for inpatient services rather than basing the decision 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the payment consequences. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00102 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
VIII. Other Required Information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Paperwork Reduction Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed in detail in section III.C. of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preamble, we are proposing several changes to forms used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gather hospital wage data, specifically the Worksheet S&hyph;3, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Part II, of the Medicare cost report, and the provider cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report questionnaire (HCFA&hyph;339). The current information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
collection requirements associated with these items have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been approved by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under approval numbers 0938&hyph;0050 and 0938&hyph;0301, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
respectively. We are proposing that the changes to these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forms would be effective for cost reporting periods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning on or after October 1, 1994. However, these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes will not be effective until OMB approval is received 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the authority of the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1980 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(44 U.S.C. 3501 et seq.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Requests for Data from the Public 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In order to respond promptly to public requests for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data related to the prospective payment system, we have set 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
up a process under which commenters can gain access to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
raw data on an expedited basis. Generally, the data are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available in computer tape format or cartridges; however, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some files are available on diskette. Data files are listed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below with the cost of each. Anyone wishing to purchase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data tapes, cartridges, or diskettes should submit a written 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
request along with a company check or money order (payable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to HCFA-PUF) to cover the cost, to the following address: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Health Care Financing Administration, Public Use Files, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accounting Division, P.O. Box 7520, Baltimore, Maryland 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
21207&hyph;0520, (410) 597&hyph;5151. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Expanded Modified MedPAR-Hospital (National) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Medicare Provider Analysis and Review (MedPAR) file 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contains records for 100 percent of Medicare beneficiaries 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
using hospital inpatient services in the United States. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
records are stripped of most data elements that will permit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
identification of beneficiaries. Provider numbers and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beneficiary claim numbers have been encrypted to protect the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
privacy of individuals. The hospital is identified by the 6 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
position Medicare billing number. The file is available to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
persons qualifying under the terms of the Notice of Proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
New Routine Uses for an Existing System of Records published 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 on December 24, 1984 (49 FR 49941), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and amended by the July 2, 1985 notice (50 FR 27361). The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national file consists of approximately 11 million records. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under the requirements of these notices, a data release must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be signed by the purchaser before release of these data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For all files requiring a signed data release agreement, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
please write or call to obtain a blank agreement form before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
placing order. Two versions of this file are created each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year. They support the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (NPRM) published in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, usually available by the end of May. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
file is derived from the MedPAR file with a cutoff of 3 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
months after the end of the fiscal year (December file). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Final Rule published in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, usually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available by the first week of September. This file is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
derived from the MedPAR file with a cutoff of 9 months after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the end of the fiscal year (June file). 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=25 g=1 f=1 -->
Media: Tape/Cartridge 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
File cost: $3,415.00 per fiscal year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Periods available: FY 1988 through FY 1993, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and FY 1992 for Severity DRGs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Expanded Modified MedPAR-Hospital (State) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The State MedPAR file contains records for 100 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of Medicare beneficiaries using hospital inpatient services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in a particular State. The records are stripped of most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data elements that will permit identification of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beneficiaries. Provider numbers and beneficiary claim 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
numbers have been encrypted to protect the privacy of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individuals. The hospital is identified by the 6 position 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare billing number. The file is available to persons 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifying under the terms of the Notice of Proposed New 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Routine Uses for an Existing System of Records published in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the December 24, 1984 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 notice, and amended 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the July 2, 1985 notice. This file is a subset of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Expanded Modified MedPAR-Hospital (National) as described 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above. Under the requirements of these notices, a data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
release must be signed by the purchaser before release of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these data. Two versions of this file are created each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year. They support the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;NPRM published in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, usually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available by the end of May. This file is derived from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MedPAR file with a cutoff of 3 months after the end of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fiscal year (December file). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Final Rule published in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, usually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available by the first week of September. This file is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
derived from the MedPAR file with a cutoff of 9 months after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the end of the fiscal year (June file). 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

