<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
For the final rule, we will recalculate the FY 1995 update factor on the basis of the June 1994 update of HCRIS. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We note that the effect of the update on the Federal rate is limited by the requirement of budget neutrality until FY
1996. Thus, although the update factor for inflation is 2.22 percent, the FY 1995 Federal rate is lower than the FY 1994
Federal rate because of the effects of the budget neutrality adjustment. We also note that the FY 1995 budget neutrality
target is determined by the estimate of FY 1995 capital costs, not by the 2-year average update factor applied to the
FY 1995 Federal rate. We further discuss the basis for the budget neutrality target in section III.A.4 below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
2. Outlier Payment Adjustment Factor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Section 412.312(c) establishes a unified outlier methodology for inpatient operating and inpatient capital-related
costs. A single set of thresholds is used to identify outlier cases for both inpatient operating and inpatient capital-related
payments. Outlier payments are made only on the portion of the Federal rate that is used to calculate the hospital's
inpatient capital-related payments (for example, 40 percent for cost reporting periods beginning in FY 1995 for
hospitals paid under the fully prospective methodology). Section 412.308(c)(2) provides that the standard Federal
rate for inpatient capital-related costs be reduced by an adjustment factor equal to the estimated additional payments
under the Federal rate for outlier cases, determined as a proportion of inpatient capital-related payments under
the Federal rate. The outlier thresholds are set so that estimated outlier payments are 5.1 percent of estimated inpatient
operating payments. The inpatient capital-related outlier reduction factor is then set according to the estimated
inpatient capital-related outlier payments that would be made if all hospitals were paid according to 100 percent
of the Federal rate. For purposes of calculating the outlier thresholds and the outlier reduction factor, we model
all hospitals as if paid 100 percent of the Federal rate because, as explained above, outlier payments are made only
on the portion of the Federal rate that is included in the hospital's inpatient capital-related payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the September 1, 1993 final rule, we estimated that outlier payments for FY 1994 would equal 5.46 percent of inpatient
capital-related payments based on the Federal rate. Accordingly, we applied an outlier adjustment factor of 0.9454
to the Federal rate. Based on the thresholds as set forth in section II.A.4.d of the addendum, we estimate that outlier
payments will equal 6.28 percent of inpatient capital-related payments based on the Federal rate in FY 1995. We are
therefore proposing an outlier adjustment factor of 0.9372 to the Federal rate. When we modeled the combined operating
and capital outlier payments, we found that using a common set of thresholds resulted in a higher percentage of outlier
payments for capital-related costs than for operating costs. We believe the increase in capital-related outlier
payments for FY 1995 results from a combination of the lower proposed capital standard payment amount and the change
to a fixed loss threshold for cost outlier payments. As a result, estimated capital outlier payments for FY 1995 represent
a higher percentage of total capital standard payments than in FY 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The outlier reduction factors are not built permanently into the rates; that is, they are not applied cumulatively
in determining the Federal rate. Therefore, the net change in the outlier adjustment to the Federal rate for FY 1995
is .9913 (.9372/.9454). Thus, the proposed outlier adjustment would reduce the FY 1995 Federal rate by 0.87 percent
(.9913&hyph;1) compared with the FY 1994 outlier adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00127 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
3. Budget Neutrality Adjustment Factor for Changes in DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Weights and the Geographic Adjustment Factor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Section 412.308(c)(4)(ii) requires that the Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate be adjusted so that estimated aggregate payments for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the fiscal year based on the Federal rate after any changes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resulting from the annual reclassification and recalibration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the DRG weights and changes in the geographic adjustment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factor equal estimated aggregate payments that would have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been made on the basis of the Federal rate without such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes. We used the actuarial model described in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Appendix B to estimate the aggregate payments that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have been made on the basis of the Federal rate without 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes in the DRG classifications and weights and in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geographic adjustment factor. We also used the model to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimate aggregate payments that would be made on the basis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the Federal rate as a result of those changes. We then 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used these figures to compute the adjustment required to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintain budget neutrality for changes in DRG weights and in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the geographic adjustment factor. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For FY 1994, we calculated a GAF/DRG budget neutrality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factor of 1.0053. In this proposed rule, we are applying a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factor of 1.00115. The GAF/DRG budget neutrality factors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are built permanently into the rates; that is, they are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applied cumulatively in determining the Federal rate. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
follows from the requirement that aggregate payments each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year be no more than it is estimated that they would have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been in the absence of the changes from the annual DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification and recalibration and in the geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment factor. The incremental change in the adjustment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from FY 1994 to FY 1995 is 1.00115. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We note that this factor accounts for DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassifications and recalibration and changes in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geographic adjustment factor. It also incorporates the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effects on the geographic adjustment factor of FY 1995 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geographic reclassification decisions made by the MGCRB 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compared to FY 1994 decisions. However, it does not account 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for changes in payments due to changes in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disproportionate share and indirect medical education 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment factors or in the large urban add-on. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
4. Budget Neutrality Adjustment Factor to Assure Aggregate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Payments Equal 90 Percent of Reasonable Cost Payments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Section 1886(g)(1)(A) of the Act requires that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aggregate payments made each year in FY 1992 through FY 1995 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for hospital inpatient services be reduced in a manner that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
results in payments equal to 90 percent of the amount the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Secretary estimates would have been payable on a reasonable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost basis for inpatient capital-related costs in that year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
No retroactive increase or decrease is made if aggregate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments are greater than or less than 90 percent of actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare inpatient capital-related costs for that year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 412.352 of the regulations provides that HCFA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determines an adjustment to the hospital-specific rate and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Federal rate proportionately, so that the estimated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments for capital in each year from FY 1992 through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995 will equal 90 percent of what would have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payable that year on a reasonable cost basis. The effect of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this provision is that the reduction required under section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1886(g)(1)(A) of the Act is realized entirely through a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction in the prospective payments for capital costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(that is, no reduction is made for this purpose in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonable cost payments for old capital) in FY 1992 through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For FY 1994, we determined that a budget neutrality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factor of 0.8947 was required so that estimated aggregate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments for inpatient capital-related costs would equal 90 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent of what would have been payable on a reasonable cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basis in that year. For FY 1995, we are proposing a budget 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neutrality factor of 0.7986. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As we explain in Appendix B, we determine the budget 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neutrality adjustment factor on the basis of a projected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995 capital costs per case budget neutrality target. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
develop this target from available data on average Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital costs per case for all short-term acute care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals subject to the capital prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(that is, data from excluded and waiver hospitals were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eliminated). For the FY 1995 proposed rule, we have actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data for FY 1992 from the December 1993 HCRIS update. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also have revised estimates of the rates of increase in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare capital costs per case for the years FY 1993 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through FY 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
At the time of the final rule for FY 1994, we had 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated that there would be a 30.53 percent increase in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare inpatient capital cost per case between FY 1992 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1995. In this proposed rule for FY 1995, we estimate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that there will be a 12.83 percent increase in Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inpatient capital cost per case between FY 1992 and FY 1994, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a decline of 13.56 percent (1.1283/1.3053=.8644, or ^13.56 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent) in the estimated rate of increase since the final 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule for FY 1995. The following chart shows how the rate-of-increase estimates for FY 1992 through FY 1995 were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculated in the final rule for FY 1994 and in this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed rule for FY 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



