<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
The chart shows the causes of the decline in the rate-of-increase projections since the final rule for FY 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As the chart shows, the decline in the rate of increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projections is due almost entirely to a decline in the rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of increase projections for Medicare inpatient capital. (As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the chart also shows, the rate of increase projections for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare enrollment, Medicare admissions incidence, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare admissions, have changed only marginally.) In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
turn, the decline in the rate of increase projections for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare inpatient capital is the result of declines in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate of increase projections for total inpatient capital and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare share. For FY 1993, the panel survey data that we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use to develop the projected rate of increase for total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inpatient capital shows a decline in actual interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments compared to FY 1992. We believe that this decline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in interest payments is due to refinancing of debt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
instruments by hospitals to take advantage of lower interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rates. For FY 1994 and FY 1995, we are projecting that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total inpatient capital cost increases will return to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level at which increases for depreciation costs alone have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been running (i.e., 7&hyph;8 percent) as the number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refinancings declines. The projection of the rate of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase in Medicare share for FY 1993 is similarly grounded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in panel survey data. The panel survey data for that year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
show that, contrary to our previous assumption, Medicare day 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
share has not been increasing in proportion to the increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Medicare admissions. We have accordingly reduced the FY 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1994 and FY 1995 rate of increase projections for Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
share to account for that fact. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition to the decline in the rate of increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimates since the FY 1994 final rule, there has been a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
slight (^0.54 percent) decrease in the FY 1992 cost per case 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the December 1993 HCRIS data, compared to the June 1993 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
HCRIS data that we used for FY 1994. The following chart 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shows the combined effect of the reduced rates of increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimates and the reduced FY 1992 cost per case by comparing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the projections from FY 1992 to FY 1995 at the time of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FY 1994 final rule and in this proposed rule.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

