<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
Appendix D: Recommendation of Update Factors for Operating Cost Rates of Payment for Inpatient Hospital Services

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I. Background 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Several provisions of the Social Security Act (the Act) address the setting of update factors for services furnished
in FY 1995 by hospitals subject to the prospective payment system and those excluded from the prospective payment
system. Section 1886(b)(3)(B)(i)(X) of the Act sets the FY 1995 percentage increases in the operating cost standardized
amounts equal to the rate of increase in the hospital market basket minus 2.5 percentage points for prospective payment
hospitals located in urban areas. For hospitals located in rural areas, section 1886(b)(3)(B)(i)(X) requires the
Secretary to apply an update so as to make the rural national average standardized amounts equal to the other urban
national average standardized amount. Section 1886(b)(3)(B)(iv) sets the FY 1995 percentage increase to the hospital-specific
rate applicable to sole community hospitals equal to the rate of increase in the hospital market basket minus 2.2 percentage
points. Section 1886(b)(3)(B)(ii) of the Act sets the FY 1995 percentage increase in the rate-of-increase limits
for hospitals excluded from the prospective payment system equal to the rate of increase in the excluded hospital
market basket minus a reduction factor (not to exceed 1.0 percent) depending on the provider's operating costs and
target amounts. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In accordance with section 1886(d)(3)(A) of the Act, we are proposing to update the average standardized amounts,
the hospital-specific rates and the rate-of-increase limits for hospitals excluded from the prospective payment
system as provided in section 1886(b)(3)(B) of the Act. Based on the currently forecasted market basket increase
of 3.6 percent for hospitals subject to the prospective payment system, the proposed updates in the standardized
amounts are 1.1 percent for hospitals in urban areas and approximately 8.4 percent for hospitals in rural areas. The
proposed update in the hospital-specific rate applicable to sole community hospitals is 1.4 percent (that is, the
market basket rate of increase of 3.6 percent minus 2.2 percentage points). The proposed update for hospitals excluded
from the prospective payment system is based on the percentage increase in the excluded hospital market basket (currently
estimated at 3.7 percent) minus a reduction factor (not to exceed 1.0 percent). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sections 1886(e)(2)(A) and (3)(A) of the Act require that the Prospective Payment Assessment Commission (ProPAC)
recommend to the Congress by March 1, 1994 an update factor that takes into account changes in the market basket rate
of increase index, hospital productivity, technological and scientific advances, the quality of health care provided
in hospitals, and long-term cost effectiveness in the provision of inpatient hospital services. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In its March 1, 1994 report, ProPAC recommended update factors to the standardized amounts equal to the percentage
increase in the market basket minus 1.5 percentage points for urban hospitals and the market basket rate of increase
plus 1.6 percentage points for hospitals located in rural areas. Based on its market basket rate of increase estimate
of 3.7 percent, ProPAC's recommended updates to the standardized amounts equal 2.2 percent for hospitals located
in urban areas and 5.3 percent for hospitals located in rural areas. ProPAC recommended that the update for the hospital-specific
rates applicable to sole community hospitals be equal to the average update for all hospitals (that is, equal to the
percentage increase in the market basket minus 1.0 percentage point, or 2.7 percent based on a market basket rate of
increase estimate of 3.7 percent). The components of ProPAC's update factor recommendations are described in detail
in the ProPAC report, which is published as Appendix F to this document. We discuss ProPAC's recommendations concerning
the update factors and our responses to those recommendations below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 1886(e)(4) of the Act requires that the Secretary, taking into consideration the recommendations of ProPAC,
recommend update factors for FY 1995 that take into account the amounts necessary for the efficient and effective
delivery of medically appropriate and necessary care of high quality. Under section 1886(e)(5) of the Act, we are
required to publish the recommended FY 1995 update factors that are provided for under section 1886(e)(4) of the Act.
Accordingly, this appendix provides our recommendations of appropriate update factors, our analysis of the derivation
of the amount of the update factors, and our responses to the ProPAC recommendations concerning the update factors.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
II. Secretary's Recommendations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under section 1886(e)(4) of the Act, we are recommending that the standardized amounts be increased by an average
amount equal to the market basket rate of increase minus 2.5 percentage points for hospitals located in urban areas
and the market basket rate of increase plus 4.8 percentage points for hospitals located in rural areas (before the
effects of adjustments for outlier, geographic reclassification and recalibration of the DRG relative weights).
Our recommendation for a higher update to the rural standardized amount is intended to eliminate the differential
between the standardized amounts for other urban and rural hospitals, as required by section 1886(b)(3)(B)(i)(X)
of the Act. We are also recommending an update of the market basket rate of increase minus 2.2 percentage points to the
hospital-specific rate for sole community hospitals. These figures are consistent with the President's budget
recommendation, given the new market basket forecast of 3.6 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We recommend that hospitals excluded from the prospective payment system receive an update equal to the percentage
increase in the market basket that measures input price increases for services furnished by excluded hospitals minus
1.0 percentage point. That market basket rate of increase is currently forecast at 3.7 percent. Subtracting 1.0 percentage
point would result in an update for hospitals excluded from the prospective payment system of 2.7 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As required by section 1886(e)(4) of the Act, we have taken into consideration the recommendations of ProPAC in setting
these recommended update factors. Our responses to the ProPAC recommendations concerning the update factors are
discussed below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
III. ProPAC Recommendation for Updating the Prospective Payment System Standardized Amounts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For FY 1995, ProPAC recommends that the standardized amount be updated by the following factors: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The projected rate of increase in the hospital market basket (which was estimated to be 3.7 percent in ProPAC's
March 1, 1994 report) with an adjustment of 0.0 percentage points to account for the different wage and salary price
proxies used for the ProPAC market basket rate of increase. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;A correction of ^1.1 percent to reflect the forecast error in the FY 1993 market basket rate of increase. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00208 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;A discretionary adjustment factor of ^0.4 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
composed of an allowance of 0.3 percent for scientific and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technological advancement and an allowance of ^0.7 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for productivity improvement. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;An adjustment for case-mix change of 0.0 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;A differential update of 3.1 percent for hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
located in rural areas to reflect the phasing out of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
differential in the standardized amounts between rural and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other urban hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Overall, the net increase employing the above factors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is the percentage increase in the hospital market basket 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minus 1.5 percentage points for urban hospitals and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market basket percentage increase plus 1.6 percentage points 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for rural hospitals. Based on the market basket rate of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase estimate of 3.7 percent used in its March 1, 1994 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report, ProPAC recommends that urban hospitals receive a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2.2 percent update and rural hospitals receive a 5.3 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
update. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Response: We are recommending an update that is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent with the Administration's budget proposal and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of section 1886(b)(3)(B)(i) of the Act, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amended by section 13501(a) of Public Law 103&hyph;66. Our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommendation is that: 1) the average update for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system hospitals located in urban areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for FY 1995 be equal to market basket rate of increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forecast minus 2.5 percentage points, 2) the update to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rural standardized amounts be sufficient to equate the rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the other urban standardized amounts. Based on HCFA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current forecast of the market basket rate of increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3.6 percent), we recommend an update for FY 1995 for urban 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals equal to 1.1 percent (the market basket rate of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase minus 2.5 percentage points), and for rural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals equal to 8.4 percent (the market basket percentage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase plus 4.8 percentage points). Under these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommendations, we now estimate that the average update for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all prospective payment system hospitals would be the market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basket minus 1.4 percentage points, or approximately 2.2 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent. As we have explained in section II of the Addendum 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to this proposed rule, our recommended updates would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applied to the unadjusted FY 1994 standardized payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amounts. That is, the FY 1994 standardized payment amounts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
published in our September 1, 1993 final rule (at 58 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
46362) would be increased to remove the effects of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustments made for outliers and geographic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification before the recommended updates would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applied. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our recommendation is supported by the following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyses that measure changes in hospital productivity, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scientific and technological advances, practice pattern 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes, and changes in case mix: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Productivity: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Service level productivity is defined as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ratio of total service output to full time equivalent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employees (FTEs). While we recognize that productivity is a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
function of many variables (for example, labor, non-labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
material and capital inputs), we use a labor productivity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measure in our framework, since the current update framework 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applies to operating payment. To recognize that we are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
apportioning the short run output changes to the labor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
input, we weight our productivity measure for operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs by the appropriate share of labor input to total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating input to determine the expected effect on cost per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
case. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our recommendation for the service productivity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
component is based on historical trends in productivity and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total output for both the hospital industry and the general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
economy, and projected levels of future hospital service 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
output. ProPAC has also estimated cumulative service 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
productivity growth to be 4.9 percent from 1985&hyph;1989 or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.2 percent annually. At the same time, they estimate total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
service growth at 3.4 percent annually, implying a ratio of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
service productivity growth to output growth of 0.35. Our 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MedPAR analysis indicates total Medicare service output 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(charges per admission, adjusted for CPI change) increased 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
20.9 percent from 1985&hyph;1993, or an approximate average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual increase of 2.4 percent. Since it is not possible at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this time to develop a productivity measure specific to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare patients, we examined productivity (output per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hour) and output (gross domestic product) for the economy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Depending on the exact time period, annual changes in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
productivity range from 0.3 to 0.35 of the change in output 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(that is, a 1.0 percent increase in output would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
correlated with an 0.3 to 0.35 percent change in output per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hour). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under our framework, the recommended update is based in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part on expected productivity_that is, projected service 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
output during the year multiplied by the historical ratio of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
service productivity to total service output, multiplied by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the share of labor in total operating inputs, as calculated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the hospital market basket rate of increase. This method 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimates an expected labor productivity improvement in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
same proportion to expected total service growth that has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occurred in the past and assumes that, at a minimum, growth 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in FTEs changes proportionally to the growth in total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
service output. Thus, the recommendation allows for unit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
productivity to be smaller than the historical averages in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years that output growth is relatively low, and higher in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years that output growth is larger than the historical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
trend. Based on the above estimates from both the hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
industry and the economy, we have chosen to employ the range 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of ratios of productivity change to output change of 0.30 to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.35. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00209 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The expected change in total hospital service output is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the product of projected growth in total admissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(adjusted for outpatient usage), projected real case-mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
growth, and expected quality enhancing intensity growth, net 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of expected decline in intensity due to reduction of cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ineffective practice. Case-mix growth and intensity numbers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for Medicare are used as proxies for those of the total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital, since case-mix increases (used in the intensity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measure as well) are unavailable for non-Medicare patients. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, expected output growth is simply the product of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected change in intensity (0.0 percent), projected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
admissions change (3.2 percent for 1995) and projected real 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
case-mix growth (1.0 to 1.4 percent for 1995), or 4.2 to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4.6 percent. The share of direct labor services in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital market basket rate of increase (consisting of wages 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and salaries and employee benefits) is 61.7 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Multiplying the expected change in total hospital service 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
output by the ratio of historical service productivity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change to total service growth of 0.30 to 0.35 and by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
direct labor share percentage provides our productivity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard of 0.8 to 1.0 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ProPAC also believes hospitals should be given an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentive for additional productivity improvement. ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures productivity as the ratio of hospital admissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(adjusted for case mix and outpatient services) per FTE 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
employee (adjusted for changes in skill mix). ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes in its productivity measurement the effect of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes in practice patterns. We treat practice pattern 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes as a portion of our intensity adjustment, described 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below. This year, ProPAC assumes a productivity gain of at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
least 1.4 percent and recommends a ^0.7 percentage point 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment on the basis that any productivity gains should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be shared equally by Medicare and hospitals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Intensity
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
: We base our intensity standard on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combined effect of three separate factors: changes in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use of quality enhancing services, changes in the use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services due to shifts in within-DRG severity, and changes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the use of services due to reductions of cost-ineffective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practices. For 1995, we recommend an adjustment of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.0 percent. The basis of this recommendation is discussed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We have no empirical evidence that accurately gauges 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the level of quality-enhancing technology changes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Typically, a specific new technology increases costs in some 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
uses and decreases costs in other uses. Concurrently, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
health status is improved in some situations while in other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
situations it may be unaffected or even worsened using the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
same technology. It is difficult to separate out the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relative significance of each of the cost-increasing effects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for individual technologies and new technologies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The quality enhancing technology component is intended 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to recognize the use of services which increase cost but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whose value in terms of enhanced health-status is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commensurate with these costs. Such services may result 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from technological change, or in some cases, increased use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of existing technologies. The latter recognizes that as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost and medical effectiveness studies become available, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some increased use of existing, as well as new, services may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be warranted. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The component for reduction of cost-ineffective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practice recognizes that some improvements in practice 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
patterns could be made so that the intensity of services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provided is more consistent with the efficient use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited resources. That is, improvements could be made so 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the number of services provided during an inpatient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stay, and their complexity, produce an improvement in health 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
status that is consistent with the cost of care. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
component of our update recommendation is intended to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
encourage both hospitals and physicians to more carefully 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consider the cost-effectiveness of medical care. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
component of the framework also accounts for real within-DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change, since that should be directly reflected in the CMI- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjusted growth in real charges per case. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We note that, since 1985, the intensity of services per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inpatient admission has increased, even after adjusting for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the growth in real case mix each year. These increases, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which are similar to pre-prospective payment system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increases, followed intensity decreases in both 1984 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1985. Following methods developed by HCFA's Office of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Actuary for deriving hospital output estimates from total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital charges, ProPAC has estimated that case mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constant intensity per admission increased at an average of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.5 percent annually from 1985&hyph;1990. We have developed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Medicare-specific intensity measures for inpatient services 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based on a five-year average using FY 1988&hyph;1993 MedPAR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
billing data. Consistent with ProPAC, case-mix constant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensity is calculated as the change in total Medicare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
charges per discharge adjusted for changes in the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
charge per unit of service as measured by the Medical CPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital component, and changes in real case-mix. Past 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
studies of case-mix change by the RAND Corporation indicate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the change in real case-mix ranges from 1.0 percent to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.4 percent per year. If we assume that real case-mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change is 1.4 percent per year, we estimate case-mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constant intensity to have declined by an average of 0.4 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent each year during the FY 1988&hyph;1993 period, a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cumulative decrease of 2.1 percent. If we assume that real 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
case-mix has been growing at 1.0 percent annually, case-mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constant intensity has declined by an average of 0.1 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annually, for a cumulative decrease of 0.6 percent. We note 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that regardless of which real case-mix number we assume (1.0 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or 1.4), we used the observed case-mix increase for FY 1993 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of 0.8 percent to calculate the real case-mix constant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensity. Since we estimate that intensity has declined 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during the FY 1988&hyph;1993 period, we are recommending a 0.0 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent intensity adjustment for FY 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
