<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
The above analysis supports our recommended update to the urban standardized amounts of the market basket rate of
increase (that is, 3.6 percent) minus 2.5 percentage points, or 1.1 percent. In addition, we believe a differential
update for the standardized amount applicable to rural hospitals is appropriate in order to phase out the differential
between the rural and other urban standardized amounts, as required by section 1886(d)(3)(A)(iii) of the Act. We
are recommending that the rural standardized amount be updated by the market basket plus 4.8 percentage points for
a total update of 8.4 percent. This recommendation is consistent with the President's budget, which includes an update
to the standardized amounts for rural hospitals of the market basket rate of increase plus the amount necessary to
eliminate the differential between the rural and other urban standardized amounts. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We recommend that the hospital-specific rates applicable to sole community hospitals be increased by the update
factor that approximates that applicable for urban hospitals. We believe that the considerations used to develop
the update recommendations for the rural standardized amounts that would eliminate the difference between the rural
and other urban standardized amounts are not a relevant consideration for the hospital-specific rates. Therefore,
we are recommending an update to hospital-specific rates of the market basket rate of increase minus 2.2 percentage
points, which is consistent with current law and the President's budget. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=4 -->
IV. ProPAC Recommendation for Updating the Rate-of-Increase Limits for Excluded Hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
ProPAC recommends an update factor equal to the market basket rate of increase minus 1.0 percentage point for excluded
hospitals and units. The 1.0 percentage point reduction represents a reduction of 1.0 percentage point to account
for the forecast error in the FY 1993 market basket rate of increase for excluded units, no increase to reflect the different
compensation price proxies used by ProPAC, and no allowance for new technology. ProPAC no longer recommends an additional
allowance based on the year the hospital or unit was excluded from the prospective payment system, pending our report
to Congress on payment reform for excluded hospitals and units as mandated by Public Law 101- 508. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Response: We recommend that hospitals excluded from the prospective payment system receive an update equal to the
percentage increase in the market basket that measures input price increases for services furnished by excluded
hospitals minus 1.0 percentage point. The reduction is consistent with the updates provided under current law and
in the President's Budget. The market basket rate of increase for excluded hospitals is currently forecast at 3.7
percent. Subtracting 1.0 percentage point would result in an update of 2.7 for excluded hospitals and units.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00213 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=4 -->
Appendix E: Development of Update Framework for Prospective Payment System for Inpatient Hospital Capital-Related
Costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I. Introduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
For FY 1992 through FY 1995, &sect;412.308(c)(1) provides that the update for the capital prospective payment rates
(Federal rate and hospital-specific rate) will be based on a 2-year moving average of actual increases in Medicare
inpatient capital costs per discharge. The regulations provide that, beginning in FY 1996, HCFA will determine the
update in the capital prospective payment rates based on an analytical framework that will take into account (1) changes
in the price of capital (which we will incorporate into a capital input price index), and (2) appropriate changes in
capital requirements resulting from development of new technologies and other factors (such as the diffusion of
existing technologies and existing hospital capacity and utilization). The objective of the capital update framework
is to determine a rate of increase in aggregate capital prospective payments that, along with a rate of increase in
DRG operating payments, ensures a joint flow of capital and operating services for efficient and effective care for
Medicare patients. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although the analytical framework will not be employed to determine the annual update factor until FY 1996, we are
presenting a series of preliminary models, using available data and concepts, of an update framework for the prospective
payment system for hospital inpatient capital-related costs. We have presented a series of models in our FY 1992,
FY 1993, and FY 1994 rulemaking documents. We received no comments on the most recent model of the framework, which
appeared in the September 1, 1993 final rule (58 FR 46489). Below we discuss the current version of the model, based
on our previously published version and our continued analysis of the data and concepts incorporated into the framework.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The model update framework includes a capital input price index (CIPI) that parallels the operating input price index.
The CIPI measures the pure price changes associated with changes in capital-related costs (prices x ``quantities'').
The composition of capital-related costs is maintained at base-year FY 1987 proportions in the CIPI. As such, the
composition of capital reflects the underlying capital acquisition process. We selected FY 1987 as the base year
for this preliminary CIPI for consistency with the operating input price index. We would periodically update both
the operating and the capital input price indexes to reflect the changing composition of inputs for capital and operating
costs. We expect to have rebased the capital and operating input price indexes by the time we implement the final capital
update framework for FY 1996. The CIPI below illustrates the methodology we propose to employ. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The model capital update framework, like the revised operating update framework, incorporates several policy adjustments
in addition to the CIPI. We would adjust for case-mix index-related changes, for intensity, and for the efficient
and cost-effective use of capital (such as movable equipment, buildings and fixed equipment) in the hospital industry,
as well as for error in the capital input price index forecast. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In developing the model framework, we are attempting as much as possible to maintain consistency with the current
operating framework, in order to facilitate the eventual development of a single prospective payment system update
framework. We are also concerned with promoting the goals that motivated the adoption of the capital prospective
payment system, especially the goals of promoting more effective and efficient utilization of capital resources
in the hospital industry and establishing incentives for hospitals to make cost-effective decisions regarding
acquisition of new capital resources. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
It is important to emphasize that this presentation represents our current thinking, and that we continue to encourage
submission of comments and recommendations for further improvements. We are interested in suggestions regarding
the CIPI, the proposed policy adjustment factor for intensity, and alternative methodologies for deriving the factors.
We also welcome information concerning empirical studies and sources of data that could be useful in developing the
framework. We will consider comments and recommendations on any aspect of the model framework in making further developments
for presentation in the next version of the model. Our plan is to consider comments received on this model for inclusion
in one further preliminary model to be included in this year's final rule for the prospective payment system. We will
then formally propose an analytical update framework for implementation in FY 1996 during the rulemaking process
for that year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=4 -->
II. ProPAC Recommendation for Updating the Capital Prospective Payment System Federal Rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
ProPAC recommends the use of an update framework that includes a capital market basket component that measures 1-year
changes in the purchase prices of a fixed basket of capital goods purchased by hospitals (Recommendation 10). The
ProPAC framework also includes several policy adjustment factors. A forecast error correction factor adjusts payment
rates so that the effects of past errors are not perpetuated. A financing policy adjustment accounts for the effects
of substantial deviations from long-term trends in interest rates on hospital capital costs. The capital update
framework also includes adjustments for scientific and technological advances, productivity, and case-mix change
similar to those employed in the ProPAC operating update framework. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In Recommendation 11, ProPAC also recommends the development and use of a combined update framework for the prospective
payment systems for operating and capital-related costs for FY 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Response:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Our long-term goal is to develop a single prospective payment system update framework. Currently, the regulations
provide for the determination of the capital prospective payment update by means of a lagged 2-year average of actual
Medicare capital cost increases. Once we have completed work on an analytical framework for the capital prospective
payment update, we will begin to study development of a unified framework. In the meantime, we will continue to maintain
consistency as much as possible with the current operating framework in order to facilitate the eventual development
of a unified framework. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The basic objectives of the ProPAC and HCFA update frameworks are compatible. The goal of each framework is to provide
a rate of increase in capital prospective payments that, along with the rate of increase in operating prospective
payments, will ensure a flow of capital and operating resources that will allow for efficient and effective care for
Medicare patients. Both frameworks are designed to provide increases for the purchase of quality-enhancing new
technologies. Both frameworks provide for case-mix adjustments to remove the effects of upcoding and to adjust for
changes in within-DRG severity. Both frameworks also seek to encourage efficient capital spending behavior and
to adjust the overall size of capital stock to appropriate levels. Although the frameworks adopt different methodologies
for promoting some of these goals, they are philosophically compatible to the degree that they share these goals.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00214 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
However, there is one significant philosophical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difference between the ProPAC and HCFA frameworks. ProPAC's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
framework is based on the premise that capital prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments are for future capital purchases. Thus, ProPAC's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed capital market basket reflects the projected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase in the purchase price of capital goods from one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year to the next. To put it another way, the market basket 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the ProPAC framework reflects the price of replacing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current capital stock at future prices. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
HCFA's framework is based on the premise that capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payments are for hospitals' future capital-related expenses rather than for future capital-related

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchases. That is, HCFA's framework addresses the expenses 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated with hospitals' given stock of capital in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particular fiscal year; ProPAC's framework ignores 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals' present stock of capital and focuses on costs of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital purchases that hospitals will make in a particular 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fiscal year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
There is a sound economic reason why the current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchase price of capital assets alone is an inadequate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measure of the price of capital. The crucial distinguishing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
feature of capital assets is that they are consumed over 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time. A satisfactory measure of the change in capital input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prices must therefore account for this fact. Consideration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the costs for all capital currently in use for patient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
care, not just the costs for current capital purchases, is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary for this purpose. The ProPAC index cannot 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adequately capture this aspect of changes in capital input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prices. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition to our disagreement with ProPAC over the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basic purpose of the CIPI, we also disagree over the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
treatment of interest. The pure price aspects of interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs (that is, the interest rate and the purchase price 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that is represented in the amount of loan principal) are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beyond the control of the hospital industry. To be sure, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the actual decision to purchase capital assets or acquire 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
debt is a ``quantity'' decision and typically is discretionary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for a particular span of time. However, measuring the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
actual expected price per unit of real capital, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
independently of any evaluation of the propriety of any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
actual purchase decisions, is essential to recognize that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the industry has control over the amount of capital it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchases but no control over the price it pays for capital. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, the pure price aspect of interest cost changes must be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incorporated into the CIPI. Otherwise, the CIPI will not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accurately reflect the prices faced by hospitals who must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
borrow to finance necessary capital acquisitions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Limitations on the quantity of capital are appropriately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implemented through policy adjustment factors. The ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approach artificially eliminates pure price changes related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to interest costs from the CIPI and incorporates them into a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discretionary adjustment factor. The HCFA CIPI retains all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price components of increase in interest costs as one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measure of inflation in capital-related expenses. It 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thereby keeps price and quantity aspects distinct, allowing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
separate analysis of each factor of increases in capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expenses. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We provide further comments on particular ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommendations in sections III and IV of this appendix. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The ProPAC recommendations concerning the methodology for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recognizing increases due to quality-enhancing new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technology and the case-mix index adjustment are discussed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Appendix D in the context of the update recommendation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the prospective payment system for operating costs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
III. Measurement of Capital Input Price Increases 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Introduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
HCFA proposed a capital input price index as one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
component in developing future update factors for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal rate in the September 1, 1992 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 (57 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FR 40016). We have presented revised versions of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital input price index in the May 26, 1993 (58 FR 30448) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and September 1, 1993 (58 FR 46490) issues of the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Register.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The developing capital input price index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
parallels the operating input price index. Both the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
developing capital input price index and the operating input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price index are designed to measure input price changes for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals' current year expenses, that is, to separate pure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price changes from quantity and expenditure changes. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating sector input price index measures input price 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes for operating-related expenses. The capital input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price index measures input price changes for capital-related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expenses. Capital-related expenses include depreciation, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interest, and other expenses (taxes and insurance related to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital goods.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Review of the HCFA Capital Input Price Index Methodology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The current version of the CIPI is based on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following assumptions:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;The Federal rate is based on the concept of capital-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
related expenses of capital assets used for patient care in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the fiscal year and, therefore, any change in the Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate should take into account expected changes in the input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price aspects of capital-related expenses; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Capital-related expenses are defined as the sum of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation expense, capital-related interest costs, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other capital-related costs, including taxes, insurance, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
leases; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The input prices related to capital-related expenses 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are beyond the control of the hospital industry (that is, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the hospital is a price-taker, not a price-setter). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
These assumptions lead directly to a definition of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CIPI that takes into account the price aspects of changes in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation expense, interest costs, and other capital-related costs. Further, the assumptions lead directly
to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
input prices for depreciation expense and interest costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which, unlike operating costs, have a time dimension that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must be captured in the CIPI. HCFA includes three 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories of capital-related expenses in the CIPI: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation expense, interest costs, and other capital-related costs (taxes and insurance). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00215 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Current depreciation expenses represent the summed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation charges for all past purchases of fixed capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assets that are still depreciable in the current period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The input prices associated with these depreciation expenses 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are the purchase prices attached to all past and current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital purchases for capital still depreciable in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current period. A weighted average of these purchase prices 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thus represents the input price associated with depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expenses in the current period. Thus, the depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
input price for the current period measures price aspects of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current depreciation expenses for capital just as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating input price index for the current period measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price aspects of current operating expenses for labor and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
non-capital goods and services. The depreciation input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price differs from the operating input price in that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depreciation input price is a composite of all past capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchase prices while the operating index input price 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures purchase prices for current periods only. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Current interest expenses represent the total interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
charges for all still-active past debt instruments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated with the purchase of fixed capital assets. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
input prices associated with these interest expenses are the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interest rates attached to all past debt instruments that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are still active in the current period. A weighted average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of these interest rates thus represents the input price 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated with interest expenses in the current period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, the interest input price for the current period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures price aspects of current interest expenses just as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the operating input price index for the current period 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures price aspects of current operating expenses for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labor and non-capital goods and services. The interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
input price differs from the operating input price in that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the depreciation input price is a composite of all past 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interest rates for debt instruments still active while the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating index input price measures purchase prices for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current periods only. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Current year capital-related expenses for taxes and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
insurance have an annual time dimension and, therefore, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prices associated with these expenses are, like operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
input prices, current year prices only. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Revisions to the Depreciation Component of the CIPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A commenter on a previous version of the CIPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommended that constant annual weights for capital price 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proxies be replaced by variable annual weights that reflect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the vintage purchases of capital. The commenter pointed out 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that annual purchases of real capital tend to increase over 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time. As annual purchases of capital increase, the later 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years in the moving average of depreciation costs should be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
weighted more heavily than the earlier years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We agree with this comment. Accordingly, a special 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data base was prepared to provide appropriate historical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
weights for depreciation and interest input prices. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data base starts with financial variables from the American 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Hospital Association (AHA) Panel Survey. These are enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with data from Medicare cost reports and from the Department 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of Commerce Capital Expenditure Survey. The data base 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provides annual estimates of nominal purchases for building 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and fixed equipment and for movable equipment. Leasing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amounts were converted to nominal purchases using the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relationships as appear for owner-operated capital. Nominal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchases were then converted to annual real (that is, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constant dollar) purchases by dividing nominal expenditures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by an appropriate purchase price proxy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Expected life for building and fixed equipment and for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
movable equipment were derived from Medicare cost reports by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dividing book value of assets by current year depreciation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amounts. The relative distribution of real capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchases within the respective life for building and fixed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment (25 years) and for movable equipment (10 years) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were derived from the special data base. These relative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
distributions are shown in Table 1. Relative distributions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for a number of different time periods were averaged to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obtain the distributions in Table 1. These distributions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were all very similar regardless of the periods chosen and, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therefore, we selected an average of the distributions in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to simplify the calculations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
