<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
The stability of the HCFA vintage-weighted CIPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compared to annual changes in capital purchase prices is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portrayed graphically in Figures 1 and 2. As we have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed in connection with previous versions of the CIPI, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stability is an important criterion for evaluating such an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index. This is because excessive volatility in a price 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index deprives the index of predictability, and thus 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inhibits the ability of institutions to plan for changes in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital payments resulting from changes in the CIPI. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0101 clearpage -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4725 -->

<IMPORT>

<!-- PJG importformat s,d607 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=2 -->
E:GRAPHICSEP27MY94.000
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</IMPORT>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<BILLING>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=68 g=1 f=2 -->
BILLING CODE 4120&hyph;01&hyph;M
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</BILLING>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
ProPAC recommends a capital input price index based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual changes in current capital purchase prices excluding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consideration of weighted historical capital purchase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prices. We previously argued that the ProPAC index was not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
logically consistent with the operating input index that is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently used to assist updating DRG payment rates. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would add that the volatility in annual purchase prices 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shown in Figures 1 and 2 would introduce an unacceptable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
degree of volatility in prospective capital payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=2 -->
D. Revisions to the Interest Price Component of the CIPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
Another commenter on a previous version of the CIPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommended that data from Securities Data Inc. be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incorporated into the CIPI interest computations. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source provides information on hospital issuances of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
municipal and commercial bonds. From this data base, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incorporated information that shows that the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected life of hospital bond debt instruments (that is, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the time interval between the issue date and the maturation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
date) was about 13 years for municipal serial bonds and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
about 25 years for municipal term bonds. The weighted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average life for the 2 types of bonds was 22 years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The relative nominal capital purchases within various 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
22-year periods provided appropriate annual weights for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual changes in interest rates. Not all capital purchases 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are funded by debt. Medicare cost reports suggest that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
about 80 percent of new capital acquisitions are financed by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
debt and about 20 percent by equity financing. However, if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proportion of total purchases financed by debt does not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change substantially from year to year, then it is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
irrelevant whether we use the full amount or a constant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proportion of the full amount of nominal capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acquisitions as weights for relative amounts of the debt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
instruments still active in the current period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Relative interest weights derived from our procedure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are shown in Table 1. When combined with percent changes in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual interest rates from Table 2, the weights provide a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current year estimates of interest rate changes in the CIPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Table 3. Thus, for example, the interest rate component 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change of ^3.2 percent in Table 3 for FY 1995 represents the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average of the previous 22 years in the interest rate proxy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Combined Muni/AAA) in Table 2, weighted by the interest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
weights listed in Table 1. We use a percent change in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combined municipal and AAA commercial bond interest rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(shown in Table 2 as Combined Muni/AAA), giving the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
municipal rate an 85 percent weight and the AAA rate a 15 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent weight, reflecting the relative hospital debts of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the government/non-profit hospital sector and the for-profit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sector. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



