<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
The stability of HCFA's vintage-weighted interest rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compared to annual changes in interest rates is portrayed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Figure 3. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 03 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<BILLING>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=68 g=1 f=2 -->
BILLING CODE 4120&hyph;01&hyph;P
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</BILLING>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0101 clearpage -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4725 -->

<IMPORT>

<!-- PJG importformat s,d624 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=2 -->
E:GRAPHICSEP27MY94.001
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 03 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

</IMPORT>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<BILLING>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=68 g=1 f=2 -->
BILLING CODE 4120&hyph;01&hyph;C
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</BILLING>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0101 clearpage -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
As we have discussed in connection with previous versions of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the model, stability is an important criterion for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluating the CIPI because it provides an important degree 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of predictability to the index. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although Medicare cost reports show that only 60 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent of current hospital debt is in the form of notes or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
bonds (about 40 percent is in the form of mortgages), we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assumed that the relative annual weights for all debt and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the relative annual changes in interest rates for all debt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were the same as bond-related weights and price changes. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are still searching for an appropriate source of information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on hospital commercial mortgage data. We do not expect that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the discovery of such data will materially alter our current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conclusions about trends in effective interest rates over 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00222 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
A third commenter on a previous version of the CIPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommended that we investigate the effects on interest rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes of changing structures of hospital bond ratings. If 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
bond ratings are deteriorating, hospitals incur higher 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interest rates; if bond ratings improve, hospitals incur 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lower interest rates. Our CIPI currently recognizes only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes in pure interest rates and does not recognize 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes in effective interest rates due to changes in bond 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ratings. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We examined a hospital municipal bond data base from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Securities Data Inc. to examine this issue. This data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
showed that serial bonds dominated short-term bonds and term 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
bonds dominated long-term bonds (Figure 4). We classified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all bond amounts by ratings found in the data base for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years 1980 to 1992. We defined bonds rated as Baa1 or above 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as ``Investment Grade'' bonds; all others were ``Non-Investment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Grade''. The percentage distribution of the issuance-amount-rated bonds in Figure 5 (percentage amounts applied
to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dollar amount of debt issuance) indicates no discernible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
trend in upward or downward ratings over the period. We 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conclude that there is no evidence to justify a component 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for deteriorating bond ratings in the CIPI. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 03 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<BILLING>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=68 g=1 f=2 -->
BILLING CODE 4120&hyph;01&hyph;P
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</BILLING>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

