<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
Further, the ProPAC CIPI omits interest rates. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cumulative effect of declining interest rates for all debt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
instruments in recent years has driven the rate of change in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the HCFA interest rate component downward, a trend projected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into future rate years. The declining interest rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
component drags the HCFA CIPI below the ProPAC CIPI in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projection period. Other things being equal, the ProPAC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
index would result in overpayment through the Federal rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because anticipated actual capital-related expenses will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
less than ProPAC projects due to the effects of lower 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interest rates on capital-related expenses. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=2 -->
IV. Case-Mix Adjustment and Adjustment for Forecast Error 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
The case-mix index (CMI) is the measure of the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DRG weight for cases paid under the prospective payment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system. Because the DRG weight determines the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment for each case, any percentage increase in the CMI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
corresponds to an equal percentage increase in hospital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The CMI can change for any of several reasons: because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the average resource use of Medicare patients changes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(``real'' case-mix change), because changes in hospital coding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of patient records result in higher weight DRG assignments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(``coding effects''), and because the annual DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification and recalibration changes may not be budget 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
neutral (``reclassification effect''). In the update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
framework for the prospective payment system for operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs, we adjust the update upwards to allow for real case- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mix change, but remove the effects of coding changes on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CMI. We also remove the effect on total payments of prior 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes to the DRG classifications and relative weights, in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to retain budget neutrality for all CMI-related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes other than patient severity. (For example, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjusted for the effects of the FY 1992 DRG reclassification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and recalibration as part of our FY 1994 update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recommendation.) The operating adjustment consists of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction for total observed case-mix change, an increase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the portion of case-mix change that we determine is due 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to real case-mix change rather than coding modifications, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and an adjustment for the effect of prior DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification and recalibration changes. We proposed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopt this CMI adjustment as well in the capital update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
framework. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We received no comments on the proposed CMI adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The current operating update framework contains an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment for forecast error. The input price index 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forecast is based on historical trends and relationships 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ascertainable at the time the update factor is established 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the following year. In any given year there can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unanticipated price fluctuations that can result in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
differences between the actual increase in prices faced by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals and the forecast used in calculating the update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factors. We continue to believe that the capital update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
framework should include a forecast error adjustment factor. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In setting a prospective payment rate under the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
framework, we would make an adjustment for forecast error 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only if our estimate of the capital input price index rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of increase for any year is off by 0.25 percentage points or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more. There is a 2-year lag between the forecast and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measurement of the forecast error. Thus, for example, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would adjust for a forecast error made in FY 1996 through an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment to the FY 1998 update. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We received no comments on the proposed forecasting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
error adjustment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=2 -->
V. Policy Adjustment Factors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
The capital input price index measures the pure price 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes associated with changes in capital-related costs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(prices x ``quantities''). The composition of capital-related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs is maintained at base-year 1987 proportions in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital input price index. We would address appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes in the amount and composition of capital stock 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through the policy adjustment factors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The current update framework for the prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment system for operating costs includes factors designed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to adjust the input price index rate of increase for policy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considerations. Under the revised operating framework, we 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjust for service productivity (the efficiency with which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
providers produce individual services such as laboratory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tests and diagnostic procedures) and intensity (the amount 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of services used to produce a discharge). The service 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
productivity factor for the operating update framework 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reflects a forward-looking adjustment for the changes that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospitals can be expected to make in service-level 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
productivity during the year. A hospital retains any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
productivity increases above the average. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The intensity factor for the operating update framework 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reflects how hospital services are utilized to produce the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final product, that is, the discharge. This component 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accounts for changes in the use of quality-enhancing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services, changes in within-DRG severity, and expected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modification of practice patterns to remove cost-ineffective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services. We originally proposed that the intensity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment factor in the revised operating framework be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopted in the capital update framework. That factor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
remains a part of our developing framework. Under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revised operating update framework, we calculate case-mix 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constant intensity as the change in total charges per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
admission, adjusted for price level changes (the CPI 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hospital component) and changes in real case mix. The use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of total charges in the calculation of the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensity factor makes it a total intensity factor, that is, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
charges for capital services are already built into the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculation of the factor. We can therefore incorporate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed intensity adjustment from the operating update 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
framework into the capital update framework. In the absence 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of reliable estimates of the proportions of the overall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual intensity increases that are due, respectively, to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ineffective practice patterns and to the combination of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality-enhancing new technologies and within-DRG 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
complexity, we would assume, as in the revised operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
update framework, that one-half of the annual increase is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
due to each of these factors. The capital update framework 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would thus provide an add-on to the input price index rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of increase of one-half of the estimated annual increase in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensity to allow for within-DRG severity increases and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adoption of quality-enhancing technology. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940527-1-00227 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940527-1-00014 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=2 -->
In our previous discussions of an efficiency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment, we suggested that the adjustment should take 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into account two considerations. One is that capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inputs, unlike operating inputs, are generally fixed in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
short run. The productivity target in the revised operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
framework operates on a short-term, year-to-year basis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Targets for capital efficiency and cost-effectiveness, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
however, must operate on a longer term basis. The other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consideration is that, prior to the adoption of the capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prospective payment system, Medicare payment policy for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital-related costs, as well as the policies of other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payers, did not provide sufficient incentives for efficient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and cost-effective capital spending. As a result, capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
costs per case, and therefore base year prospective capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rates, may be higher than would have been consistent with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital acquisition policy in more efficiency-oriented 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
markets. A guiding principle in devising an efficiency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment is therefore that Medicare capital prospective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
payment rates should not provide for maintenance of capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in excess of the level that would be produced in an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efficiency-oriented competitive market. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As a preliminary examination of this issue, we analyzed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the change in actual Medicare capital cost per case for FYs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1986&hyph;1991 in relation to the change in the capital input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
price index (which accounts for change in the input prices 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for capital-related costs), and the other adjustment factors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that we were then proposing to include in the framework. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(The other adjustment factors are the increase in real case 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mix and the increase in intensity due to quality-enhancing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technological change and within-DRG complexity.) We found 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rates of increase in spending per case that exceeded the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate of increase attributable to inflation in capital input 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prices, quality-enhancing intensity increases, and real 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
case-mix growth. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Economic theory suggests that an industry with a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
guaranteed return on capital (such as the hospital industry 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prior to prospective payment for capital-related costs) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would have a tendency to be overly capitalized relative to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more competitive industries. This is because the incentive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for firms in such an industry is to compete on the basis of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more capital-intensive production processes than firms in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other industries. As a result, capital costs per case, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therefore base year prospective capital rates, may be higher 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than would have been consistent with capital acquisition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
policy in more efficiency-oriented competitive markets. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Our analysis was designed to examine whether hospitals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
had in fact responded to the incentives of the cost-based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reimbursement system for capital by expanding beyond what 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
was necessary for efficient and cost-effective delivery of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
services. The analysis confirmed that volume and intensity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of capital acquisition far outpaced the increase in capital 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
input prices during the years between the implementation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the prospective payment system for operating costs and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
introduction of the capital prospective payment system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Even accounting for real CMI increases and increases in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensity attributable to cost-increasing but quality-enhancing new technologies, there remains a large excess
of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capital-related spending. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The following table shows the results of our most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recent analysis, based on the most current data available 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the most recent projections. Differences between this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
table and the tables in previous discussions in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

